var tafsirData_11 = {
  "1": "\"Alif-Lãm-Ra\" opens this chapter with three separate letters, recited one by one rather than read as a word. Several chapters of the Quran begin this way, and the honest position, held by many early scholars, is that their full meaning is known to Allah. Some Companions and later commentators offered suggestions, and one widely discussed observation is that these are letters of the same Arabic alphabet the Arabs used in their poetry and speech, placed at the head of a Book they were challenged to imitate and could not. The material was familiar; the result was beyond them.\n\n\"˹This is˺ a Book whose verses are well perfected and then fully explained\" describes the Quran with two qualities that work together. The first, in Arabic \"uhkimat,\" carries the sense of being made firm, sound, and free of flaw, like a well-built structure that nothing can shake. The verses are not loose sayings; they are precise, consistent, and secure from contradiction, which the Quran itself points to when it says that had it been from other than Allah they would have found much inconsistency in it.\n\nThe second quality, \"fussilat,\" means laid out in detail, separated part by part. A perfect message that stayed compressed would be of little use, so the Book explains belief, worship, law, character, history, and the hereafter clearly and in order.\n\nHolding both together answers two common complaints at once. One person says religion is vague; the response is that these verses are firmly built. Another says religion is impossibly complicated; the response is that they are also explained in detail.\n\n\"˹It is˺ from the One ˹Who is˺ All-Wise, All-Aware\" names the source and explains why the Book has these two qualities. The name \"al-Hakim\" indicates perfect wisdom, placing everything exactly where it belongs, which is why the verses are sound. The name \"al-Khabir\" indicates knowledge of the innermost reality of things, including what people hide, which is why the explanation reaches so precisely into human need. A message with such features could only come from a Sender described in such terms.",
  "2": "\"˹Tell them, O Prophet,˺ \"Worship none but Allah\"\" states the purpose of the Book just described. Verses that are perfectly made and fully explained were not sent to satisfy curiosity. They were sent to bring people to this one point. Every prophet mentioned later in this chapter, Noah, Hud, Salih, Shu'ayb, and the others, begins with almost these exact words to his people, which shows that the message has never changed.\n\nThe Arabic wording is a negation followed by an affirmation: none is to be worshipped except Him. Denial comes before affirmation, as it does in the declaration of faith itself. A heart cluttered with other masters cannot simply add God to the collection; the space has to be cleared.\n\nIt is worth being clear about what worship means here, since the word is often narrowed to rituals. The Arabic \"ibadah\" covers every act of devotion, submission, hope, fear, and reliance directed toward God. Prayer and fasting belong to it, but so does the direction of a person's ultimate trust. Someone who performs the rituals while placing his real hope and fear in wealth, power, or public opinion has divided what this command wants undivided.\n\n\"Surely I am a warner and deliverer of good news to you from Him\" then defines the Prophet's role in two words, and the order is meaningful. Warning comes first because danger must be recognised before rescue is valued, and good news follows because fear alone hardens people rather than reforming them. The Quran keeps this balance carefully, pairing mercy with punishment throughout.\n\nThe Prophet described himself with a similar image in a hadith recorded by al-Bukhari and Muslim, comparing himself to a man who has seen an approaching army and runs to warn his people, calling out to them for their own safety. That is exactly the role this verse names.\n\nThe phrase \"from Him\" removes any suggestion of personal ambition. He is a messenger carrying another's words, which is why the chapter goes on to insist that he asks no reward for delivering them.",
  "3": "\"And seek your Lord's forgiveness and turn to Him in repentance\" adds the second half of what the message asks. Worship alone is not enough for creatures who fail, so the call to worship none but Allah is immediately joined to a call to return after falling short.\n\nTwo distinct actions are named. The first, \"istighfar,\" means asking Allah to cover and pardon what has already been done. The second, \"tawbah,\" means turning back, changing direction, leaving the sin behind. Asking forgiveness without turning is words without movement; turning without asking forgiveness leaves the past unaddressed. Together they form a complete return, which is why the Quran so often mentions them side by side.\n\n\"He will grant you a good provision for an appointed term\" gives a promise that surprises many readers, because it is worldly rather than only spiritual. Turning to Allah is linked here to a good life in this world for the span He has appointed. Surah Nuh contains a strikingly similar passage, where Noah tells his people to seek forgiveness from their Lord, promising that He will send rain in abundance and increase them in wealth and children. Umar ibn al-Khattab is reported to have prayed for rain by seeking forgiveness for this reason.\n\nThe promise should be read with care. It does not mean that every person who repents becomes wealthy, since the Quran also shows prophets living in hardship. A \"good provision\" is enjoyment of what one has with contentment and blessing, which is a different thing from a large income.\n\n\"And graciously reward the doers of good\" moves beyond the removal of sin to positive reward. The Arabic \"fadl\" means favour given beyond what is earned, so those who excel receive more than their deeds strictly deserve.\n\n\"But if you turn away, then I truly fear for you the torment of a formidable Day\" closes with warning, and the wording is personal. He says he fears for them. This is a warner speaking out of concern for people who insulted him, not a judge announcing a sentence, and that tone is characteristic of how prophets in this chapter address their communities.",
  "4": "\"To Allah is your return\" is short, and its shortness gives it force. After the invitation to worship Him alone, to seek forgiveness, and the warning about a formidable Day, this sentence removes the option of indifference. Whatever a person decides about the message, the destination is already fixed.\n\nThe wording places the return to Allah before mentioning anything else, which in Arabic gives it the sense of exclusivity: to Him, and to no one else. People imagine many endings for themselves. Some think death is simply nothing. Others expect to be absorbed into the universe or reborn. The verse cuts through all of it with one direction of travel.\n\nThis also explains why the earlier commands matter so much. If life ended at the grave, worship, repentance, and warnings would be optional matters of taste. Because there is a return, they become the most practical concerns a person has.\n\n\"And He is Most Capable of everything\" answers the objection that always follows. The Meccans did not usually deny that God existed; they denied that bodies reduced to dust could be brought back. Surah Yasin records exactly this argument, where a man holds up a decayed bone and asks who will give life to bones that have crumbled, and the answer given is that the One who produced them the first time will revive them, since He has full knowledge of every creation.\n\nThe reasoning is straightforward. Someone who has already built something from nothing is obviously able to rebuild it from remains. Restoration is easier than origination by any human measure, though nothing is difficult for Allah at all.\n\nAttaching this description of power to the statement about returning also serves as a warning to those who feel safe because they are wealthy or protected. Power over all things includes power over the strongest people in any society, which the chapter will illustrate at length through the stories of nations that considered themselves untouchable and were removed in a morning.",
  "5": "\"Indeed, they enfold ˹what is in˺ their hearts, ˹trying˺ to hide it from Him!\" exposes a strange behaviour among the opponents of the message. They were folding their chests inward, hiding their hostility, their doubts, and their private opinions about the Prophet, as though concealment from people were concealment from God. Commentators report that some of them would turn away, bend over, or cover themselves when passing him so that he would not see them or so they would not have to hear the Quran.\n\nThe verse treats this as almost unbelievable, and the tone carries a note of astonishment. The One being hidden from is the One who created the heart doing the hiding.\n\n\"But even when they cover themselves with their clothes, He knows what they conceal and what they reveal\" makes the point physically. A person can wrap a garment around himself and disappear from human view, and this changes nothing at all. Both what is hidden and what is shown are equally visible to Allah. The chapter opened by naming Him \"All-Aware,\" and this verse shows what that name means in practice.\n\nThere is a psychological insight here that reaches far beyond those particular men. Human beings instinctively feel that not being seen means not being observed. Children cover their eyes and feel invisible, and adults behave more carefully when someone is watching. The verse identifies this instinct and corrects it, which is precisely why the Prophet defined excellence in faith, recorded in Sahih Muslim, as worshipping Allah as though you see Him, knowing that even if you do not see Him, He sees you.\n\n\"Surely He knows best what is ˹hidden˺ in the heart\" ends by moving from covered bodies to covered intentions. The Arabic phrase refers to what lies within the chests, the thoughts a person never speaks and sometimes barely admits to himself. Allah knows these better than the person does.\n\nThis is a warning against hypocrisy and, at the same time, a comfort. The sincerity that nobody notices, the intention behind a small act, the struggle no one else could possibly understand, all of it is fully known.",
  "6": "\"There is no moving creature on earth whose provision is not guaranteed by Allah\" widens the discussion from human hearts to the whole of living creation. The Arabic word \"dabbah\" refers to any creature that moves on the earth, which takes in animals, birds, insects, and the countless organisms too small to see. Not one of them is left out of the arrangement.\n\nThe word behind \"guaranteed\" indicates a commitment Allah has taken upon Himself, similar to what the previous chapter said about it being His duty to save the believers. Nobody imposes this on Him; He has bound Himself by His own promise, and that is what makes it certain.\n\nIt is worth being careful about what the guarantee means. It does not mean that food arrives without effort. Birds leave their nests each morning and search, as the Prophet described in a hadith recorded by at-Tirmidhi, saying that if people relied on Allah as they truly should, He would provide for them as He provides for the birds, who go out hungry in the morning and return full in the evening. They are provided for, and they still fly. Provision comes with means, and seeking those means is part of trusting rather than a contradiction of it.\n\n\"And He knows where it lives and where it is laid to rest\" adds knowledge to provision. Commentators explained these two words in more than one way: the place where a creature settles during its life and the place where it will finally be buried, or the place it stays and the place it is stored. Either way the point is complete awareness of every creature's whereabouts across its entire existence.\n\n\"All is ˹written˺ in a perfect Record\" closes the verse with the same expression used in the previous chapter about not an atom's weight being hidden. Nothing is left to chance or memory.\n\nFor a person worried about tomorrow, this verse quietly rearranges the question. If the smallest insect in a desert is accounted for, anxiety about being forgotten is misplaced. What remains is to work honestly and lawfully, and to leave the outcome where it already rests.",
  "7": "\"He is the One Who created the heavens and the earth in six Days\" begins a verse that moves from the care of individual creatures to the making of the whole universe. The word translated \"Days\" is \"ayyam,\" and its meaning here is not the twenty-four hour day measured by an earth that did not yet exist. In Arabic the word can indicate a period or stage, and the Quran itself speaks elsewhere of a day with your Lord being like a thousand years of what people count. Muslim scholars have long noted that these are six stages known to Allah. What matters for the argument is that creation was ordered and deliberate, done in stages by One who did not need any time at all.\n\n\"And His Throne was upon the waters\" describes a state before the heavens and earth as we know them. The Prophet described a similar scene in a hadith recorded by al-Bukhari, saying that Allah existed when there was nothing else, and His Throne was over the water. Believers accept such statements as told, without imagining a form or asking how, since these matters lie beyond human experience.\n\n\"In order to test which of you is best in deeds\" gives the purpose, and it is the heart of the verse. This vast creation was not made as decoration. It is the setting for a test. Notice the wording carefully: best in deeds, not most in deeds. The measure is quality, not quantity, which early scholars explained as the most sincere and the most correct, meaning done for Allah and done according to His guidance.\n\n\"And if you ˹O Prophet˺ say, \"Surely you will ˹all˺ be raised up after death,\" the disbelievers will certainly say, \"That is nothing but pure magic!\"\" records the predictable reaction. A Creator who built the universe in stages can obviously raise the dead, yet the response is the same label Pharaoh's court used against Moses. When a truth is unwelcome, giving it a dismissive name is easier than answering it.",
  "8": "\"And if We delay their punishment until an appointed time, they will definitely say, \"What is holding it back?\"\" describes how the deniers responded to divine patience. Instead of using the extra time, they treated it as evidence that nothing was coming. Their question was not a request for information; it was mockery dressed as curiosity, the same attitude the previous chapter recorded when they demanded to know when the threat would arrive.\n\nThe word for the delay indicates a fixed term, which the Quran has already explained as an appointed time known to Allah and unchangeable. Postponement is deliberate, and other verses explain the reason: if Allah punished people immediately for their wrongdoing, He would not leave a single living creature on earth. Mercy is what creates the gap.\n\nTheir reasoning contains a basic mistake, and it is worth naming. They took the absence of immediate consequence as proof of no consequence. Life is full of examples that refute this. A man who neglects his health for twenty years feels fine for most of them. Debts accumulate quietly before they are called in. Nothing about a delay tells you that the account has been closed.\n\n\"Indeed, on the Day it overtakes them, it will not be averted from them\" answers the question in the only way that matters. What is being held back will not always be held back, and when it arrives nothing will turn it aside. The wording suggests suddenness, arriving upon them rather than approaching slowly enough to prepare for, which matches the previous chapter's picture of punishment coming by night or by day.\n\n\"And they will be overwhelmed by what they used to ridicule\" completes the reversal. The Arabic verb indicates something surrounding and enclosing a person from every side. What was a joke becomes the environment they cannot escape. Ridicule is a way of keeping something at a distance, of refusing to take it seriously without having to argue against it, and the verse shows how completely that strategy fails.\n\nThis is one of the Quran's recurring patterns: the thing mocked becomes the thing that closes in, and it does so at a moment nobody chose.",
  "9": "\"If We give people a taste of Our mercy then take it away from them, they become utterly desperate, ungrateful\" turns from the deniers' arguments to something closer to home, describing how human beings typically behave when their circumstances change. This is a portrait of the untrained self, and most readers will recognise something of themselves in it.\n\nThe word \"taste\" is precise. A taste is a small sample, not the whole of the thing. Whatever blessing a person enjoys, health, income, security, family, is a fraction of Allah's mercy, given for a period. Describing it as a taste already implies that it was never a permanent possession.\n\nTwo reactions follow its removal. The first is despair. The Arabic word indicates cutting off all hope, the state of a person convinced that nothing good will ever come again. Losing one blessing becomes, in his mind, losing every future blessing. The Quran treats this as a serious spiritual failure rather than a mere mood, saying elsewhere that none despair of Allah's mercy except the disbelieving people, because despair contains a hidden claim that God's generosity has run out.\n\nThe second reaction is ingratitude. The word used here, \"kafur,\" is an intense form built on the root that means to cover something over. Such a person covers up everything he was given before. Years of ease are forgotten because of one hard season, and he speaks as though he has never received anything at all.\n\nBoth reactions come from the same mistake: treating blessings as entitlements rather than as gifts on loan. A guest who is served a meal and then sees the dishes cleared does not accuse the host of cruelty.\n\nThe Prophet described the believer's alternative in a hadith recorded in Sahih Muslim, marvelling at the affair of the believer, since all of it is good for him: if ease comes he is grateful and that is good for him, and if hardship strikes he is patient and that is good for him. The verse shows what happens when neither gratitude nor patience is present.",
  "10": "\"But if We give them a taste of prosperity after being touched with adversity, they say, \"My ills are gone,\"\" completes the picture begun in the previous verse by showing the opposite situation. Hardship produced despair; now relief produces something equally unhealthy.\n\nNotice the exact words they speak. They announce that the hardship has departed, and they say nothing about who removed it. The One who lifted the difficulty is missing from the sentence entirely. That silence is the whole problem. During the crisis, many such people had been praying; the moment it passes, the prayers stop and the memory fades. The Quran describes this pattern elsewhere, mentioning people who call upon Allah sincerely while a wave engulfs them at sea, and then turn away once they are brought safely to land.\n\nThe verse also captures an assumption hidden in the phrase, a sense that troubles are now over for good. A person leaving one difficulty often behaves as though he has passed through the only test he will face, though experience never supports that belief.\n\n\"And become totally prideful and boastful\" names the two results. The first word describes an inner state, a swelling of self-satisfaction, a feeling of being above others. The second describes what comes out of the mouth, the retelling of one's success to anyone who will listen. Together they trace the movement from an attitude in the heart to speech that puts it on display.\n\nThere is something instructive in the fact that both this verse and the previous one describe reactions to circumstances that Allah arranges. Hardship and relief are both tests, and most people fail one of them. Hardship exposes despair, and comfort exposes arrogance. Wealth is often the harder test precisely because it does not feel like one.\n\nThe remedy is not to reject relief when it comes. Islam does not ask people to be miserable. The remedy is contained in the missing word: recognising the Giver. Someone who says that Allah removed his hardship keeps the memory of his own helplessness alive even while enjoying the gift.",
  "11": "\"Except those who patiently endure and do good\" carves out an exception to the bleak description of human behaviour in the two verses before it. Not everyone despairs when blessings are withdrawn, and not everyone boasts when they return. A group escapes both failures, and the verse identifies exactly what makes them different.\n\nThe first quality is \"sabr,\" patience, and it is worth stating what this word actually covers. Its root sense is to restrain or hold firm. Patience in the Quran is not passive endurance or the absence of feeling. It includes holding steady during hardship, holding oneself back from sin when temptation is easy, and holding to obedience when it becomes tiring. The wording here indicates people who have made it a settled habit rather than an occasional effort.\n\nThe second quality is doing good deeds, and pairing the two is significant. Patience alone can become a kind of frozen waiting, while activity without patience burns out. A believer who has both keeps working during the difficulty rather than merely surviving it.\n\nNotice what this exception fixes. The person described earlier despaired when mercy was taken and boasted when it returned. Patience answers the first failure, since a patient person knows the loss is temporary and measured. Doing good answers the second, since someone occupied with serving others has less room for self-congratulation, and someone who keeps working in ease shows that his gratitude is real.\n\n\"It is they who will have forgiveness and a mighty reward\" gives the outcome, and the order deserves attention. Forgiveness comes first. Even people who endure well and act rightly have failures that need covering, and no one enters Paradise on the strength of a spotless record. Only after that is the reward mentioned, described with a word indicating greatness and abundance rather than a specified amount.\n\nThe Prophet spoke often of patience in these terms. A hadith recorded by Muslim mentions that no fatigue, illness, worry, grief, or harm reaches a Muslim, even the prick of a thorn, without Allah expiating some of his sins by it.",
  "12": "\"Perhaps you ˹O Prophet˺ may wish to give up some of what is revealed to you and may be distressed by it\" speaks with unusual tenderness to a man under enormous pressure. The wording does not accuse him of having done this. It touches the thought that would naturally cross the mind of anyone in his position: that certain verses were so difficult to deliver, and provoked such hostility, that leaving them unsaid would make life easier.\n\nThe verses hardest to convey were those attacking the idols directly and those condemning the practices of respected men. Delivering them meant losing relatives, allies, and any chance of peace. Yet the Quran records that he delivered them all, including passages that corrected him personally. If he had been shaping the message himself, those are exactly the parts a person would quietly drop.\n\n\"Because they say, \"If only a treasure had been sent down to him, or an angel had come with him!\"\" identifies the source of the pressure. The Meccans had a fixed idea of what a messenger from God should look like: rich, or accompanied by a visible angel. A man who walked in the market, ate food, and had no wealth did not match their expectation. The Quran answers this elsewhere by pointing out that even if an angel had been sent, it would have been sent in the form of a man, leaving them just as confused.\n\nThere is a mistake behind their demand that still appears today, the assumption that God's favour shows itself in money and spectacle. This chapter later shows the opposite, describing prophets who were poor and followed by people the elite despised.\n\n\"You are only a warner\" returns the Prophet to his actual job, which does not include satisfying demands or producing miracles on request.\n\n\"And Allah is the Trustee of All Affairs\" places everything else in the right hands. The Arabic \"wakil\" indicates the one who manages and takes charge of a matter. Results, consequences, and the fate of the deniers are His responsibility, not the messenger's, which is precisely what removes the distress described at the start of the verse.",
  "13": "\"Or do they say, \"He has fabricated this ˹Quran˺!\"?\" repeats an accusation that the previous chapter also recorded, and the response here is even more generous than the one given there. In Surah Yunus the challenge was to produce a single chapter. Here the demand is different: \"Produce ten fabricated sûrahs like it and seek help from whoever you can —other than Allah—if what you say is true!\"\n\nAsking for ten rather than one may look like a harder test, but the extra word changes everything. These ten may be \"fabricated,\" meaning invented, with no requirement that their content be true. If the Quran is simply a human invention, then producing invented material of the same quality should be no obstacle at all. They are free to make up stories, laws, and histories as they please. Only the standard of the composition has to match.\n\nThe permission to gather helpers is repeated, and it is remarkably open. Poets, orators, priests, tribal leaders, and the gods they worshipped could all be recruited. The single exclusion is Allah, since His involvement is the exact point under dispute.\n\nScholars have discussed the relationship between this challenge and the one-chapter challenge, and the common explanation is that the demands were made at different points, moving from the greater to the smaller as the failure became clearer. Whatever the sequence, the outcome was the same. Surah al-Isra widens it to its final form, stating that if all humans and jinn came together to produce the equivalent of this Quran, they could not produce its equal even if they supported one another.\n\nThe historical setting gives this force. The Quraysh were not short of literary talent, motive, or time. Producing ten invented chapters would have destroyed the movement more effectively than any boycott or campaign of violence, and both of those they were willing to attempt. They chose the harder options, which tells us plainly which one they judged possible.",
  "14": "\"But if your helpers fail you\" follows the challenge with its expected result. The Arabic wording indicates that they do not respond to you, meaning that when the poets and allies are called upon, nothing comes back. The verse does not gloat over this. It moves straight from the failure to the conclusion that the failure demands.\n\n\"Then know that it has been revealed with the knowledge of Allah\" states the first conclusion. If human effort cannot produce anything comparable, then the source is not human. The expression \"with the knowledge of Allah\" indicates both that it came down containing His knowledge and that it descended with His full awareness and permission, which rules out any suggestion that it was produced quietly by someone else.\n\n\"And that there is no god ˹worthy of worship˺ except Him!\" gives the second conclusion, and it is the point the whole argument was aiming at. The purpose of establishing where the Quran came from was never simply to win a literary dispute. Once the Book is known to be from Allah, everything in it stands, beginning with its central message that worship belongs to Him alone.\n\n\"Will you ˹not˺ then submit ˹to Allah˺?\" ends with a question rather than a command, and the choice of form matters. Argument has done its work; what remains is a decision, and a question invites a person to make it himself rather than being pushed. The Arabic word used is \"muslimun,\" those who surrender to God, which is the same word appearing throughout the Quran for the followers of every prophet.\n\nThe structure of this whole passage is worth noticing, because it models how the Quran reasons. A claim is made, a test is offered, the test is left open to everyone, the result is stated honestly, and then the listener is asked to draw the obvious conclusion. Nobody is told to believe without grounds, and nobody is dragged to the conclusion. The evidence is laid out, and the person is left holding it.",
  "15": "\"Whoever desires ˹only˺ this worldly life and its luxuries, We will pay them in full for their deeds in this life\" describes a transaction, and it is one that many people are unknowingly conducting. The subject is not people who enjoy the world, since Islam does not condemn enjoyment. It is people whose desire stops at the world, who want nothing beyond it and aim at nothing beyond it.\n\nThe answer given is startling in its fairness. Such people are paid. The wording indicates full payment, with the following words \"nothing will be left out\" removing any suggestion of being short-changed. If a person works hard for wealth, reputation, comfort, or influence, and that is genuinely what he wants, he may well receive exactly that. Allah does not withhold worldly results from those who pursue them, and this is why hard-working deniers often prosper.\n\nThe Arabic word \"zinah,\" rendered here as luxuries, means adornment, the decorative surface of things. Elsewhere the Quran calls wealth and children the adornment of worldly life while describing lasting good deeds as better with your Lord in reward and in hope. The word suggests something attractive on the outside and not deep, like a beautifully wrapped box whose contents are the actual question.\n\nUnderstanding this verse solves a problem that troubles many believers. They see people with no interest in God living comfortable, successful lives, and they wonder how that fits with divine justice. This verse answers directly: those people asked for the world and received it. There is nothing unjust in a person getting precisely what he wanted.\n\nThe trouble is what the arrangement leaves out, which the next verse states. A payment received in full here means the account has been settled here.\n\nThere is also a warning aimed at believers hidden in this. Even good deeds can be aimed at worldly returns, done for praise, position, or reputation. A well-known hadith recorded by al-Bukhari and Muslim states that actions are judged by intentions, and that whoever migrated for some worldly gain or a woman to marry, his migration was for what he migrated to.",
  "16": "\"It is they who will have nothing in the Hereafter except the Fire\" completes the transaction described in the previous verse. The account was paid in full during this life, so nothing remains to be collected afterwards. This is not extra punishment added out of anger; it is the natural end of a deal a person made himself. Someone who asks for his entire salary in advance cannot complain when payday arrives and nothing is left.\n\nThe wording is stark. Not that they will have little in the Hereafter, but that they will have nothing except the Fire. Everything they built, achieved, and were admired for stayed behind in the world where they wanted it.\n\n\"Their efforts in this life will be fruitless\" then describes their work in a way that deserves careful reading. It does not say their efforts failed to produce results. Many such people succeed enormously by ordinary standards, building companies, monuments, and reputations. The Arabic indicates that what they made in this world has come to nothing in the reckoning that follows, like an elaborate construction discovered to be standing on sand.\n\n\"And their deeds will be useless\" repeats the point with a word that means void, cancelled, without effect. Surah al-Furqan gives the most vivid image of this, describing Allah turning to whatever deeds they did and making them like scattered dust. Even apparently good actions, charity given for reputation, generosity performed for influence, lose all value when the intention behind them was purely worldly.\n\nA misunderstanding should be cleared here. This is not saying that a person who works for a living, builds a business, or seeks a good life has ruined his hereafter. The Quran praises seeking Allah's bounty and mentions those who ask for good in this world and good in the Hereafter together. What is being described is the person whose entire aim ends at the horizon of this life.\n\nThe practical question the verse leaves is not about how much a person owns, but about what he is finally working for. Two people can perform identical actions and receive completely different results.",
  "17": "\"˹Can these people be compared to˺ those ˹believers˺ who stand on clear proof from their Lord\" sets up a contrast with the worldly people just described. Those people had nothing to stand on except desire and results. These stand on something firm. The Arabic word \"bayyinah\" means a clear evidence that makes the truth obvious, and standing upon it suggests solid ground under the feet rather than an opinion held loosely.\n\n\"Backed by ˹the Quran as˺ a witness from Him\" adds a second support. Commentators differ over what the witness refers to. Many understood it as the Quran testifying to the truth of the Prophet's message, while others reported the view that it refers to the angel Gabriel bringing it down, and some early scholars mentioned other possibilities. What is agreed is that the believer's position is not a private hunch; it is supported by testimony from Allah Himself.\n\n\"And preceded by the Book of Moses ˹which was revealed˺ as a guide and mercy\" brings in a third support, and it is historical. The Torah came before, teaching the same fundamentals and describing what would come. Its description as a guide and mercy is worth noticing. The Quran does not treat earlier scripture with contempt; it honours what was revealed while correcting what people later changed.\n\nSo the believer's certainty rests on three things: clear evidence, present testimony, and the agreement of earlier revelation. That is a considerable foundation compared to the guesswork the previous chapter kept describing.\n\n\"But whoever from the ˹disbelieving˺ groups rejects it, the Fire will be their destiny\" widens the warning beyond the Meccans to every faction that refuses, whatever its background or religious label. Membership of a community saves nobody.\n\n\"So do not be in doubt of it. It is certainly the truth from your Lord\" is the same style of address seen at the end of the previous chapter, where a statement is directed at the Prophet while the audience is everyone listening.\n\n\"But most people do not believe\" ends soberly, reminding the reader once more that numbers have never been the measure of truth.",
  "18": "\"Who does more wrong than those who fabricate lies against Allah?\" is a question shaped so that it answers itself. Nobody does. Among all forms of injustice the Quran describes, inventing falsehood about God is placed at the very top, and the reason is worth thinking through. A lie about a person harms that person's reputation. A lie about God misdirects worship, distorts what is lawful and forbidden, and misleads everyone who trusts the liar, sometimes for generations.\n\nThe previous chapter raised the same point when it asked what those who fabricate lies against Allah expect on Judgment Day. Here the answer is supplied in the form of a courtroom scene.\n\n\"They will be brought before their Lord\" places them in a position they spent their lives denying. The word suggests being presented, displayed for inspection, with no opportunity to arrange the evidence beforehand.\n\n\"And the witnesses will say, \"These are the ones who lied against their Lord.\"\" introduces testimony. The identity of these witnesses has been discussed by commentators, who mention the angels who recorded the deeds, the prophets who delivered the message, and the believers who will testify about their communities. The Quran elsewhere describes other witnesses too, including a person's own limbs speaking about what they did. Whichever is intended, the effect is the same: the accusation is not made in secret, and denial is impossible.\n\nThere is something particularly heavy about being exposed publicly by those who knew. Many of these people were respected figures in their societies, whose invented rules were followed by others precisely because of their standing. On that Day the audience remains, but the verdict is reversed.\n\n\"Surely Allah's condemnation is upon the wrongdoers\" delivers the sentence. The Arabic word \"la'nah\" means being driven far away from Allah's mercy, which is the essence of the punishment rather than merely an angry curse.\n\nThe practical warning in this reaches ordinary people, not only famous scholars. Anyone who declares confidently what Allah permits or forbids, or what He loves or hates, without knowledge, has stepped onto this ground. Saying honestly that one does not know has always been part of religious knowledge, not a failure of it.",
  "19": "\"Who hinder ˹others˺ from Allah's Path\" continues describing the wrongdoers named in the previous verse, and the description moves from what they believe to what they do to other people. Their crime is no longer private. They stand in the road and turn others back.\n\nThe Arabic verb indicates blocking, preventing, and turning away. This happened in Mecca in very concrete ways. The leaders warned visiting tribes not to listen to the Prophet, stationed men on the roads during the pilgrimage season to intercept travellers, tortured slaves who accepted Islam, and eventually imposed a boycott that cut the Muslims off from food and trade. Every one of those actions was an attempt to keep others away from the path.\n\n\"Striving to make it ˹appear˺ crooked\" describes a second, subtler method. Rather than blocking the road, they misrepresent it. The Arabic suggests seeking to bend something that is straight, and the wish is to make the path look distorted, unreasonable, or unattractive so that people will avoid it by choice. In Mecca this took the form of calling the Prophet a poet, a soothsayer, or a man who divided families. The road itself was straight, so the only option was to make it appear otherwise.\n\nBoth methods are still recognisable. Faith can be obstructed by force, and it can be obstructed by caricature, which is often more effective because it requires no violence and leaves the audience feeling they reached their own conclusion.\n\n\"And disbelieve in the Hereafter\" identifies what makes such behaviour possible. A person who genuinely expected to answer for turning others away from God would not do it. Removing the reckoning from the picture removes the restraint, which is why the Quran so often ties moral collapse to denial of the Last Day.\n\nThere is a sobering weight in this for anyone in a position of influence. A person who discourages a family member from praying, mocks someone's return to religion, or makes practice appear ridiculous has taken a small share in the behaviour this verse condemns, and the effects can outlast the moment considerably.",
  "20": "\"They will never frustrate Allah on earth\" answers a thought that naturally follows the previous verse. Those who block others from the path often seem to succeed. They hold power, control opinion, and go unpunished for years. The verse states plainly that this appearance is temporary and shallow. The Arabic expression means they cannot escape Him, outrun Him, or render Him unable to reach them. Nowhere on earth places anyone beyond His grasp.\n\n\"And they will have no protector besides Allah\" removes the other hope. In tribal Arabia, protection was everything. A man without a powerful clan behind him could be killed with no consequence, and much of the persecution the early Muslims suffered fell hardest on those who had no protector. The verse tells the persecutors that in the matter that finally counts, they are the ones with nobody to shield them.\n\n\"Their punishment will be multiplied\" explains why their case is heavier than that of an ordinary wrongdoer. They did not merely reject for themselves; they turned others away, and the harm they set in motion continued in people who never met them. The Prophet described this principle in a hadith recorded by Muslim, saying that whoever calls to misguidance carries a burden equal to the burdens of those who follow him, without lessening their own burdens in the least.\n\n\"For they failed to hear or see ˹the truth˺\" gives the reason for their condition, and it recalls language used in the previous chapter about people who listen but cannot be made to hear and who look but cannot be guided. The Arabic here indicates an inability that grew out of unwillingness. They were not physically deaf or blind; they had refused so consistently that the faculties stopped functioning for this purpose.\n\nThe verse contains a quiet warning about how a soul deteriorates. Refusal repeated long enough stops being a choice made each time and becomes a condition a person lives inside. This is why the Quran urges responding early, while hearing and sight still work.",
  "21": "\"It is they who have ruined themselves\" delivers the verdict on the fabricators and obstructers described in the preceding verses. The wording locates the damage precisely. They ruined themselves. Nobody did it to them, and this matches the principle stated in the previous chapter, that Allah does not wrong people in the least but it is people who wrong themselves.\n\nThe Arabic word here indicates loss in the sense of a failed business, ending with less than you began with. What they lost was not property but their own souls, which the Quran repeatedly identifies as the only capital a person truly owns. Surah az-Zumar describes the greatest loss as losing oneself and one's family on the Day of Resurrection, and calls it precisely that, the manifest loss.\n\nThe irony is that these were people who considered themselves successful. They had status, wealth, followers, and the confidence that comes from being obeyed. In the accounting that matters, they turn out to have spent everything they had on nothing.\n\n\"And whatever ˹gods˺ they fabricated will fail them\" completes the collapse. The Arabic verb indicates something wandering away and being lost, so that when a person reaches for it, it is simply not there. Everything they invented, the idols, the imagined intercessors, the false rulings they attached to Allah, disappears at the moment they are needed.\n\nThe Quran describes this abandonment in several places, including scenes where those who were worshipped disown their worshippers and declare that they never asked to be worshipped. Nothing they built comes to their defence.\n\nAn everyday comparison helps. A person may spend years cultivating relationships with influential people, confident that these connections will protect him if trouble comes. When real trouble arrives, the phone calls are not returned. That experience, painful as it is, is only a small picture of what this verse describes.\n\nThe point being made throughout this passage is consistent. Whatever a person leans on other than Allah will hold his weight for a while, and will give way exactly when he needs it most.",
  "22": "\"Without a doubt, they will be the worst losers in the Hereafter\" closes this section with a summary judgement, and its brevity gives it weight. Everything described over the preceding verses, fabricating lies about Allah, blocking others from His path, denying the Hereafter, comes to this single conclusion.\n\nThe opening expression indicates certainty, meaning there is no question about it and no room for argument. In a chapter that has repeatedly discussed doubt, this is a deliberate contrast. The deniers were unsure about resurrection and demanded proofs; the Quran states their outcome without any hesitation at all.\n\nBeing described as the worst losers invites a comparison. Loss is a familiar human experience. People lose money, health, reputation, homes, and those they love, and each of these is genuinely painful. What the verse claims is that all such losses are small beside this one. The reason is straightforward: every worldly loss happens within a life that continues and can improve, while this loss is final, with nothing left afterwards to recover with.\n\nThe Arabic construction used here is emphatic, indicating not merely that they are among the losers but that they are the greatest of them. Their loss is worse than that of an ordinary sinner because of the scale of what they did, having damaged others as well as themselves, as the earlier verses described.\n\nIt is worth noticing where the loss occurs, \"in the Hereafter,\" because it implies that this was not visible earlier. During their lives these people appeared to be winning by every measure their society recognised. The results were simply not in yet.\n\nThe Quran uses this trading language deliberately and often. Life is presented as a transaction in which a fixed amount of time, energy, and attention is spent on something. Surah al-Asr states that humanity is in loss, then names the exceptions: those who believe, do righteous deeds, and encourage one another to truth and patience. Everyone is spending. The only question is what is being purchased, and this verse names the buyers who came away with nothing.",
  "23": "\"Surely those who believe, do good, and humble themselves before their Lord will be the residents of Paradise\" turns from the losers to the winners, and the contrast is placed deliberately. The Quran often pairs the two descriptions so that a reader sees both outcomes together rather than dwelling on one alone.\n\nThree qualities are named, and they build on each other. Belief comes first, since it is the foundation that gives everything else meaning. Good deeds come second, because faith that produces no action is a claim rather than a reality, and the Quran almost always mentions the two together.\n\nThe third quality is the one that gives this verse its distinct character. The Arabic word \"akhbatu\" comes from a root that describes low, level ground, land that is soft and easy to walk on. Applied to a person, it indicates humility, calmness, and a heart that submits without resistance. Commentators have explained it as tranquillity before Allah, sincerity, and the absence of arrogance.\n\nThe choice of this word matters because a person can believe and can perform many good deeds while remaining proud. Pride can grow out of religious practice itself, producing someone who prays, gives charity, and looks down on everyone who does less. This third quality closes that door. What is being described is a believer whose heart has become soft toward Allah, not hardened by his own record.\n\nThe image of level ground is worth holding on to. Hard, rocky ground rejects water and grows nothing. Soft, low ground receives rain and becomes fertile. Hearts work the same way with guidance.\n\n\"They will be there forever\" answers the anxiety that shadows every good thing in this life. Everything here ends, which is why even great happiness carries a trace of sadness. This reward does not end, and the Quran repeatedly stresses this permanence.\n\nThe Prophet warned in a hadith recorded by Muslim that no one with an atom's weight of arrogance in his heart will enter Paradise, and he explained arrogance as rejecting the truth and looking down on people. This verse describes the opposite disposition, and promises it the opposite end.",
  "24": "\"The example of these two parties is that of the blind and the deaf, compared to the seeing and the hearing\" gathers the whole discussion into a single comparison. Two groups have been described at length: those who wanted only this world and fabricated lies, and those who believed, acted well, and humbled themselves. Rather than continue arguing, the Quran now offers a picture.\n\nThe comparison is carefully built. Loss of sight and loss of hearing represent the two ways knowledge normally reaches a person. Sight takes in evidence directly, and hearing receives what is transmitted by others. The believer has both: he can see the signs in creation and hear the message from the messengers. The denier has lost both channels, which is exactly what the previous chapter said about people who listen without hearing and look without seeing.\n\nThere is also a practical dimension. Someone who can see and hear can move through the world, avoid dangers, and reach destinations. Someone deprived of both is dependent, vulnerable, and easily lost. Applied to religion, one person is navigating life with information about where he came from, why he is here, and where he is going, while the other is making decisions in the dark.\n\n\"Can the two be equal?\" asks the obvious question. Nobody would claim that these two conditions are the same, and no one asked to choose between them would hesitate. The Quran uses this rhetorical method often, placing a spiritual truth beside a physical fact so obvious that denying it becomes impossible.\n\nIt should be said clearly that this is not a comment on people with physical disabilities. The Quran elsewhere honours such people and corrected the Prophet himself when he turned away from a blind man seeking guidance. The blindness described here is entirely a matter of the heart, and a person with perfect eyesight may possess it while a blind man sees the truth clearly.\n\n\"Will you not then be mindful?\" closes with an appeal to memory and reflection, asking listeners to draw the conclusion themselves rather than being handed it.",
  "25": "“Surely We sent Noah to his people. ˹He said,˺ “Indeed, I am sent to you with a clear warning”” begins the long series of prophetic histories that occupy this chapter, and the commentators note the place of Noah at the head of the series. The verse opens with the affirmation of the mission: “Surely We sent Noah to his people,” or “wa laqad arsalna Noohan ila qawmihi,” and the commentators explain that Noah was the first of the messengers sent to a people sunk in idolatry, the pioneer of the long line whose stories the chapter recounts, and his mission is the template of every mission that follows. Ibn Kathir notes the phrase “to his people,” for the messenger is always one of the people, sent to call them from within, and the kinship of the messenger is the proof of the mercy of the message, the sending of the familiar to save the familiar. The verse then records Noah’s opening words: “Indeed, I am sent to you with a clear warning,” or “innee lakum natheerun mubeen,” and the commentators note the two words, the “natheer,” the warner, and the “mubeen,” the clear, for Noah declared himself the warner with the manifest warning, the warning that left no room for the plea of the ignorance, and the clarity of the warning is the measure of the responsibility that it placed upon the people. The verse is the opening of the account, and the statement of the warning that the chapter will trace through the rejection and the flood.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the warning, and the commentators connect it to the whole series that follows. The chapter presents the messengers in succession — Noah, Hud, Salih, Abraham, Lot, Shu‘ayb, and Moses — and each comes with the same warning, and the repetition of the warning is the teaching, for the message of the prophets is one, and the warning that Noah delivered is the warning that the Prophet Muhammad was delivering, and the one who rejects the warning of his age is the one who repeats the rejection of every age. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse is the first note of the chapter’s great theme: the warning is sent, and the response of the people decides their fate, and the believer is summoned to be among those who heed the warning rather than among those who repeat the rejection. The verse is the opening of the series, and the model of the clear warning that every prophet carried.",
  "26": "“that you should worship none but Allah. I truly fear for you the torment of a painful Day.”” states the content of the warning Noah brought, and the commentators note the two parts of the message. The first is the command: “that you should worship none but Allah,” or “an la ta‘budoo illa Allaha,” and the commentators explain the phrase, for the entire warning of Noah was the call to the worship of the One, the “tawhid” that is the foundation of every message, and the command to worship none but Allah is the negative and the positive together, the denial of every false god and the affirmation of the One, and the command is the heart of the warning that Noah delivered. Ibn Kathir notes the precedence of the worship in the message, for the call to the oneness of God is the first of every prophet’s duties, and the worship of the One is the root from which every other obedience grows. The second part is the warning: “I truly fear for you the torment of a painful Day,” or “innee akhafu ‘alaykum ‘athaba yawmin aleem,” and the commentators note the motive of the fear, for Noah warned his people of the punishment of the Day of Judgment, and the fear that drove the warning was the fear of the love, the anxiety of the one who wished his people to be saved, and the “yawm aleem,” the painful Day, is the Day of the reckoning that the heedless denied.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the order of the message, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own calling. The believer is taught that the call to the worship of the One is the first of the duties, and the warning of the punishment is the second, and the two together are the complete message, the command and the warning, the invitation to the mercy and the caution against the wrath, and the one who calls to God must join the two, for the worship alone without the warning is incomplete, and the warning alone without the worship is the fear without the direction. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the structure of the da‘wah: the affirmation of the One and the warning of the Day, and the believer who delivers the message delivers both, the command that saves and the warning that cautions. The verse is the statement of the message, and the model of the warning that the worship and the fear together compose.",
  "27": "“The disbelieving chiefs of his people said, “We see you only as a human being like ourselves, and we see that no one follows you except the lowliest among us, who do so ˹hastily˺ without thinking. We do not see anything that makes ˹all of˺ you any better than us. In fact, we think you are liars.”” records the answer of the chiefs to the warning, and the commentators note the three objections that the verse joins. The first is the objection of the humanity: “We see you only as a human being like ourselves,” or “ma naraka illa basharan mithlana,” and the commentators explain the objection, for the chiefs found it incredible that a mere man should be the messenger of God, and the objection is the oldest of the rejecters’ arguments, the refusal of the truth because its bearer is a fellow human. Ibn Kathir notes the irony, for the humanity of the messenger is the very mercy of the message, and the people who rejected the messenger for his humanity were the people who would have rejected the angel for his strangeness, and the objection was the excuse that the arrogance had found. The second is the objection of the followers: “we see that no one follows you except the lowliest among us, who do so hastily without thinking,” or “wa ma naraka ittaba‘aka illa allatheena hum arathiluna badiya ar-ra’y,” and the commentators explain the sneer, for the chiefs despised the poor and the weak who had believed, and the despising of the followers was the despising of the message, and the word “badiya ar-ra’y” — the hasty, the unthinking — was the accusation that the believers had followed without reflection, and the accusation was the reverse of the truth, for the believers had reflected and believed, while the chiefs had reflected and refused. The third is the verdict: “We do not see anything that makes all of you any better than us. In fact, we think you are liars,” or “ma nara lakum ‘alayna min fadlin bal nazunnukum kathibeen,” and the commentators note the finality, for the chiefs saw no merit in the believers and judged the messengers liars, and the judgment was the seal of their rejection.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the three objections, and the commentators connect them to the believer’s own trials. The believer is taught that the truth is rejected first for its bearer, then for its followers, and then for its claim, and the three objections are the three stages of the rejection that the truth faces in every age, and the believer who carries the truth must expect the sneer at the humanity, the contempt for the lowly, and the charge of the lie, and must answer all three with the steadfastness that the truth itself supplies. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the nature of the opposition: the powerful reject the truth because it comes through the weak, and the contempt for the followers is the contempt for the truth, and the believer who is despised for the faith is the believer whose company is the company of Noah and his few. The verse is the record of the rejection, and the warning against the three objections that the truth always meets.",
  "28": "“He said, “O my people! Consider if I stand on a clear proof from my Lord and He has blessed me with a mercy from Himself, which you fail to see. Should we ˹then˺ force it on you against your will?”” is how Noah answers men who have just called him a liar and insulted his followers, and the commentators note the gentleness of the reply. Noah did not answer the insult with the insult but with the appeal of the reason: “O my people! Consider if I stand on a clear proof from my Lord,” or “ya qawmi a-ra’aytum in kuntu ‘ala bayyinatin min rabbee,” and the commentators explain the form, for the “a-ra’aytum” is the invitation to consider, the asking of the people to suppose, for the sake of the argument, that the messenger stood upon the clear proof, and the supposition was the method of the prophet, the leading of the people to the truth through their own reflection. Ibn Kathir notes the force of the question, for the people who had accused Noah of the error were being asked to consider the possibility that the error was their own, and the question was the mercy that the accusation had not extinguished. The verse then states the mercy and the blindness: “and He has blessed me with a mercy from Himself, which you fail to see,” or “wa atanee rahmatan min ‘indihi fa-‘ummiyat ‘alaykum,” and the commentators explain the “rahmat,” the mercy, the prophethood and the guidance that Noah had received, and the “‘ummiyat,” the blinding, the failure of the people to see it, and the failure of the sight is the diagnosis of the rejection, for the truth was present and the people could not see it. The verse closes with the question: “Should we then force it on you against your will?” or “a-nulzimukumooha wa antum laha karihoon,” and the commentators note the principle, for the faith cannot be forced, and the prophet does not compel the unwilling, and the question is the declaration of the freedom that the truth respects.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the method, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own calling. The believer is taught that the response to the accusation is not the anger but the invitation to the reflection, and the question “consider if” is the method of the prophets, the asking of the opponent to weigh the possibility, and the one who calls to the truth with the question has called with the mercy, for the question leaves the door open that the anger would close. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the patience of the messenger: Noah answered the insult with the appeal, the lie with the question, and the contempt with the gentleness, and the gentleness is the mark of the one who seeks the salvation of the people rather than the victory over them. The verse is the model of the patient reply, and the teaching of the question that the anger cannot ask.",
  "29": "“O my people! I do not ask you for a payment for this ˹message˺. My reward is only from Allah. And I will never dismiss the believers, for they will surely meet their Lord. But I can see that you are a people acting ignorantly.”” removes the suspicion that has been hinted at, and the commentators note the three declarations that the verse joins. The first is the disavowal of the reward: “I do not ask you for a payment for this message. My reward is only from Allah,” or “wa ya qawmi la as’alukum ‘alayhi malan in ajriya illa ‘ala Allahi,” and the commentators note the refrain, for the disavowal of the payment is the declaration of every prophet, the assurance that the message is not a trade and the messenger is not a merchant, and the one who seeks the reward from God alone is the one whose call cannot be silenced by the withholding of the world’s goods. Ibn Kathir notes the suspicion that the declaration removes, for the chiefs had imagined that Noah sought the leadership or the gain, and the declaration of the reward was the answer to the suspicion. The second is the refusal to dismiss the believers: “And I will never dismiss the believers, for they will surely meet their Lord,” or “wa ma ana bi-taridi allatheena amanoo innahum mulaqoo rabbihim,” and the commentators explain the context, for the chiefs had demanded that Noah drive away the poor and the weak who followed him, and Noah refused, and the refusal was the defence of the dignity of the believers, the declaration that the lowly in the eyes of the world are the honoured in the sight of the Lord. The third is the verdict: “But I can see that you are a people acting ignorantly,” or “wa lakinnee arakum qawman tajhaloon,” and the commentators note the word “tajhaloon,” the ignorant, for the demand that the believers be dismissed was the demand of the ignorance, and the verdict of Noah was the description of the state that the demand revealed.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the dignity of the believers, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own society. The believer is taught that the worth of a soul is not measured by the wealth or the status that the world assigns, and the believer who is despised by the powerful is the believer whom the Lord will meet and honour, and the one who would dismiss the weak for the sake of the powerful has dismissed the very ones whom the Lord has chosen. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the constancy of the messenger: Noah refused the trade and refused the dismissal, and the two refusals are the two pillars of the prophetic integrity, the freedom from the wealth and the loyalty to the weak, and the believer who follows the messenger follows both. The verse is the declaration of the integrity, and the defence of the dignity of the believers.",
  "30": "“O my people! Who would protect me from Allah if I were to dismiss them? Will you not then be mindful?”” turns the chiefs’ proposal back on them, and the commentators note the two questions and their force. The first is the question of the protection: “Who would protect me from Allah if I were to dismiss them?” or “wa ya qawmi man yansurunee mina Allahi in taradtuhum,” and the commentators explain the argument, for the dismissal of the believers would be the sin that the chiefs were demanding, and the one who commits the sin has no protector against the punishment, and the question placed before the chiefs the reality that they had ignored, the reality that the dismissal of the believers would draw the wrath of God, and no one could shield the one who drew it. Ibn Kathir notes the force of the question, for the chiefs had demanded the dismissal as if it were a small matter, and Noah revealed that it was the sin for which there is no protector, and the revelation was the correction of the scale by which the chiefs had weighed the believers. The second is the summons: “Will you not then be mindful?” or “afala tathakkaroon,” and the commentators note the appeal, for the question of the protection was followed by the call to the remembrance, the invitation to the mindfulness that the chiefs had lost, and the two questions together are the complete rebuke, the exposure of the sin and the summons to the correction.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the scale, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own judgment. The believer is taught that the worth of the souls is weighed by the Lord, and the one who is despised by the world may be the one whom the Lord protects, and the dismissal of the believer is the sin whose protection no one can offer, and the one who would curry the favour of the powerful by the injury of the weak has traded the protection of the Lord for the protection that no creature can give. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the fear of the dismissal: the dismissal of the believer is the dismissal of the one whom the Lord has accepted, and the one who drives away the accepted has driven away the acceptance, and the question “who would protect me from Allah” is the question that every leader must ask before he despises the weak. The verse is the rebuke of the proposal, and the teaching of the scale that the Lord alone sets.",
  "31": "“I do not say to you that I possess Allah’s treasuries or know the unseen, nor do I claim to be an angel, nor do I say that Allah will never grant goodness to those you look down upon. Allah knows best what is ˹hidden˺ within them. ˹If I did,˺ then I would truly be one of the wrongdoers.”” sets out the limits Noah honored, and the commentators note the three disclaimers and their meaning. The first is the disclaimer of the wealth and the unseen: “I do not say to you that I possess Allah’s treasuries or know the unseen,” or “la aqoolu lakum ‘indee khaza’inu Allahi wa la a‘lamu al-ghayb,” and the commentators explain the meaning, for Noah declared that he was not the owner of the divine treasures and not the knower of the hidden, and the declaration was the correction of the false expectations that the people had attached to the messenger, the expectation that the prophet should be the rich or the seer, and the correction was the humility of the servant who claims nothing for himself. The second is the disclaimer of the angelhood: “nor do I claim to be an angel,” or “wa la aqoolu innee malak,” and the commentators note the significance, for the people had demanded that the messenger be an angel, and Noah declared that he was the man that God had sent, and the humanity of the messenger is the mercy that the people had scorned. The third is the disclaimer of the judgment: “nor do I say that Allah will never grant goodness to those you look down upon,” or “wa la aqoolu lillatheena tazdaree a‘yunukum lan yu’tiyahumu Allahu khayran,” and Ibn Kathir explains the meaning, for Noah refused to judge the hidden worth of the believers whom the chiefs despised, and the refusal was the humility of the one who leaves the judgment of the hearts to the One who knows them. The verse closes with the confession: “Allah knows best what is hidden within them. If I did, then I would truly be one of the wrongdoers,” or “Allahu a‘lamu bima fee anfusihim innee ithan la-mina az-zalimeen,” and the commentators note the fear of the messenger, for the claim to the knowledge of the hidden would be the wrong, and the fear of the wrong is the guard of the humility.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the limits, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own speech. The believer is taught to know the limits of the station, and to claim nothing that the station does not carry, and the one who speaks of the treasuries and the unseen and the hidden worth of the hearts has exceeded the limit that the messenger himself refused to cross, and the humility of Noah is the model of the servant who knows his place. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the discipline of the tongue: the messenger claimed nothing but the message, and the believer who claims more has claimed what the messenger renounced, and the fear of the wrong is the beginning of the truth. The verse is the declaration of the limits, and the teaching of the humility that the station demands.",
  "32": "“They protested, “O Noah! You have argued with us far too much, so bring upon us what you threaten us with, if what you say is true.”” marks the moment the discussion ended, and the commentators note the two parts of the protest. The first is the weariness: “You have argued with us far too much,” or “qad jadaltana fa-aktharta jidalana,” and the commentators explain the meaning, for the people of Noah, after the long years of the debate, declared that the argument had grown long, and the declaration was the confession of their own exhaustion, the weariness of the one who has heard the truth and refused it and found the refusal itself a burden. Ibn Kathir notes the irony, for the argument that the people called “too much” was the patience of the prophet who had called them for nine hundred and fifty years, and the weariness of the people was the measure of their hardness, for the heart that is closed to the truth grows weary of the truth that the open heart drinks. The second part is the challenge: “so bring upon us what you threaten us with, if what you say is true,” or “fa’tina bima ta‘iduna in kunta mina as-sadiqeen,” and the commentators note the recklessness of the challenge, for the people demanded the punishment that they had been warned of, and the demand was the dare of the one who does not believe the warning, and the dare was the last word of the rejection, the invitation of the destruction that the warning had described.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the weariness, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own persistence. The believer is taught that the truth must be repeated until the door is closed, and the weariness of the rejecter is not the signal of the silence, for the prophet who was accused of the long argument is the prophet whose patience the Lord rewarded, and the one who calls to the truth must continue until the decree, for the weariness of the people is the test of the caller’s constancy. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the patience of the message: the argument that the people called long was the mercy that the people refused, and the challenge that followed was the seal of the refusal, and the believer who faces the weariness of the heedless must remember that the weariness is not the failure of the truth but the fruit of the hardness. The verse is the record of the challenge, and the warning against the dare that the heedless fling.",
  "33": "“He responded, “It is Allah Who can bring it upon you if He wills, and then you will have no escape!”” corrects the mistake buried in the challenge, and the commentators note the two parts of the answer. The first is the correction: “It is Allah Who can bring it upon you if He wills,” or “innama ya’teekum bihi Allahu in sha’a,” and the commentators explain the meaning, for the people had demanded that Noah bring the punishment, as if the punishment were in the hand of the prophet, and Noah corrected the demand, declaring that the punishment is in the hand of God alone, and the will of God is the condition of its coming, and the correction is the teaching that the messenger is the warner, not the punisher, and the punishment belongs to the One who alone can send it. Ibn Kathir notes the significance, for the challenge of the people had misdirected the demand, and the answer of Noah returned the matter to its true Owner, and the return is the lesson of the verse, the teaching that the judgment of the people is the decree of the Lord, not the act of the servant. The second part is the warning: “and then you will have no escape!” or “wa ma antum bi-mu‘jizeen,” and the commentators explain the word, for the “mu‘jizeen” are the ones who escape, the ones who elude, and the declaration is that the people would have no escape from the punishment when it came, and the declaration is the answer to the dare, for the ones who challenged the punishment would find, when it came, that the challenge had bought them nothing but the inability to flee.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the attribution, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own speech. The believer is taught to attribute the punishment to the Lord alone, and to know that the messenger is the warner and the Lord is the judge, and the one who speaks of the punishment as the act of the servant has confused the roles, and the correction of the confusion is the beginning of the reverence. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the humility of the warner: Noah did not claim the power to punish but declared that the punishment is God’s, and the believer who warns must warn with the same humility, the acknowledgment that the judgment is the Lord’s and the warning is the servant’s. The verse is the correction of the challenge, and the teaching of the attribution that the truth demands.",
  "34": "“My advice will not benefit you—no matter how hard I try—if Allah wills ˹for˺ you to stray. He is your Lord, and to Him you will ˹all˺ be returned.”” is Noah’s sober final word, and the commentators note the two truths that the verse joins. The first is the limit of the advice: “My advice will not benefit you—no matter how hard I try—if Allah wills for you to stray,” or “wa la yanfa‘ukum nushee in aradtu an ansaha lakum in kana Allahu yureedu an yughwiyakum,” and the commentators explain the meaning, for the advice of the prophet, however sincere and however prolonged, cannot benefit the one whom God has willed to stray, and the willing of the straying is the consequence of the rejection, the sealing of the heart that the persistence has earned, and the declaration is not the despair of the prophet but the statement of the reality, the acknowledgment that the guidance is the gift of the Lord and the misguidance is the fruit of the refusal. Ibn Kathir explains the principle, for the verse teaches that the benefit of the advice is not in the power of the advisor but in the will of the Lord, and the one who has willed the straying has shut the door that the advice sought to open. The second truth is the return: “He is your Lord, and to Him you will all be returned,” or “huwa rabbukum wa ilayhi turja‘oon,” and the commentators note the closing, for the declaration of the return to the Lord is the answer to the challenge of the punishment, the assurance that the reckoning is certain and the escape is impossible, and the return to the Lord is the end that the people had denied and the prophet affirmed.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the limit, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own effort. The believer is taught that the effort of the caller is real but the outcome is the Lord’s, and the caller who has delivered the message has done his duty, and the benefit of the advice is not his to grant but the Lord’s to give, and the one who has willed the straying has removed himself from the benefit, and the caller must continue with the effort and leave the outcome to the One who alone decides it. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the peace of the caller: Noah did not despair and did not cease, but he knew the limit of the advice, and the knowledge of the limit is the peace of the one who has done his duty and entrusted the result to the Lord. The verse is the statement of the limit, and the teaching of the trust that the caller must hold.",
  "35": "“Or do they say, “He has fabricated this ˹Quran˺!”? Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ “If I have done so, then I bear the burden of that sin! But I am free from your sinful accusation.”” interrupts the story of Noah and speaks directly to the Prophet’s own accusers, and the commentators note the shift and the argument. The verse turns from the narrative to the present: “Or do they say, ‘He has fabricated this Quran!’?” or “am yaqooloona iftarahu,” and the commentators explain the occasion, for the disbelievers of Mecca had accused the Prophet of the fabrication of the Quran, and the verse answers the accusation by the mouth of the Prophet, and the shift from the story of Noah to the accusation of the Quraysh is the teaching, for the accusation of the fabrication is the same accusation that every people had leveled at its messenger, and the Prophet is the heir of the accusation that Noah and his successors had borne. Ibn Kathir notes the connection, for the story of Noah was being related to the Prophet as the comfort of the one who faces the same rejection, and the verse steps out of the story to address the rejection directly. The verse then records the answer: “Say, O Prophet, ‘If I have done so, then I bear the burden of that sin! But I am free from your sinful accusation,’” or “qul ini iftaraytuhu fa-‘alayya ijramee wa ana baree’un mimma tujrimoon,” and the commentators explain the argument, for the Prophet was commanded to say that if he had fabricated the Quran, the sin of the fabrication would be his own, and that he was free of the crime of the accusers, and the answer is the declaration of the confidence of the truthful, for the one who knows his own innocence can leave the accusation to its accuser.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the accusation, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own defence. The believer is taught that the accusation of the fabrication is the oldest of the charges, and the answer to it is not the anger but the declaration of the innocence, and the one who is accused of the lie and knows his truth can say, as the Prophet was commanded, that the burden of the lie, if it were his, would be his own, and that he is free of the crime of the accuser, and the declaration is the peace of the truthful. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the confidence of the innocence: the accusation of the falsehood is the tribute of the falsehood, and the truthful heart that knows its truth leaves the accusation to its author, for the burden of the lie is the burden of the liar, and the free is the one who spoke the truth. The verse is the answer to the accusation, and the teaching of the peace of the truthful.",
  "36": "“And it was revealed to Noah, “None of your people will believe except those who already have. So do not be distressed by what they have been doing.”” brings the divine word that ends the long calling, and the commentators note the two parts of the revelation. The first is the decree: “None of your people will believe except those who already have,” or “annahu lan yu’mina min qawmika illa man qad amana,” and the commentators explain the meaning, for the revelation declared that the door of the belief had closed, and that no one beyond the few who had already believed would be added to them, and the declaration was the end of the calling, the divine verdict upon the people whose rejection had persisted through the centuries of the warning. Ibn Kathir notes the significance, for the revelation did not come until the people had exhausted every mercy, and the closing of the door was the fruit of the persistence, the divine confirmation of the rejection that the people had chosen. The second part is the comfort: “So do not be distressed by what they have been doing,” or “fa-la tabta’is bima kanoo yaf‘aloon,” and the commentators note the tenderness of the address, for the Lord comforted His prophet, and the comfort was the mercy of the One who knew the grief of the messenger, the grief of the one who had called and been refused, and the command not to grieve was the release of the prophet from the sorrow that the rejection had caused, for the matter had been decided and the grief was no longer of use.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the closed door, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own calling. The believer is taught that the calling has its term, and the door of the belief, once closed by the decree, is not reopened by the effort, and the caller who has delivered the message and seen the door close is the caller whose duty is done, and the grief over the closed door is the grief that the Lord Himself relieves, for the matter is the Lord’s and the effort is the servant’s. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the peace of the decree: Noah was told that the belief would not come, and he was told not to grieve, and the two words together are the complete comfort of the caller, the knowledge of the decree and the release from the sorrow, and the believer who knows both has found the peace that the effort alone cannot give. The verse is the record of the decree, and the teaching of the comfort that follows the closing.",
  "37": "“And build the Ark under Our ˹watchful˺ Eyes and directions, and do not plead with Me for those who have done wrong, for they will surely be drowned.”” moves Noah from speech to construction, and the commentators note the three elements of the command. The first is the command to build: “And build the Ark,” or “wasna‘ al-fulk,” and the commentators explain the scene, for Noah was commanded to build the great ship, the Ark, in the dry land, and the building of the ship in the waterless place was itself the sign and the test, the act of the obedience that the people would mock. Ibn Kathir notes the detail: Noah built the Ark with the planks and the nails, and the building took, according to the reports, a long time, and the people passed by and jeered, and the building was the patience of the prophet made visible. The second element is the phrase of the protection: “under Our watchful Eyes and directions,” or “bi-a‘yunina wa wahyina,” and the commentators explain the meaning, for the building was under the “a‘yun,” the eyes of the Lord, the watchful care that accompanied the construction, and under the “wahy,” the revelation, the directions that guided it, and the phrase is the assurance that the work of the believer is never done alone, and the eyes and the revelation are the companions of the obedience. The third is the prohibition: “and do not plead with Me for those who have done wrong, for they will surely be drowned,” or “wa la tukhatibnee fi allatheena zalamoo innahum mughraqoon,” and the commentators note the prohibition, for Noah was forbidden to intercede for the wrongdoers, and the prohibition was the declaration that the decree of the drowning was final, and the intercession for the doomed was of no avail.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the obedience, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own work. The believer is taught that the command of God is sometimes the building in the desert, the work whose wisdom is hidden, and the one who builds under the eyes of the Lord is the one whose work the Lord guides and protects, and the prohibition of the intercession is the teaching that the decree of the Lord is not reversed by the plea, and the believer who knows the decree submits to it without the bargaining. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the trust of the worker: the Ark was built in the dry land under the eyes of the Lord, and the flood that followed justified the building, and the believer who obeys the command that the world mocks is the believer whose work the Lord crowns. The verse is the command of the building, and the teaching of the obedience that the mockery cannot stop.",
  "38": "“So he began to build the Ark, and whenever some of the chiefs of his people passed by, they mocked him. He said, “If you laugh at us, we will ˹soon˺ laugh at you similarly.”” records the building and the mockery, and the commentators note the two responses that the verse joins. The verse describes the building: “So he began to build the Ark,” or “wa yasna‘u al-fulk,” and the mockery: “whenever some of the chiefs of his people passed by, they mocked him,” or “wa kullama marra ‘alayhi mala’un min qawmihi sakhiru minhu,” and the commentators explain the scene, for the chiefs of the people passed by the building and jeered, mocking the prophet who built a ship in the desert, and the mockery was the laughter of the heedless who could not see the flood that the ship was for, and the verse preserves the mockery as the measure of the blindness, for the ones who laughed at the ship were the ones whom the ship would not save. Ibn Kathir notes the detail: the people said, “O Noah, you have become a carpenter after being a prophet,” and the like, and the mockery was the scorn of the disbelievers for the obedience that they could not understand. The verse then records Noah’s answer: “If you laugh at us, we will soon laugh at you similarly,” or “in taskharoo minna fa-inna naskharu minkum kama taskharoon,” and the commentators note the calm of the reply, for Noah did not rage at the mockery but answered with the promise of the reversal, the declaration that the laughter would be returned, and the reversal would come when the flood rose and the ship sailed, and the ones who had laughed at the building would weep at the drowning.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the mockery, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own patience. The believer is taught that the obedience to the command of God is often the object of the world’s mockery, and the mocker is the one who cannot see the wisdom, and the believer who is mocked for the obedience is the believer whose patience the Lord rewards, and the promise of the reversal is the comfort of the mocked, for the laughter of the heedless is the prelude of the tears. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the endurance of the scorn: Noah built under the jeers and answered the jeers with the promise, and the believer who builds his obedience under the mockery of the age is the believer whose work the Lord will justify, and the laughter that is returned is the justice that the mockery invited. The verse is the record of the building and the mockery, and the teaching of the patience that the scorn cannot break.",
  "39": "“You will soon come to know who will be visited by a humiliating torment ˹in this life˺ and overwhelmed by an everlasting punishment ˹in the next˺.”” continues Noah’s reply to the mockers, and the commentators note the two punishments that the verse names. The verse is the completion of the promise of the reversal: “You will soon come to know who will be visited by a humiliating torment in this life,” or “fa-sawfa ta‘lamoona man ya’teehim ‘athabun yukhzeehi,” and the commentators explain the first punishment, the “athab yukhzeeh,” the torment that humiliates, the worldly punishment that disgraces, and the meaning, for the flood would be the humiliation of the mockers, the drowning that would strip them of the pride with which they had jeered. Ibn Kathir notes the connection, for the “soon” of the verse was the answer to the mockery, the promise that the knowledge would come, and the knowledge would be the drowning. The second punishment is then named: “and overwhelmed by an everlasting punishment in the next,” or “wa yahillu ‘alayhi ‘athabun muqeem,” and the commentators explain the “athab muqeem,” the abiding punishment, the torment of the Hereafter that remains, and the two punishments together are the complete recompense of the mockery, the humiliation of the world and the permanence of the next, and the verse places them before the mockers as the knowledge that the “soon” would bring.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the two punishments, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own perspective. The believer is taught that the mockery of the truth is the sin that earns the two punishments, the disgrace of the world and the permanence of the next, and the one who laughs at the obedience is the one who will know the two punishments that the verse names, and the believer who is mocked is the believer whose end is the reverse, the honour of the world and the permanence of the next. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the patience of the reversal: Noah did not curse the mockers but promised them the knowledge, and the knowledge that came was the flood and the Fire, and the believer who is mocked for the truth can leave the mockers to the knowledge that the Lord will bring, for the two punishments are the recompense of the two laughters. The verse is the completion of the promise, and the teaching of the recompense that the mockery invites.",
  "40": "“And when Our command came and the oven burst ˹with water˺, We said ˹to Noah˺, “Take into the Ark a pair from every species along with your family—except those against whom the decree ˹to drown˺ has already been passed—and those who believe.” But none believed with him except for a few.”” marks the moment the waiting ended, and the commentators describe the sign and the command. The verse states the sign: “when Our command came and the oven burst with water,” or “hatta itha ja’a amruna wa fara at-tannooru,” and the commentators explain the “tannur,” the oven, and the meaning, for the bursting of the oven with water was the sign of the flood, the gushing of the water from the earth that announced the beginning of the decree, and the reports differ on the location of the oven, but the meaning is one: the command of God came, and the water began, and the sign was the signal for the boarding. Ibn Kathir relates the detail: the oven was the baker’s oven, and the bursting of the oven was the sign that Noah had been given, and when it burst, the boarding began. The verse then records the command: “Take into the Ark a pair from every species along with your family—except those against whom the decree to drown has already been passed—and those who believe,” or “ihmil feeha min kullin zawjayni ithnayni wa ahlaka illa man sabaqa ‘alayhi al-qawlu minhum wa man amana,” and the commentators explain the contents of the Ark: the pairs of every species, the family of Noah except the doomed, and the believers, and the exception is the son and the wife who were not of the saved, and the verse closes with the scarcity: “But none believed with him except for a few,” or “wa ma amana ma‘ahu illa qaleel,” and the commentators note the smallness of the number, for after the centuries of the calling, the believers were the few, and the Ark carried the few and the pairs against the flood that would sweep the many.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the sign and the obedience, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own readiness. The believer is taught that the command of God has its signs, and the one who watches the signs is the one who is ready when the command comes, and the boarding of the Ark was the obedience that the sign had prepared, and the scarcity of the believers is the warning that the path of the truth is the path of the few, and the one who would be saved must be among the few who believed before the flood. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the readiness of the faithful: the oven burst, and the Ark was boarded, and the pairs and the family and the few were taken, and the believer who lives in the obedience is the believer who is ready for the sign that the decree will send. The verse is the record of the boarding, and the teaching of the readiness that the obedience produces.",
  "41": "“And he said, “Board it! In the Name of Allah it will sail and cast anchor. Surely my Lord is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.”” is Noah’s instruction to the saved, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The first is the command and the name: “Board it! In the Name of Allah it will sail and cast anchor,” or “irkaboo feeha bismi Allahi majraha wa mursaha,” and the commentators explain the meaning, for Noah commanded the boarding in the name of Allah, and the sailing and the anchoring of the Ark were attributed to the name of the Lord, the “bismillah” that the believer invokes at the beginning of every act, and the invocation of the name was the declaration that the voyage of the Ark was the voyage of the Lord, the sailing and the anchoring both in His hand. Ibn Kathir notes the teaching, for the verse establishes the sunnah of the invocation of the name at the beginning of the journey and the act, and the Ark that sailed in the name of the Lord was the model of the life that is lived in the name of the Lord. The second part is the praise: “Surely my Lord is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful,” or “inna rabbee la-ghafoorun raheem,” and the commentators note the closing, for the invocation of the name was followed by the praise of the attributes, and the praise of the All-Forgiving, the Most Merciful is the confession of the hope that the saved carried, the trust that the Lord who had saved them would forgive and show mercy, and the pairing of the name and the praise is the complete prayer of the believer at the threshold of the deliverance.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the name, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own life. The believer is taught to begin every act in the name of the Lord, and to attribute the movement and the rest of the life to the One whose name is invoked, and the Ark that sailed and anchored in the name of the Lord is the symbol of the life that the believer entrusts to the Lord, and the praise of the forgiving and the merciful is the hope that the entrusting carries. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the completeness of the reliance: the boarding was in the name, the voyage was in the name, and the praise was of the attributes, and the believer who lives in the name and the praise is the believer whose journey is the journey of the saved. The verse is the instruction of the boarding, and the teaching of the name that the believer invokes.",
  "42": "“And ˹so˺ the Ark sailed with them through waves like mountains. Noah called out to his son, who stood apart, “O my dear son! Come aboard with us and do not be with the disbelievers.”” gives the scale of the flood and the plea of the father, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The first is the description of the flood: “the Ark sailed with them through waves like mountains,” or “wa hiya tajree bihim fee mawjin kal-jibal,” and the commentators explain the image, for the waves of the flood rose like mountains, the “mawj kal-jibal,” the towering waters that lifted and dropped the Ark, and the scale of the waves is the scale of the destruction, the flood that covered the earth and left no height unbowed, and the Ark that sailed through the mountains of water was the ship of the saved, the vessel that the mercy of the Lord carried above the ruin. Ibn Kathir notes the vividness of the image, for the waves like mountains are the measure of the terror and the deliverance, the waters that drowned the earth and spared the Ark. The second part is the plea: “Noah called out to his son, who stood apart, ‘O my dear son! Come aboard with us and do not be with the disbelievers,’” or “wa nada Noohunu ibnahu wa kana fee ma‘zilin ya bunayya irkab ma‘ana wa la takun ma‘a al-kafireen,” and the commentators note the tenderness of the address, the “ya bunayya,” the “O my dear son,” the diminutive of the love, and the plea of the father to the son who stood apart, the call to board the Ark and to leave the company of the disbelievers, and the plea is the heart of the verse, the last attempt of the father to save the son, and the son’s refusal, which the next verse records, is the tragedy that the plea foreshadows.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the two paths, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own choice. The believer is taught that the flood of the trial separates the saved and the drowned, and the Ark is the faith and the waves are the ruin, and the father who calls is the mercy and the son who stands apart is the refusal, and the believer who hears the call is the believer who boards, and the one who stands apart has chosen the waves. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the urgency of the boarding: Noah called his son with the love of the father, and the call was the last mercy, and the refusal was the last choice, and the believer who is called to the Ark of the faith must board with the haste that the waves demand. The verse is the record of the plea, and the teaching of the choice that the flood forces.",
  "43": "“He replied, “I will take refuge on a mountain, which will protect me from the water.” Noah cried, “Today no one is protected from Allah’s decree except those to whom He shows mercy!” And the waves came between them, and his son was among the drowned.”” is the son’s refusal and the father’s final cry, and the commentators note the three parts of the verse. The first is the refusal: “I will take refuge on a mountain, which will protect me from the water,” or “sa-awee ila jabalin ya‘simunee mina al-ma’,” and the commentators explain the reply, for the son trusted the mountain, the height that the water would not reach, and the trust in the mountain was the trust in the created, the reliance upon the refuge that the flood would show to be no refuge, and the reply is the confession of the heart that trusted the world instead of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the irony, for the mountain that the son trusted was the mountain that the flood covered, and the refuge of the world is the refuge that the decree overcomes. The second part is the cry of Noah: “Today no one is protected from Allah’s decree except those to whom He shows mercy!” or “la ‘asima al-yawma min amri Allahi illa man rahima,” and the commentators note the truth of the cry, for the protection of that day was not the height of the mountain but the mercy of the Lord, and the one whom the mercy did not reach was the one whom no height could save, and the cry is the father’s last teaching to the son, the declaration of the truth that the son had refused. The third part is the outcome: “And the waves came between them, and his son was among the drowned,” or “wa hala baynahuma al-mawju fa-kana mina al-mughraqeen,” and the commentators note the brevity of the record, for the wave came between the father and the son, and the son was of the drowned, and the brevity is the gravity, the statement of the loss that the plea could not prevent.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the refuge, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own trust. The believer is taught that the refuge of the world is the refuge that fails, and the only refuge from the decree is the mercy, and the one who trusts the mountain instead of the mercy has trusted the thing that the decree will cover, and the believer who would be saved in the flood of the trial must seek the mercy, not the height. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the truth of the protection: the son trusted the mountain and drowned, and the father trusted the Lord and sailed, and the two trusts and the two ends are the lesson, for the protection of that day is the mercy, and the mercy is the refuge of the one who trusts the Lord. The verse is the record of the refusal and the drowning, and the teaching of the refuge that the mercy alone supplies.",
  "44": "“And it was said, “O earth! Swallow up your water. And O sky! Withhold ˹your rain˺.” The floodwater receded and the decree was carried out. The Ark rested on Mount Judi, and it was said, “Away with the wrongdoing people!”” is one of the Quran’s most majestic descriptions of the end of the flood, and the commentators note the address and the outcome. The verse records the divine command: “O earth! Swallow up your water. And O sky! Withhold your rain,” or “ya ardu ibla‘ee ma’aki wa ya sama’u aqli‘ee,” and the commentators note the address, for the Lord spoke to the earth and the sky, commanding the earth to swallow its water and the sky to withhold its rain, and the address is the display of the sovereignty, the command of the Creator to the creation, and the obedience of the earth and the sky is the witness of the power that the flood had shown. Ibn Kathir explains the scene: the earth absorbed the water and the sky ceased the rain, and the flood receded, and the verse records the receding: “The floodwater receded and the decree was carried out,” or “wa gheeda al-ma’u wa qudiya al-amru,” the lowering of the water and the completion of the decree, and then the resting: “The Ark rested on Mount Judi,” or “wastawat ‘ala al-Joodiyy,” the settling of the Ark upon the mountain of Judi, and the commentators identify the mountain, and the verse closes with the verdict: “and it was said, ‘Away with the wrongdoing people!’” or “wa qeela bu‘dan lil-qawmi az-zalimeen,” the pronouncement of the distance upon the wrongdoers, the declaration that the drowned were cast away, and the two commands and the two outcomes are the complete account of the end of the flood.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the sovereignty, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s trust. The believer is taught that the creation obeys the command of the Creator, and the earth that swallowed the water and the sky that withheld the rain are the witnesses of the power that the believer trusts, and the Ark that rested on the mountain is the symbol of the salvation that the trust attains, and the verdict upon the wrongdoers is the measure of the distance that the rejection earns. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the peace of the decree: the flood began by the command and ended by the command, and the Ark that the command saved rested by the command, and the believer who is carried by the command is the believer who rests where the command sets him, and the distance of the wrongdoers is the warning that the rejection invites. The verse is the record of the end of the flood, and the teaching of the sovereignty that the creation obeys.",
  "45": "“Noah called out to his Lord, saying, “My Lord! Certainly my son is ˹also˺ of my family, Your promise is surely true, and You are the most just of all judges!”” records the plea of the father for the drowned son, and the commentators note the three parts of the prayer. The first is the claim: “Certainly my son is also of my family,” or “inna ibnee min ahlee,” and the commentators explain the occasion, for the Lord had promised Noah the salvation of his family, and the son had drowned, and Noah, in his grief, invoked the promise, saying that the son was of his family, and the invocation was not the questioning of the decree but the seeking of the understanding, the plea of the father who wished to reconcile the promise with the loss. Ibn Kathir notes the tenderness of the plea, for the son was the flesh of the father, and the plea was the grief of the heart seeking the explanation. The second part is the affirmation: “Your promise is surely true,” or “wa inna wa‘daka al-haqqu,” and the commentators note the faith of Noah, for even in the plea he affirmed the truth of the promise, and the affirmation is the proof that the plea was not the doubt, but the seeking of the understanding within the faith. The third part is the praise: “and You are the most just of all judges!” or “wa anta ahkamu al-hakimeen,” and the commentators note the closing, for Noah appealed to the justice of the Lord, and the appeal is the confession that the judgment of the Lord is the most just, and the plea that the just judge might show the wisdom of the decree.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the plea, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own grief. The believer is taught that the grief of the loss is not the sin, and the plea of the heart for the understanding is not the doubt, and the one who asks the Lord about the wisdom of the decree while affirming the truth of the promise is the believer whose faith the grief has not shaken, and the answer of the Lord, which the next verse records, is the teaching that the plea sought. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the manner of the grief: Noah pleaded and affirmed and praised, and the three together are the complete posture of the believer in the loss, the seeking of the understanding, the holding of the faith, and the trust in the justice, and the believer who loses and pleads with the same posture is the believer whose grief the Lord answers. The verse is the record of the plea, and the teaching of the grief that the faith disciplines.",
  "46": "“Allah replied, “O Noah! He is certainly not of your family—he was entirely of unrighteous conduct. So do not ask Me about what you have no knowledge of! I warn you so you do not fall into ignorance.”” answers a father’s question by redefining family, and the commentators note the correction and its teaching. The first is the correction: “He is certainly not of your family—he was entirely of unrighteous conduct,” or “innahu laysa min ahlika innahu ‘amalun ghayru salihin,” and the commentators explain the meaning, for the Lord declared that the son was not of the family of Noah in the sense that the promise meant, for the family that the promise saved was the family of the faith, and the son whose conduct was unrighteous had severed himself from the family of the salvation, and the correction is the redefinition of the kinship, the teaching that the bond of the faith is the bond that the promise protects, and the bond of the blood is not the bond that the decree respects. Ibn Kathir notes the depth of the correction, for the son who shared the blood of Noah had placed himself outside the family by his conduct, and the verse teaches that the family of the prophet is the family of the faith, not the family of the flesh. The second part is the warning: “So do not ask Me about what you have no knowledge of! I warn you so you do not fall into ignorance,” or “fa-la tas’alni ma laysa laka bihi ‘ilmun inni a‘izuka an takoona mina al-jahileen,” and the commentators note the tenderness of the warning, for the Lord warned His prophet against the question that arose from the ignorance, and the warning was the teaching, the instruction that the plea had exceeded the knowledge, and the instruction is the mercy of the Lord who corrects His prophet with the gentleness of the teacher.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the kinship, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own ties. The believer is taught that the true kinship is the kinship of the faith, and the bond that saves is the bond of the righteousness, and the one who shares the blood but not the faith is the one whom the faith does not claim, and the believer who understands this will value the ties of the faith above the ties of the flesh, and will know that the family of the believer is the family of the believers. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the measure of the kinship: the son of Noah was not of his family because his conduct was unrighteous, and the believer who lives in the righteousness is the believer of the family that the Lord protects, and the warning against the question of the ignorance is the warning against the plea that the faith has not disciplined. The verse is the correction of the kinship, and the teaching of the family that the faith defines.",
  "47": "“Noah pleaded, “My Lord, I seek refuge in You from asking You about what I have no knowledge of, and unless You forgive me and have mercy on me, I will be one of the losers.”” is Noah’s immediate return, and the commentators note the two parts of the plea. The first is the refuge: “I seek refuge in You from asking You about what I have no knowledge of,” or “inni a‘oothu bika an as’alaka ma laysa lee bihi ‘ilmun,” and the commentators note the haste of the return, for Noah, upon the warning, turned at once to the refuge, and the seeking of the refuge from the question was the acknowledgment of the fault, the confession that the plea had exceeded the knowledge, and the haste of the return is the model of the believer who corrects the slip the moment the slip is shown. Ibn Kathir notes the contrast, for the son had refused the refuge and drowned, and the father sought the refuge and was answered, and the two refuges are the two ends of the story. The second part is the plea: “and unless You forgive me and have mercy on me, I will be one of the losers,” or “wa illa taghfir lee wa tarhamnee akun mina al-khasireen,” and the commentators note the humility, for Noah asked the forgiveness and the mercy and confessed that without them he would be of the losers, and the confession is the fear of the prophet, the fear that the slip, however small, would be the loss without the mercy, and the fear is the model of the believer who never trusts his own worth.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the return, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own correction. The believer is taught that the slip is corrected by the return, and the return is the refuge and the plea, and the one who seeks the refuge from the fault and asks the forgiveness and the mercy is the one whom the Lord receives, and the haste of Noah is the haste that the believer must make, for the delay of the return is the delay of the mercy. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the discipline of the correction: the warning came and the return followed, and the return was the refuge and the plea, and the believer who hears the correction of the Lord and returns with the haste of Noah is the believer whose slip the mercy erases. The verse is the record of the return, and the teaching of the haste that the correction demands.",
  "48": "“It was said, “O Noah! Disembark with Our peace and blessings on you and some of the descendants of those with you. As for the others, We will allow them ˹a brief˺ enjoyment, then they will be touched with a painful punishment from Us.”” opens the new chapter after the flood, and the commentators note the greeting and the decree. The first part is the greeting: “Disembark with Our peace and blessings on you,” or “ihbit bi-salamin minna wa barakatin ‘alayka,” and the commentators note the words, for the disembarking was accompanied by the “salam,” the peace, and the “barakah,” the blessing, and the two together are the divine greeting of the saved, the declaration that the flood was behind and the peace and the blessing were upon Noah and his company, and the greeting is the reward of the patience, the peace of the one whose trust the flood had justified. Ibn Kathir notes the beauty of the greeting, for the Lord addressed Noah with the peace and the blessing as he stepped from the Ark, and the address is the model of the divine reception of the faithful. The second part is the decree concerning the descendants: “and some of the descendants of those with you. As for the others, We will allow them a brief enjoyment, then they will be touched with a painful punishment from Us,” or “wa ‘ala umamin mimman ma‘aka wa umamun sa-numatti‘uhum thumma yamassuhum minna ‘athabun aleem,” and the commentators explain the meaning, for the descendants of the saved were of two kinds, the believing communities that would carry the blessing, and the others who would be granted the brief enjoyment of the world and then the painful punishment, and the decree is the declaration that the flood was not the end of the test but the beginning of the new test, the history that would unfold from the Ark.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the peace and the test, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own life. The believer is taught that the deliverance is followed by the peace and the blessing, and the peace and the blessing are followed by the new test, and the life of the believer is the alternation of the deliverance and the trial, and the one who receives the peace of the deliverance must prepare for the test that the new day brings, and the brief enjoyment of the heedless is the prelude of the punishment that the verse declares. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the completeness of the divine dealing: the Ark disembarked with the peace, and the earth was repopulated with the test, and the believer who steps from his own Ark of the deliverance steps into the new trial with the peace that the Lord has given. The verse is the record of the disembarking, and the teaching of the peace and the test that follow the deliverance.",
  "49": "“This is one of the stories of the unseen, which we reveal to you ˹O Prophet˺. Neither you nor your people knew it before this. So be patient! Surely the ultimate outcome belongs ˹only˺ to the righteous.”” steps out of the story to address the Prophet, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The first is the declaration of the source: “This is one of the stories of the unseen, which we reveal to you,” or “tilka min anba’i al-ghaybi nooheeha ilayka,” and the commentators explain the meaning, for the story of Noah was not a tale that the Prophet had learned from the books of the people but a revelation of the unseen, the “ghayb” that the Lord alone knew, and the revelation of the story is the proof of the prophethood, for the Prophet could not have known the details of the flood except by the revelation, and the verse declares the source, the unseen that the Lord disclosed. Ibn Kathir notes the confirmation: “Neither you nor your people knew it before this,” or “ma kunta ta‘lamuha anta wa la qawmuka min qabli hatha,” and the commentators explain, for the Arabs had no knowledge of the story of Noah, and the knowledge that the Quran brought was the witness of the revelation, the proof that the Prophet spoke from the Lord and not from the learning. The second part is the command: “So be patient! Surely the ultimate outcome belongs only to the righteous,” or “fasbir inna al-‘aqibata lil-muttaqeen,” and the commentators note the command, for the story of Noah was the comfort of the Prophet, the example of the messenger who was patient and prevailed, and the command to the patience is the application of the story, the assurance that the outcome, the “aqibah,” belongs to the righteous, the “muttaqeen,” and the patience of the Prophet is the waiting for the outcome that the story had promised.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the patience, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own endurance. The believer is taught that the stories of the unseen are the comfort of the patient, and the outcome of the struggle is the reward of the righteous, and the one who is patient in the face of the rejection is the one to whom the outcome belongs, and the patience that the story teaches is the patience that the believer must hold until the decree of the Lord is fulfilled. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the end of the patience: the story of Noah was the story of the patience and the outcome, and the believer who reads it and is patient is the believer whose outcome is the outcome of the righteous, and the command to the patience is the summary of the whole account. The verse is the closing of the story of Noah, and the teaching of the patience that the outcome rewards.",
  "50": "“And to the people of ’Âd We sent their brother Hûd. He said, “O my people! Worship Allah. You have no god other than Him. You do nothing but fabricate lies ˹against Allah˺.”” opens the second of this chapter’s prophetic histories, and the commentators note the kinship and the message. The verse introduces the people and the prophet: “And to the people of ‘Ad We sent their brother Hud,” or “wa ila ‘Adin akhahum Hoodan,” and the commentators explain the “their brother,” for Hud was one of the ‘Ad, a man of their own lineage and tongue, and the kinship of the messenger is the mercy of the message, the sending of the familiar to save the familiar, and the ‘Ad were the ancient Arab people of great strength and stature who dwelt in al-Ahqaf, the sand dunes of southern Arabia. Ibn Kathir notes the identity, for the ‘Ad were the successors of the people of Noah, and their story is the second instance of the pattern that the chapter traces. The verse then records the message: “O my people! Worship Allah. You have no god other than Him. You do nothing but fabricate lies against Allah,” or “ya qawmi i‘budoo Allaha ma lakum min ilahin ghayruhu in antum illa muftaroon,” and the commentators note the three parts: the command to the worship, the declaration of the oneness, and the verdict upon the people, the “muftaroon,” the fabricators, for the worship of the false gods is the fabrication of the lies against the Lord, and the verdict is the diagnosis of the state that the message had come to correct. Al-Sa’di observes that the message of Hud is the message of Noah, and the repetition of the message is the teaching of the unity of the truth, the same call to the One delivered by the successive messengers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the kinship, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own calling. The believer is taught that the caller to the truth is the caller to his own people, and the kinship of the caller is the advantage of the message, the familiarity that the truth uses, and the one who calls the nearest is the one who has discharged the first duty of the calling, for the love of the people begins with the warning of the people. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the beginning of the da‘wah: the messenger was sent to his brother, and the believer who calls his brother has followed the example of the prophets, and the fabricating of the lies is the state that the call must correct, the state of the heart that has invented the gods. The verse is the opening of the story of Hud, and the model of the call to the brother.",
  "51": "“O my people! I do not ask you for any reward for this ˹message˺. My reward is only from the One Who created me. Will you not then understand?”” repeats, from a different prophet, the disavowal of the reward, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The first is the disavowal: “I do not ask you for any reward for this message,” or “ya qawmi la as’alukum ‘alayhi ajran,” and the commentators note the refrain, for the disavowal of the reward is the declaration of every prophet, the assurance that the message is not the trade and the messenger is not the merchant, and the one who seeks the reward from the people is the one whose call the people can buy, while the one who seeks the reward from the Lord is the one whose call no bribe can silence. Ibn Kathir notes the repetition across the prophets, for Noah and Hud and Salih and Lot and Shu‘ayb all declared the same, and the repetition is the proof of the integrity of the messengers, the freedom from the accusation of the self-interest. The second part is the attribution: “My reward is only from the One Who created me. Will you not then understand?” or “in ajriya illa ‘ala allathee fataranee afala ta‘qiloon,” and the commentators note the beauty of the attribution, for Hud attributed the reward to the One who created him, the “fatarani,” the Originator, and the attribution is the reminder of the creation, the teaching that the one who created is the one who rewards, and the question “will you not then understand” is the appeal to the intellect, the summons to the reflection that the reminder should produce.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the integrity, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own sincerity. The believer is taught that the reward of the calling is the reward of the Creator, and the one who seeks the reward from the people has sold the calling to the people, and the integrity of the caller is the freedom that the disavowal declares, the freedom of the tongue that no wealth can buy and no poverty can silence. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the source of the sincerity: the reward that is sought from the Creator is the reward that the creation cannot give, and the one who understands this is the one who calls with the purity that the prophets modeled. The verse is the declaration of the integrity, and the teaching of the reward that the Creator alone gives.",
  "52": "“And O my people! Seek your Lord’s forgiveness and turn to Him in repentance. He will shower you with rain in abundance, and add strength to your strength. So do not turn away, persisting in wickedness.”” gives the same two commands with the promise attached, and the commentators note the command and the promise. The first part is the command: “Seek your Lord’s forgiveness and turn to Him in repentance,” or “wa ya qawmi istaghfiroo rabbakum thumma tooboo ilayhi,” and the commentators explain the two commands, the “istighfar,” the seeking of the forgiveness, and the “tawbah,” the repentance, the turning to the Lord, and the two together are the complete return, the asking of the pardon and the turning of the heart, and the commands are the remedy that Hud prescribed for the state of the ‘Ad, the cure of the fabrication that the previous verse had named. Ibn Kathir notes the order, for the seeking of the forgiveness precedes the repentance, and the two are the stages of the return. The second part is the promise: “He will shower you with rain in abundance, and add strength to your strength,” or “yursili as-sama’a ‘alaykum midraran wa yazidkum quwwatan ila quwwatikum,” and the commentators explain the promise, for the ‘Ad were a people of the strength and the agriculture, and the promise was the rain in the abundance and the strength upon the strength, the worldly reward of the repentance, and the commentators note the teaching, for the forgiveness of the Lord opens the gates of the provision, and the repentance is the key of the abundance. The verse closes with the warning: “So do not turn away, persisting in wickedness,” or “wa la tatawallaw mujrimeen,” and the commentators note the prohibition of the turning, for the turning away in the persistence of the wickedness is the refusal of the promise, and the warning is the caution against the path that the refusal opens.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the repentance and the provision, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own life. The believer is taught that the repentance is the key of the provision, and the seeking of the forgiveness opens the gates that the sin had closed, and the one who turns to the Lord with the repentance is the one whom the Lord showers with the abundance and the strength, and the turning away in the wickedness is the closing of the gates that the repentance had opened. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the link between the faith and the provision: the rain and the strength are the rewards of the forgiveness, and the believer who seeks the forgiveness with the sincerity is the believer whom the Lord provides, and the promise of Hud is the promise that the believer inherits. The verse is the command of the return, and the teaching of the provision that the repentance unlocks.",
  "53": "“They argued, “O Hûd! You have not given us any clear proof, and we will never abandon our gods upon your word, nor will we believe in you.”” is their first objection, and the commentators note the two claims and their falsehood. The first is the claim of the lack of the proof: “You have not given us any clear proof,” or “ma ji’tana bi-bayyinatin,” and the commentators explain the objection, for the ‘Ad demanded the clear proof, the “bayyinah,” the manifest sign, and the objection was the rejection of the message that Hud had brought, and the rejection was not the lack of the proof but the refusal of the proof, for the call to the worship of the One was itself the proof, and the proof of the reason was the proof that the arrogance refused. Ibn Kathir notes the irony, for the people who worshipped the idols of their own carving demanded the proof from the messenger of the truth, and the demand was the excuse that the refusal had found. The second is the declaration of the refusal: “we will never abandon our gods upon your word, nor will we believe in you,” or “ma nahnu bi-tarikee alihatina ‘an qawlika wa ma nahnu laka bi-mu’mineen,” and the commentators note the finality, for the people declared that they would not abandon the gods and would not believe, and the two refusals are the seal of the rejection, the declaration of the persistence that the warning had not broken, and the verse records the refusal as the prelude of the destruction that the story will trace.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the proof, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own witness. The believer is taught that the demand for the proof is often the excuse of the refusal, and the truth that is clear to the sincere is the truth that the arrogant calls obscure, and the one who demands the sign as the condition of the belief is the one who has already decided not to believe, and the believer who carries the truth must know that the proof is not the obstacle, and the obstacle is the will. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the nature of the refusal: the ‘Ad demanded the proof and then declared the persistence, and the two together revealed that the proof was never the question, and the believer who faces the same demand faces the same will, and the truth is not moved by the refusal of the will. The verse is the record of the objection, and the warning against the demand that the refusal hides.",
  "54": "““All we can say is that some of our gods have possessed you with evil.” He said, “I call Allah to witness, and you too bear witness, that I ˹totally˺ reject whatever you associate”” is the explanation they reached for, and the commentators note the accusation and the reply. The first part is the accusation: “All we can say is that some of our gods have possessed you with evil,” or “in naqoolu illa i‘taraka ba‘du alihatina bi-soo’,” and the commentators explain the meaning, for the ‘Ad, unable to answer the message, attributed the madness to the gods, the claim that some of their gods had struck Hud with the evil, the possession that had deranged his mind, and the attribution is the last refuge of the one who has no argument, the explanation of the truth as the madness, and the word “i‘taraka” — the striking, the possession — is the accusation that the falsehood hurls when the argument fails. Ibn Kathir notes the irony, for the gods that the ‘Ad claimed had possessed Hud were the idols that could not possess anything, and the accusation was the confession of the powerlessness of the very gods that they defended. The second part is the reply: “I call Allah to witness, and you too bear witness, that I totally reject whatever you associate,” or “qala inni ushhidu Allaha wash-hadoo annee baree’un mimma tushrikoon,” and the commentators note the boldness of the reply, for Hud called God to witness and called the people to witness that he was free of the association, and the calling of the witnesses was the declaration of the innocence, the public severing of the messenger from the falsehood, and the verse continues into the next, where the rejection is completed.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the accusation, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own trials. The believer is taught that the truth, when it cannot be refuted, is called the madness, and the accusation of the possession is the last word of the falsehood, and the believer who is accused of the madness is the believer whose truth the falsehood has confessed, and the answer to the accusation is the declaration of the innocence, the witness of the Lord and the severing of the association. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the boldness of the witness: Hud called the Lord to witness against the accusation, and the believer who is accused of the falsehood can call the same witness, and the declaration of the innocence is the shield of the truthful. The verse is the record of the accusation and the reply, and the teaching of the witness that the falsehood cannot refute.",
  "55": "““with Him ˹in worship˺. So let all of you plot against me without delay!”” completes the declaration begun in the previous verse, and the commentators note the two parts of the completion. The first is the completion of the severing: “with Him in worship,” or “min doonihi,” and the commentators explain the phrase, for Hud completed his declaration of the innocence by specifying that he rejected whatever the people associated with God besides Him, the “min doonihi,” the partners besides the Lord, and the completion is the full statement of the monotheism, the rejection of every partner and the affirmation of the One. Ibn Kathir notes the continuation, for the verse is the second half of the declaration of Hud, and the two halves together are the complete severing of the messenger from the falsehood. The second part is the challenge: “So let all of you plot against me without delay!” or “fa-keedoonee jamee‘an thumma la tunziroon,” and the commentators note the audacity of the challenge, for Hud invited the whole people and their gods to plot against him without the delay, and the invitation is the courage of the one who has entrusted his affair to the Lord, the same challenge that Noah had flung at his people, and the challenge is the declaration that the plots of the falsehood cannot touch the one whom the Lord protects, and the verse continues into the next, where the trust is explained.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the challenge, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own courage. The believer is taught that the severing of the falsehood is followed by the challenge, and the one who has declared his innocence of the association is the one who can invite the plots of the associators, and the courage of the challenge is the fruit of the trust, the confidence of the one who knows that the plots of the creation are the tools of the Creator. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the boldness of the faith: Hud severed the falsehood and challenged the plotters, and the believer who has severed the falsehood of his age can face its plots with the same boldness, for the trust that the challenge declares is the trust that the Lord honours. The verse is the completion of the challenge, and the teaching of the courage that the trust produces.",
  "56": "“I have put my trust in Allah—my Lord and your Lord. There is no living creature that is not completely under His control. Surely my Lord’s Way is perfect justice.”” explains the courage displayed in the challenge, and the commentators note the three parts of the verse. The first is the declaration of the trust: “I have put my trust in Allah—my Lord and your Lord,” or “innee tawakkaltu ‘ala Allahi rabbee wa rabbikum,” and the commentators note the phrase “my Lord and your Lord,” for Hud declared that the Lord in whom he trusted was the Lord of them all, the One who held both the messenger and the plotters, and the declaration is the ground of the courage, the trust that the challenge had displayed. Ibn Kathir explains the meaning, for the trust of the believer is the reliance upon the One who holds all, and the one who trusts the Lord of all does not fear the plot of any. The second part is the statement of the control: “There is no living creature that is not completely under His control,” or “ma min dabbatin illa huwa akhithun bi-nasiyatiha,” and the commentators explain the image, the “nasiyah,” the forelock, for every living creature is held by its forelock in the hand of the Lord, and the image is the declaration of the total control, the sovereignty that no creature escapes, and the one who is held by the Lord is the one whom the Lord protects. The third part is the verdict: “Surely my Lord’s Way is perfect justice,” or “inna rabbee ‘ala siratin mustaqeem,” and the commentators note the meaning, for the way of the Lord is the straight path, the “sirat mustaqeem,” the path of the perfect justice, and the declaration is the assurance that the outcome of the struggle is the justice, the vindication of the truthful and the ruin of the false.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the trust, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own reliance. The believer is taught that the trust in the Lord is the trust in the One who holds the forelocks of all, and the one who is held by the Lord is the one whom no plot can harm, and the way of the Lord is the justice that the believer awaits, the straight path that leads the truthful to the triumph and the false to the fall. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the foundation of the courage: the trust, the control, and the justice, and the three together are the complete ground of the confidence, the certainty that the Lord who holds all and judges justly is the Lord who protects the one who trusts. The verse is the explanation of the courage, and the teaching of the trust that the challenge displays.",
  "57": "“But if you turn away, I have already delivered to you what I have been sent with. My Lord will replace you with others. You are not harming Him in the least. Indeed, my Lord is a ˹vigilant˺ Keeper over all things.”” is Hud closing his case, and the commentators note the three declarations that the verse joins. The first is the discharge of the duty: “if you turn away, I have already delivered to you what I have been sent with,” or “fa-in tawallaw fa-qad ablaghtukum ma ursiltu bihi ilaykum,” and the commentators note the declaration, for the messenger who has delivered the message is the messenger whose account is clear, and the turning away of the people does not undo the delivery, and the declaration is the peace of the one who has done his duty. Ibn Kathir explains the meaning, for the prophet is not responsible for the belief of the people but for the delivery of the message, and the delivery of Hud was complete, and the turning away was the choice of the people. The second is the warning: “My Lord will replace you with others. You are not harming Him in the least,” or “wa yastakhlifu rabbee qawman ghayrakum wa la tadurroonahu shay’an,” and the commentators note the warning, for the people who turned away would be replaced, and the replacement is the declaration that the cause of the truth does not depend upon the rejecters, and the harm that they imagined they were doing was no harm at all, for the Lord is beyond the harm of the creatures. The third is the praise: “Indeed, my Lord is a vigilant Keeper over all things,” or “inna rabbee ‘ala kulli shay’in hafeez,” and the commentators note the closing, for the Lord is the “hafeez,” the Keeper, the Guardian, the vigilant over all, and the closing is the assurance that the delivered message and the rejected people are both in the keeping of the One who guards all.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the discharge, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own duty. The believer is taught that the duty is the delivery, and the outcome is the Lord’s, and the one who has delivered the message is the one whose account is clear, and the turning away of the people is the choice that the Lord will judge, and the replacement of the rejecters is the warning that the truth does not need them, and the believer who has delivered with the sincerity of Hud is the believer whose peace is the peace of the discharged. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the limit of the responsibility: the messenger delivers and the Lord keeps, and the believer who has delivered his trust and left the keeping to the Lord is the believer whose account the Lord accepts. The verse is the closing of the case, and the teaching of the delivery that the turning cannot undo.",
  "58": "“When Our command came, We rescued Hûd and those who believed with him by a mercy from Us, saving them from a harsh torment.” moves from the speech to the outcome, and the commentators note the rescue and its description. The verse states the rescue: “When Our command came, We rescued Hud and those who believed with him by a mercy from Us,” or “wa lamma ja’a amruna najjayna Hoodan wallatheena amanoo ma‘ahu bi-rahmatin minna,” and the commentators note the pattern, for the rescue of the messenger and the believers is the constant outcome of the prophetic stories, and the rescue is “by a mercy from Us,” the “bi-rahmatin minna,” the mercy that the Lord grants to the faithful, and the phrase teaches that the salvation is not the reward of the strength but the gift of the mercy. Ibn Kathir explains the detail: the command of the destruction came upon the ‘Ad, and Hud and the believers were saved, and the salvation was the mercy that the verse names. The verse closes with the description of the torment: “saving them from a harsh torment,” or “wa najjaynahum min ‘athabin ghaleez,” and the commentators note the word “ghaleez,” the harsh, the thick, the severe, for the torment of the ‘Ad was the wind that the following verses describe, the “athab ghaleez” that the mercy saved the believers from, and the description of the torment is the measure of the mercy, the rescue from the severity that the falsehood had earned.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the rescue, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own hope. The believer is taught that the mercy of the Lord rescues the faithful from the torment of the falsehood, and the rescue of Hud and his few is the promise of the rescue of the believers in every age, and the harshness of the torment that the mercy saves from is the measure of the gratitude that the saved owe. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the constancy of the divine rescue: the command came, and the believers were saved, and the salvation is the mercy that the Lord never withholds from the faithful, and the believer who stands with the truth stands with the mercy that saves. The verse is the record of the rescue, and the teaching of the mercy that the faithful inherit.",
  "59": "“That was ’Âd. They denied the signs of their Lord, disobeyed His messengers, and followed the command of every stubborn tyrant.”” begins a summary of the case against them, and the commentators note the three charges that the verse lists. The first is the denial: “They denied the signs of their Lord,” or “jaḥadoo bi-ayati rabbihim,” and the commentators note the word “jahadoo,” the denial, the rejection with the knowledge, for the ‘Ad had not been ignorant of the signs but had denied them despite the knowledge, and the denial with the knowledge is the gravest form of the rejection. Ibn Kathir explains the meaning, for the “juhud” is the denial of the known, the refusal of the truth that the heart has recognized, and the verse names it as the first charge. The second is the disobedience: “disobeyed His messengers,” or “wa ‘asaw rusulahu,” and the commentators note the plural, for the rejection of Hud was the rejection of the message that all the messengers had brought, and the disobedience of the one was the disobedience of the line, and the plural is the teaching that the message is one and the rejection is one. The third is the following: “and followed the command of every stubborn tyrant,” or “wattaba‘oo amra kulli jabbarin ‘aneed,” and the commentators note the charge, for the ‘Ad followed the “jabbar ‘aneed,” the stubborn tyrant, the arrogant oppressor, and the following of the tyrant is the corruption that the denial and the disobedience had produced, the surrender of the people to the command of the arrogant instead of the command of the Lord, and the three charges together are the complete case against the ‘Ad.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the three charges, and the commentators connect them to the believer’s own warning. The believer is taught that the rejection of the truth is the denial of the known, the disobedience of the messengers, and the following of the tyrants, and the three are the stages of the fall, the denial that opens the door, the disobedience that walks through it, and the following of the tyrant that completes the fall, and the believer who avoids the three is the believer who stands with the signs, the messengers, and the Lord. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the anatomy of the rejection: the ‘Ad denied and disobeyed and followed, and the believer who sees the three in the history sees the warning against the three in the life, and the following of the stubborn tyrant is the end that the denial and the disobedience prepare. The verse is the summary of the case, and the teaching of the three charges that the rejection compounds.",
  "60": "“They were followed by a curse in this world, as they will be on the Day of Judgment. Surely ’Âd denied their Lord, so away with ’Âd, the people of Hûd!”” describes the two-fold curse, and the commentators note the two worlds and the verdict. The verse states the curse: “They were followed by a curse in this world, as they will be on the Day of Judgment,” or “wa utbi‘oo fee hathihi ad-dunya la‘natan wa yawma al-qiyamah,” and the commentators explain the meaning, for the “la‘nah,” the curse, the expulsion from the mercy, followed the ‘Ad in this world and will follow them on the Day of Judgment, and the two curses are the two installments of the recompense, the worldly curse of the destruction and the remembrance of the shame, and the eternal curse of the Fire, and the verse teaches that the rejection of the truth is the rejection that the curse pursues in both worlds. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning of the curse, for the “la‘nah” is the distance from the mercy, and the ‘Ad were distanced in the world and will be distanced in the next. The verse then states the verdict: “Surely ‘Ad denied their Lord, so away with ‘Ad, the people of Hud!” or “ala inna ‘Adan kafaroo rabbahum ala bu‘dan li-‘Adin qawmi Hood,” and the commentators note the word “bu‘dan,” the away, the distance, and the identification of the ‘Ad as the people of Hud, for the people of the messenger who rejected him are the people whose name is joined to the rejection, and the verdict “away with ‘Ad” is the pronouncement of the distance, the seal of the curse that the denial earned.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the curse, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own fear. The believer is taught that the rejection of the Lord is the rejection that the curse follows, and the curse of the world and the Day is the recompense of the denial, and the one who would be free of the curse is the one who holds to the faith, and the verdict “away with ‘Ad” is the warning that the distance from the mercy is the end of the distance from the Lord. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the permanence of the recompense: the curse of the world is the first installment, and the curse of the Day is the completion, and the believer who fears the two curses is the believer who clings to the mercy that the rejection forfeits. The verse is the verdict upon the ‘Ad, and the teaching of the curse that the denial invites.",
  "61": "“And to the people of Thamûd We sent their brother Ṣâliḥ. He said, “O my people! Worship Allah. You have no god other than Him. He ˹is the One Who˺ produced you from the earth and settled you in it. So seek His forgiveness and turn to Him in repentance. Surely my Lord is Ever Near, All-Responsive.”” introduces the third community in this chapter, and the commentators note the message and the argument. The verse introduces the people and the prophet: “And to the people of Thamud We sent their brother Salih,” or “wa ila Thamooda akhahum Salihan,” and the commentators explain the identity, for the Thamud were the successors of the ‘Ad, the Arab people who dwelt in al-Hijr, and Salih was their brother, the man of their own lineage, and the message that he brought was the same message: “O my people! Worship Allah. You have no god other than Him,” or “ya qawmi i‘budoo Allaha ma lakum min ilahin ghayruhu,” the call to the worship of the One. Ibn Kathir notes the continuity, for the message of Salih is the message of Hud and Noah, and the repetition is the teaching of the unity of the truth. The verse then adds the argument of the creation: “He is the One Who produced you from the earth and settled you in it,” or “huwa ansha’akum mina al-ardi wasta‘marakum feeha,” and the commentators explain the meaning, for the Lord created the Thamud from the earth and settled them in it, the “ista‘marakum,” the granting of the life and the dwelling, and the argument is the appeal to the creation, the reminder that the One who created and settled is the One who deserves the worship. The verse closes with the command and the praise: “So seek His forgiveness and turn to Him in repentance. Surely my Lord is Ever Near, All-Responsive,” or “fastaghfiroohu thumma tooboo ilayhi inna rabbee qareebun mujeeb,” and the commentators note the two names, the “qareeb,” the Near, and the “mujeeb,” the Responsive, for the Lord who is near and answers is the Lord whose forgiveness the repentance seeks.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the creation, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own gratitude. The believer is taught that the argument of the creation is the foundation of the worship, and the One who created the believer from the earth and settled him in the land is the One whose worship the believer owes, and the remembrance of the creation is the remedy of the ingratitude, and the nearness and the responsiveness of the Lord are the comfort of the one who turns in the repentance. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the nearness of the Lord: Salih called his people to the repentance with the assurance of the nearness, and the believer who knows that his Lord is near and responsive is the believer whose repentance is the joy rather than the burden. The verse is the opening of the story of Salih, and the teaching of the nearness that the repentance seeks.",
  "62": "“They argued, “O Ṣâliḥ! We truly had high hopes in you before this.”” is an objection with a note of disappointment, and the commentators note what the objection reveals about Salih’s standing. The verse records the reply of the people: “O Salih! We truly had high hopes in you before this,” or “qaloo ya Salihu qad kunta feenaa marjuwwan qabla hatha,” and the commentators explain the meaning, for the Thamud had hoped in Salih, and the hope had been the expectation that he would become one of their leaders, one of the honoured of the tribe, and the disappointment of the hope is the confession of the standing, for Salih had been the man whom the people had expected to rise, and his call to the worship of the One had disappointed the expectation, and the objection is the reproach of the disappointed, the complaint that the prophet had not fulfilled the hope. Ibn Kathir notes the significance, for the objection reveals that Salih had been held in the esteem of his people, and the esteem is the measure of the disappointment, and the disappointment is the measure of the rejection, for the people who had hoped in the man refused the message. The verse continues in the next, where the objection is completed with the accusation of the prohibition of the worship of the fathers, and the commentators note that the two parts together are the full objection of the Thamud.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the disappointment, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own experience. The believer is taught that the truth often disappoints the hopes that the people had placed in the bearer, and the hope that the truth disappoints is the hope of the world, the expectation that the bearer would serve the ambition of the people, and the disappointment is the cost of the truth, the price that the messenger pays for the fidelity to the message. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the reality of the calling: the people had hoped in Salih, and the hope was the world’s expectation, and the truth that he brought shattered the expectation, and the believer who carries the truth must be ready to disappoint the hopes that the falsehood had invested in him. The verse is the record of the objection, and the teaching of the disappointment that the truth brings.",
  "63": "“He responded, “O my people! Consider if I stand on a clear proof from my Lord”” is Salih answering in the same pattern, and the commentators note the method of the reply. Salih answered the reproach of the disappointment with the same invitation that Noah had made: “Consider if I stand on a clear proof from my Lord,” or “qala ya qawmi a-ra’aytum in kuntu ‘ala bayyinatin min rabbee,” and the commentators explain the form, for the “a-ra’aytum” is the invitation to the consideration, the asking of the people to suppose, for the sake of the argument, that the messenger stood upon the clear proof, and the supposition is the method of the prophets, the leading of the people to the truth through their own reflection. Ibn Kathir notes the continuity, for the reply of Salih is the reply of Noah, and the repetition of the method is the teaching of the constancy of the prophetic argument, the same question that the truth asks of every age. The verse continues in the next, where the reply is completed with the mercy and the question of the protection, and the commentators note that the two parts together are the full answer of Salih, the appeal to the proof and the question of the accountability, and the method is the method that the falsehood cannot answer.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the method, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own calling. The believer is taught that the answer to the reproach is the question, and the question “consider if” is the method of the truth, the invitation to the reflection that the anger cannot make, and the one who calls to the truth with the question has called with the mercy, for the question leaves the door open that the accusation would close. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the patience of the reply: Salih answered the disappointment with the question, and the question is the gentleness of the truth, and the believer who faces the reproach of the disappointed must answer with the same gentleness, the question that invites rather than the anger that drives. The verse is the opening of the reply, and the teaching of the question that the truth asks.",
  "64": "“And O my people! This she-camel of Allah is a sign for you. So leave her to graze ˹freely˺ on Allah’s earth and do her no harm, or a swift punishment will overtake you!”” introduces the miracle given to Thamud, and the commentators describe the she-camel and the command. The verse records the sign: “This she-camel of Allah is a sign for you,” or “wa hathihi naqatu Allahi lakum ayah,” and the commentators explain the miracle, for the Thamud had demanded a sign from Salih, and God brought forth the she-camel from the rock, and the camel was the “naqat Allah,” the she-camel of God, the sign that the people had challenged, and the commentators describe her origin: she emerged from the rock that the people had designated, a great she-camel of the miracle, and her emergence was the proof of the message. Ibn Kathir relates the detail: the people had asked Salih to bring forth a camel from the rock, and the camel came forth, and the sign was the answer of the challenge. The verse then records the command: “So leave her to graze freely on Allah’s earth and do her no harm, or a swift punishment will overtake you!” or “fatharooha ta’kul fee ardi Allahi wa la tamassooha bi-soo’in fa-ya’khuthakum ‘athabun qareeb,” and the commentators note the two parts of the command, the leaving of the camel to graze, and the prohibition of the harm, and the warning of the swift punishment, and the command is the test, for the people who honoured the sign of God would be the saved, and the people who harmed it would be the destroyed, and the warning of the “athab qareeb,” the near punishment, is the measure of the severity of the harm.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the sign, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own obedience. The believer is taught that the sign of God is the test of the obedience, and the honouring of the sign is the obedience that saves, and the harm of the sign is the rebellion that destroys, and the swift punishment is the warning that the harm invites, and the believer who honours the signs of the Lord is the believer whom the Lord protects, and the one who harms them is the one whom the punishment overtakes. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the weight of the sign: the she-camel was the test of the Thamud, and the harm of the camel was the harm of the test, and the punishment that followed was the reward of the harm, and the believer who honours the signs of his age is the believer whose obedience the Lord rewards. The verse is the introduction of the sign, and the teaching of the obedience that the sign demands.",
  "65": "“But they killed her, so he warned ˹them˺, “You have ˹only˺ three ˹more˺ days to enjoy life in your homes— this is an unfailing promise!”” reports the outcome of the test in three words, and the commentators note the crime and the warning. The verse states the crime with the Quran’s characteristic brevity: “But they killed her,” or “fa-‘aqarooha,” and the commentators note the brevity, for the Quran gives no drama to the killing but states it in the single word, and the brevity is the gravity, the statement of the deed that the whole story had been building toward, the killing of the sign of God. Ibn Kathir relates the fuller account: the she-camel was hamstrung and killed by the most wretched of the Thamud, the man named Qudar ibn Salif and his accomplices, and the killing was the consent of the people, the act of the community that the individual performed, and the verse attributes the deed to the people as a whole, for the silence of the many was the participation of the many. The verse then records the warning of Salih: “You have only three more days to enjoy life in your homes—this is an unfailing promise!” or “qala tamatta‘oo fee darikum thalathata ayyamin thalika wa‘dun ghayru makthoob,” and the commentators note the warning, for Salih declared that the people had three days of the enjoyment before the punishment, and the three days were the final mercy, the last span in which the repentance was possible, and the promise was the “wa‘d ghayr makthoob,” the promise that cannot be denied, and the warning is the last word of the prophet before the destruction.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the brevity, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own urgency. The believer is taught that the sin is stated and the punishment is promised, and the three days are the mercy of the delay, the span that the repentance could still use, and the one who kills the sign of God is the one whom the warning finds, and the believer who hears the warning of his own age must use the span that the mercy grants, for the promise of the punishment is the promise that cannot be denied. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the urgency of the return: the three days were the last mercy, and the believer who is given the span must return within it, for the promise of the punishment is not the threat but the truth. The verse is the record of the crime and the warning, and the teaching of the span that the mercy grants.",
  "66": "“When Our command came, We saved Ṣâliḥ and those who believed with him by a mercy from Us and spared them the disgrace of that Day. Surely your Lord ˹alone˺ is the All-Powerful, Almighty.”” repeats the rescue pattern, and the commentators note the salvation and the sparing. The verse states the rescue: “When Our command came, We saved Salih and those who believed with him by a mercy from Us,” or “wa lamma ja’a amruna najjayna Salihan wallatheena amanoo ma‘ahu bi-rahmatin minna,” and the commentators note the pattern, for the rescue of Salih and the believers is the same rescue that Noah and Hud received, the salvation by the mercy, and the constancy of the pattern is the teaching of the constancy of the divine dealing. Ibn Kathir notes the phrase “by a mercy from Us,” for the salvation is the gift of the mercy, and the believer who is saved is the one whom the mercy reached. The verse then adds the sparing: “and spared them the disgrace of that Day,” or “wa min khizyi yawmi’ithin,” and the commentators explain the “khizy,” the disgrace, for the punishment of the Thamud was the disgrace, the humiliation of the blast that the following verse describes, and the believers were spared the disgrace, and the sparing is the mercy of the salvation, the deliverance from the shame that the falsehood earned. The verse closes with the praise: “Surely your Lord alone is the All-Powerful, Almighty,” or “inna rabbaka huwa al-qawiyyu al-‘azeez,” and the commentators note the two names, the “qawiyy,” the Strong, and the “‘azeez,” the Almighty, for the power that rescued the believers and destroyed the wrongdoers is the power that the verse praises, and the praise is the assurance that the Lord of the rescue is the Lord of the strength.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the rescue, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own hope. The believer is taught that the mercy of the Lord rescues the faithful from the disgrace of the falsehood, and the sparing of the believers is the mercy that the Lord grants to the ones who held to the truth, and the power that the verse praises is the power that the believer trusts, the strength that the falsehood cannot withstand. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the constancy of the deliverance: the command came, and the believers were spared, and the Lord who is the Strong and the Almighty is the Lord who delivers the faithful in every age. The verse is the record of the rescue, and the teaching of the mercy that spares the disgrace.",
  "67": "“And the ˹mighty˺ blast overtook the wrongdoers, so they fell lifeless in their homes,” describes the instrument of Thamud’s destruction, and the commentators note the blast and the fall. The verse states the punishment: “And the mighty blast overtook the wrongdoers,” or “wa akhatha allatheena zalamoo as-sayhatu,” and the commentators explain the “sayhah,” the blast, the cry, the thunderous sound, and the commentators note that the punishment of the Thamud was the blast, the sound from the sky that struck them, and the fuller accounts combine it with the earthquake, the “rajfah,” so that the Thamud were seized from above and below, and the blast is the instrument of the destruction that the verse names. Ibn Kathir relates the detail: the blast came upon the Thamud, and the earthquake followed, and the people fell, and the verse records the fall: “so they fell lifeless in their homes,” or “fa-asbahoo fee darihim jathimeen,” and the commentators note the word “jathimeen,” the fallen, the kneeling, the still, for the people fell in their homes, struck down in the very dwellings that they had carved from the rock, and the image is the irony that the Quran preserves, the people who trusted their carved homes perishing within them, and the homes that had been their pride becoming their graves.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the blast, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own fear. The believer is taught that the punishment of the falsehood comes with the suddenness of the blast, and the ones who trust their homes and their strength are the ones whom the blast finds in the morning, and the believer who lives in the fear of the Lord is the believer who is spared the blast, and the lifeless bodies in the homes are the warning of the end that the heedlessness invites. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the suddenness of the judgment: the blast came and the people fell, and the homes that they had trusted were the place of their fall, and the believer who sees the scene in the verse is the believer who prepares for the judgment that the verse foretells. The verse is the record of the blast, and the teaching of the suddenness that the heedless forget.",
  "68": "“as if they had never lived there. Surely Thamûd denied their Lord, so away with Thamûd!”” is one of the most haunting phrases in the Quran, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The first is the erasure: “as if they had never lived there,” or “ka-an lam yaghnaw feeha,” and the commentators explain the meaning, for the destruction of the Thamud was so complete that it was as if they had never dwelt in the land, and the phrase is the declaration of the totality of the ruin, the erasure of the people and their memory, and the commentators note the force of the image, for the carved homes of the Thamud remained as the empty shells, and the people were gone, and the land was as if it had never known them. Ibn Kathir notes the haunting of the phrase, for the “ka-an lam yaghnaw” is the Quran’s way of teaching the vanity of the worldly permanence, the truth that the people who built for the ages were removed as if they had never been. The second part is the verdict: “Surely Thamud denied their Lord, so away with Thamud!” or “ala inna Thamooda kafaroo rabbahum ala bu‘dan li-Thamood,” and the commentators note the parallel with the verdict upon the ‘Ad, for the “away with Thamud” is the same “bu‘d” that the ‘Ad received, and the parallel is the teaching that the denial of the Lord is the sin whose verdict is the same in every age, the distance from the mercy, and the verse closes the account of the Thamud with the verdict that the denial earned.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the erasure, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own perspective. The believer is taught that the permanence of the world is the illusion, and the people who built the homes and trusted the strength were removed as if they had never lived, and the believer who builds for the Hereafter is the believer whose work the erasure cannot touch, and the verdict “away with Thamud” is the warning that the denial of the Lord is the erasure that the world and the Hereafter alike confirm. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the vanity of the worldly: the Thamud carved the mountains and were erased, and the believer who carves the works of the Hereafter is the believer whose work remains, and the erasure of the deniers is the measure of the permanence of the truth. The verse is the verdict upon the Thamud, and the teaching of the erasure that the denial earns.",
  "69": "“And surely Our messenger-angels came to Abraham with good news ˹of a son˺. They greeted ˹him with˺, “Peace!” And he replied, “Peace ˹be upon you˺!” Then it was not long before he brought ˹them˺ a ˹fat,˺ roasted calf.”” opens a new episode, and the commentators describe the guests and the hospitality. The verse introduces the scene: “And surely Our messenger-angels came to Abraham with good news of a son,” or “wa laqad ja’at rusuluna Ibraheema bil-bushra,” and the commentators explain the mission, for the angels came to Abraham with the good news of the birth of a son, and the same angels were the ones who would proceed to the people of Lot, and the verse is the opening of the account of the guests. Ibn Kathir notes the identity of the guests, for they were the angels in the form of the men, and the greeting that the verse records: “They greeted him with, ‘Peace!’ And he replied, ‘Peace be upon you!’” or “qaloo salaman qala salamun,” and the commentators note the exchange, for the guests offered the peace and Abraham returned the better greeting, the “salam” answered by the fuller “salam,” and the exchange is the model of the greeting of the believer, and the verse then records the hospitality: “Then it was not long before he brought them a fat, roasted calf,” or “fa-ma labitha an ja’a bi-‘ijlin haneeth,” and the commentators note the haste and the generosity, for Abraham did not delay but brought the roasted calf, the “ijil haneeth,” the fat calf that was the best of his provision, and the hospitality is the model of the host, the haste and the generosity that the verse records, and the commentators connect the verse to the sunnah of the hospitality, the honouring of the guest with the best and the prompt.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the hospitality, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own conduct. The believer is taught that the honouring of the guest is the honouring of the Lord, and the haste and the generosity of Abraham are the model of the host, and the greeting of the peace is the greeting that the believer returns with the better, and the one who honours the guest with the best of the provision is the one whom the Lord honours with the best of the reward. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the etiquette of the guest: the peace spoken, the calf brought, and the generosity shown, and the believer who follows the example of Abraham is the believer whose hospitality the Lord records. The verse is the opening of the account of the guests, and the teaching of the hospitality that the believer models.",
  "70": "“And when he saw that their hands did not reach for the food, he became suspicious and fearful of them. They reassured ˹him˺, “Do not be afraid! We are ˹angels˺ sent ˹only˺ against the people of Lot.”” records the suspicion and the reassurance, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The first is the suspicion: “when he saw that their hands did not reach for the food, he became suspicious and fearful of them,” or “fa-lamma ra’a aydiyahum la tasilu ilayhi nakirahum wa awjasa minhum khifah,” and the commentators explain the scene, for the angels did not eat, and the refusal of the food was the sign that the guests were not the ordinary men, and the sign alarmed Abraham, for the custom of the land was that the refusal of the food was the sign of the evil intent, and the suspicion and the fear are the reaction of the host who saw the sign and did not understand it. Ibn Kathir notes the detail, for the angels did not eat because they were the angels, and the refusal of the food was the sign that alarmed the host, and the alarm is the human reaction that the verse records. The second part is the reassurance: “Do not be afraid! We are angels sent only against the people of Lot,” or “qaloo la takhaf inna ursilna ila qawmi Loot,” and the commentators note the reassurance, for the angels calmed the fear of Abraham and declared their mission, the sending against the people of Lot, and the declaration is the first mention of the mission, the prelude of the account of the destruction that the following verses record.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the alarm and the calm, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own trust. The believer is taught that the signs of the unseen sometimes alarm the heart, and the alarm is the human reaction, and the reassurance of the Lord is the calm that follows, and the one who trusts the Lord is the one whose alarm the reassurance calms, and the fear of the host is the fear that the declaration of the angels dissolved. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the comfort of the reassurance: Abraham feared and was reassured, and the believer who fears the unknown is the believer whom the Lord reassures, and the declaration “do not be afraid” is the word that the believer hears in every trial. The verse is the record of the suspicion and the reassurance, and the teaching of the calm that the Lord sends.",
  "71": "“And his wife was standing by, so she laughed, then We gave her good news of ˹the birth of˺ Isaac, and, after him, Jacob.”” brings Sarah into the scene, and the commentators note the laugh and the good news. The verse describes the presence of the wife: “And his wife was standing by, so she laughed,” or “wamra’atuhu qa’imatun fa-dahikat,” and the commentators explain the laugh, and the reports differ on its meaning: some say that Sarah laughed in the delight of the relief, the joy that the fear of Abraham had been removed and the guests were the angels of the mercy, and others say that she laughed in the astonishment at the good news that the verse records, and the difference is the richness of the scene, for the laugh is the human reaction of the woman who heard the news of the son in the old age, and the verse preserves the laugh as the touch of the humanity that the Quran never loses. Ibn Kathir relates both meanings and notes that the good news that follows gives the context of the laugh. The verse then records the good news: “then We gave her good news of the birth of Isaac, and, after him, Jacob,” or “fa-bashsharnaha bi-Ishaqa wa min wara’i Ishaqa Ya‘qoob,” and the commentators note the two parts of the news, the birth of Isaac and the birth of Jacob after him, and the double news is the mercy of the Lord, the announcement that the barren old woman would bear the son and the grandson, and the news is the fulfillment of the promise that the angels had brought, and the laugh of Sarah is the prelude of the astonishment that the next verse records.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the good news, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own hope. The believer is taught that the mercy of the Lord is not bound by the laws of the age, and the barren and the old are the vessels of the promise when the Lord wills, and the good news of Isaac and Jacob is the proof that the power of the Lord overcomes the impossibility, and the believer who despairs of the mercy has forgotten the news that the angels brought. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the scope of the divine power: Sarah laughed at the news of the son in the old age, and the news was the truth, and the believer who hears the promise of the Lord must not measure it by the limits of the creation. The verse is the record of the good news, and the teaching of the mercy that the impossibility cannot bind.",
  "72": "“She wondered, “Oh, my! How can I have a child in this old age, and my husband here is an old man? This is truly an astonishing thing!”” records the astonishment of Sarah, and the commentators note the two parts of her wonder. The first is the exclamation: “Oh, my!” or “ya waylata,” and the commentators explain the word, for the “waylata” is the exclamation of the astonishment, the cry of the woman who heard the impossible, and the exclamation is the human reaction of the one whose mind cannot grasp the news. Ibn Kathir notes the context, for Sarah was old and barren, and the news of the son was the news that the laws of the age contradicted, and the exclamation is the confession of the astonishment. The second part is the question: “How can I have a child in this old age, and my husband here is an old man? This is truly an astonishing thing!” or “a-alidu wa ana ‘ajoozun wa hatha ba‘lee shaykhan inna hatha la-shay’un ‘ajeeb,” and the commentators note the two facts that Sarah cited, her own old age and the old age of her husband, and the citation of the two is the human reasoning, the measurement of the promise by the limits of the nature, and the verdict “this is truly an astonishing thing” is the confession of the wonder, the acknowledgment that the news exceeded the ordinary, and the verse records the wonder as the human face of the faith that the next verse will complete with the correction of the angels.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the wonder, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own astonishment. The believer is taught that the promise of the Lord is often the news that the reason finds astonishing, and the astonishment is the human reaction, and the correction of the astonishment, which the next verse records, is the teaching that the decree of the Lord is not measured by the limits of the reason, and the believer who wonders at the promise must hear the answer of the angels, the reminder that the mercy of the Lord is the astonishing thing that the reason cannot bind. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the limit of the reason: Sarah reasoned from the age and the barrenness, and the reason was the human measure, and the promise of the Lord was the divine reality, and the believer who hears the promise must not confine it to the measure of the reason. The verse is the record of the wonder, and the teaching of the astonishment that the decree exceeds.",
  "73": "“They responded, “Are you astonished by Allah’s decree? May Allah’s mercy and blessings be upon you, O people of this house. Indeed, He is Praiseworthy, All-Glorious.”” is the angels’ gentle correction of Sarah’s wonder, and the commentators note the two parts of the reply. The first is the question: “Are you astonished by Allah’s decree?” or “a-ta‘jabeena min amri Allahi,” and the commentators explain the meaning, for the angels corrected the astonishment of Sarah, and the correction is the teaching that the decree of the Lord is not the thing to be wondered at, and the wonder belongs to the one who measures the decree by the limits of the creation, and the question is the gentle rebuke, the reminder that the mercy of the Lord is the reality that the reason cannot confine. Ibn Kathir notes the tenderness of the question, for the angels did not rebuke the faith of Sarah but corrected the astonishment, and the correction is the teaching of the scope of the divine power, the declaration that the decree of the Lord is the astonishment that the wonder must yield to. The second part is the greeting: “May Allah’s mercy and blessings be upon you, O people of this house. Indeed, He is Praiseworthy, All-Glorious,” or “rahmatu Allahi wa barakatuhu ‘alaykum ahla al-bayti innahu hameedun majeed,” and the commentators note the greeting, for the angels invoked the mercy and the blessings upon the household of Abraham, and the invocation is the honour of the house, the “ahl al-bayt,” the people of the house, and the closing praise, the “hameed,” the Praiseworthy, and the “majeed,” the Glorious, is the praise of the Lord whose mercy and blessings the angels invoked, and the verse is the complete correction, the question that removes the astonishment and the greeting that confers the honour.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the correction, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own wonder. The believer is taught that the astonishment at the decree is the measure of the human, and the correction of the astonishment is the teaching of the divine, and the believer who hears the promise of the Lord and wonders must hear the question of the angels, the reminder that the decree is not the wonder but the reality, and the greeting of the mercy and the blessings is the honour that the household of the faith inherits. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the scope of the mercy: the angels corrected the wonder and invoked the blessings, and the believer who is of the household of the faith is the believer whom the mercy and the blessings of the Lord encompass. The verse is the correction of the wonder, and the teaching of the mercy that the decree carries.",
  "74": "“Then after the fear had left Abraham, and the good news had reached him, he began to plead with Us for the people of Lot.”” records the turn of the scene from the good news to the plea, and the commentators note the two states and the transition. The verse describes the two states: “after the fear had left Abraham, and the good news had reached him,” or “fa-lamma thahaba ‘an Ibraheema ar-raw‘u wa ja’athu al-bushra,” and the commentators explain the sequence, for the fear of the guests had left Abraham and the good news of the son had reached him, and the two states — the departure of the fear and the arrival of the news — are the preparation of the plea, for the heart that is at peace and the hope that is confirmed are the conditions of the intercession that follows. Ibn Kathir notes the sequence, for the fear had been the suspicion of the host, and the news had been the promise of the son, and the two together opened the door of the plea, and the verse records the transition. The verse then states the plea: “he began to plead with Us for the people of Lot,” or “yujadiluna fee qawmi Loot,” and the commentators explain the “mujadalah,” the pleading, the arguing, for Abraham began to plead with the angels for the people of Lot, and the pleading is the intercession of the friend of God for the doomed, the attempt of the merciful heart to delay or to avert the punishment, and the commentators note the significance, for the plea of Abraham is the proof of his “hilm,” the forbearance that the next verse names, the tenderness that could not accept the destruction of a people without the attempt to save them.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the plea, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own compassion. The believer is taught that the heart that has received the good news is the heart that pleads for the doomed, and the intercession for the sinners is the mark of the mercy, and the believer who intercedes for the people of the sin is the believer who follows the example of Abraham, the friend whose compassion could not rest while the destruction approached. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the mercy of the intercession: Abraham pleaded for the people of Lot, and the pleading is the tenderness that the Lord Himself praised in the next verse, and the believer who pleads for the sinners is the believer whose heart the mercy has not left. The verse is the record of the plea, and the teaching of the compassion that the good news does not extinguish.",
  "75": "“Truly, Abraham was forbearing, tender-hearted, and ever turning ˹to his Lord˺.”” explains the behavior the previous verse described, and the commentators note the three qualities that the verse names. The verse states the three qualities: “Truly, Abraham was forbearing, tender-hearted, and ever turning to his Lord,” or “inna Ibraheema la-haleemun awwahun muneeb,” and the commentators explain each: the “haleem,” the forbearing, the one who restrains the anger and bears the wrong; the “awwah,” the tender-hearted, the one who sighs and sorrows, the one whose heart is soft and whose compassion is deep, and the commentators note the meanings of the “awwah,” the sighing, the entreating, the tender, and the “muneeb,” the ever-turning, the one who returns to his Lord in the repentance and the devotion, and the three qualities together are the portrait of the character that the plea of Abraham had displayed. Ibn Kathir notes the significance, for the verse explains the plea of the previous verse, and the explanation is the teaching that the intercession for the sinners is the fruit of the forbearance, the tenderness, and the turning, and the three qualities are the measure of the friend of God. The verse is the praise of Abraham, and the commentators note that the praise is the Lord’s own testimony to the character of His friend, and the testimony is the honour that the believer seeks to inherit.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the three qualities, and the commentators connect them to the believer’s own character. The believer is taught that the forbearance and the tenderness and the turning are the qualities of the friend of God, and the one who pleads for the sinners with the heart of the tender and the forbearing is the one who follows the example of Abraham, and the turning to the Lord is the root of the two, for the heart that returns to its Lord is the heart that is soft and patient, and the believer who cultivates the three qualities is the believer whom the Lord praises as He praised Abraham. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the character of the intercessor: the plea for the doomed is not the hard demand but the tender sigh, and the forbearance and the turning are the two wings of the compassion, and the believer who intercedes with the heart of Abraham is the believer whose intercession the Lord records. The verse is the praise of Abraham, and the teaching of the three qualities that the intercession requires.",
  "76": "“˹The angels said,˺ “O Abraham! Plead no more! Your Lord’s decree has already come, and they will certainly be afflicted with a punishment that cannot be averted!”” ends the discussion, and the commentators note the two parts of the address. The first is the command: “O Abraham! Plead no more!” or “ya Ibraheemu a‘rid ‘an hatha,” and the commentators explain the meaning, for the angels commanded Abraham to cease the pleading, and the command is the declaration that the intercession had reached its limit, for the decree had been issued and the pleading could not reverse it, and the command is the gentle closing of the door, the teaching that the mercy of the intercession is bounded by the decree of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the directness of the address, for the angels spoke to Abraham by name, and the address is the intimacy of the messengers of the Lord with the friend of the Lord, and the command is the mercy that spares the pleader the futility. The second part is the declaration: “Your Lord’s decree has already come, and they will certainly be afflicted with a punishment that cannot be averted!” or “innahu qad ja’a amru rabbika wa innahum ateehim ‘athabun ghayru mardood,” and the commentators note the two clauses, the arrival of the decree and the certainty of the punishment, and the word “mardood,” the averted, the returned, is the key, for the punishment of the people of Lot was the punishment that could not be returned, and the declaration is the statement of the finality, the teaching that the decree of the Lord, once issued, is the decree that the plea cannot turn.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the limit, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own intercession. The believer is taught that the intercession is the duty of the compassion, and the limit of the intercession is the decree of the Lord, and the believer who pleads for the sinners must know the boundary, and the ceasing of the plea is the submission, the acceptance that the decree of the Lord is the final word, and the one who pleads and then submits is the believer whose compassion is complete and whose faith is whole. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the balance of the intercession: Abraham pleaded and then ceased, and the two together are the model of the believer, the compassion that intercedes and the faith that submits, and the decree of the Lord is the boundary that both honour. The verse is the closing of the plea, and the teaching of the submission that the decree commands.",
  "77": "“When Our messenger-angels came to Lot, he was distressed and worried by their arrival. He said, “This is a terrible day.”” moves the scene to Lot, and the commentators note the distress and its cause. The verse describes the arrival and the reaction: “When Our messenger-angels came to Lot, he was distressed and worried by their arrival,” or “wa lamma ja’at rusuluna Lootan see’a bihim wa daqa bihim thar‘an,” and the commentators explain the distress, for the angels came in the form of the handsome young men, and Lot feared for their safety, for he knew the wickedness of his people and the danger that the guests would face, and the distress is the anxiety of the host who knew the evil of the town, and the word “daqa bihim thar‘an” — the narrowing of the arm, the constraint — is the description of the anguish that the arrival caused. Ibn Kathir notes the detail, for Lot did not know that the guests were the angels, and the fear for the guests was the fear of the man who knew what his people would do, and the verse records the fear as the measure of the wickedness of the town. The verse then records the cry: “This is a terrible day,” or “qala hatha yawmun ‘aseeb,” and the commentators note the word “‘aseeb,” the terrible, the harsh, for Lot saw the day of the arrival as the day of the trial, and the cry is the foreboding of the host, and the commentators note the irony, for the day that Lot called terrible was the day of his salvation, and the night of the destruction was the night of the deliverance.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the distress, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own anxiety. The believer is taught that the distress of the host for the guest is the mark of the honour, and the one who fears for the safety of the guest is the one whose hospitality the Lord values, and the day that the believer calls terrible is sometimes the day of the deliverance, and the foreboding of the heart is not the measure of the outcome, for the Lord who sent the trial is the Lord who turns it to the rescue. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the honour of the host: Lot feared for the guests, and the fear was the proof of his purity, and the believer who fears for the honour of his guest is the believer whose fear the Lord turns to the safety. The verse is the record of the distress, and the teaching of the honour that the anxiety reveals.",
  "78": "“And ˹the men of˺ his people—who were used to shameful deeds—came to him rushing. He pleaded, “O my people! Here are my daughters ˹for marriage˺—they are pure for you. So fear Allah, and do not humiliate me by disrespecting my guests. Is there not ˹even˺ a single right-minded man among you?”” shows Lot’s fear realized, and the commentators note the scene and the plea. The verse describes the rush: “And the men of his people—who were used to shameful deeds—came to him rushing,” or “wa ja’ahu qawmuhu yuhra‘oona ilayhi wa min qablu kanoo ya‘maloona as-sayyi’at,” and the commentators explain the scene, for the men of the town, having heard of the handsome guests, rushed to the house of Lot, and the rush is the description of the eagerness of the corrupt, and the phrase “who were used to shameful deeds” is the diagnosis of the state, the habitual sin that the rush revealed. Ibn Kathir notes the detail, for the men came to the house demanding the guests, and the demand was the shamelessness that the verse records. The verse then records the plea of Lot: “O my people! Here are my daughters for marriage—they are pure for you. So fear Allah, and do not humiliate me by disrespecting my guests. Is there not even a single right-minded man among you?” or “qala ya qawmi ha’ula’i banatee hunna atharu lakum fattaqoo Allaha wa la tukhzooni fee dayfee a-laysa minkum rajulun rasheed,” and the commentators note the plea, for Lot offered his daughters in the lawful marriage and begged the people to fear God and to spare his guests the humiliation, and the question “is there not a single right-minded man” is the plea of the despairing, the search for the one man of the reason among the crowd of the corrupt, and the plea is the measure of the isolation of the prophet in the town of the sin.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the plea, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own loneliness. The believer is taught that the prophet in the corrupt town is the loneliest of men, and the plea for the single right-minded man is the plea of the one who stands alone, and the believer who holds to the purity in the midst of the corruption is the believer whose plea the Lord hears, and the rescue that the following verses record is the answer of the plea. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the reality of the isolation: Lot stood against the rushing crowd and pleaded, and the plea was the loneliness of the pure, and the believer who stands for the purity in the corrupt society is the believer whose loneliness the Lord will turn to the deliverance. The verse is the record of the rush and the plea, and the teaching of the loneliness that the purity endures.",
  "79": "“They argued, “You certainly know that we have no need for your daughters. You already know what we desire!”” is their answer, and the commentators note the shamelessness of the reply. The verse records the reply of the people: “You certainly know that we have no need for your daughters. You already know what we desire!” or “qaloo laqad ‘alimta ma lana fee banatika min haqqin wa innaka la-ta‘lamu ma nureed,” and the commentators explain the shamelessness, for the people declared that they had no desire for the daughters and that Lot knew what they desired, and the declaration is the confession of the sin, the open avowal of the desire that the plea of Lot had sought to divert, and the shamelessness of the reply is the measure of the corruption, the people who no longer hid the sin but declared it, and the declaration is the seal of the destruction, for the sin that is avowed is the sin that the punishment cannot spare. Ibn Kathir notes the gravity of the reply, for the people answered the plea of the purity with the declaration of the desire, and the declaration is the proof that the town had passed the point of the return, and the shamelessness is the threshold of the ruin.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the avowal, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own witness. The believer is taught that the sin that is hidden is the sin that may be healed, and the sin that is avowed is the sin that the punishment awaits, and the people of Lot passed from the hidden to the avowed, and the passage was the sealing of the fate, and the believer who sees the sin of his age avowed is the believer who knows that the judgment is near, and the warning of the verse is the warning against the shamelessness that the Lord does not spare. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the threshold of the ruin: the avowal of the sin is the declaration of the war against the Lord, and the war is the war that the punishment answers, and the believer who holds to the purity while the sin is avowed is the believer whose deliverance the Lord prepares. The verse is the record of the avowal, and the teaching of the shamelessness that the judgment answers.",
  "80": "“He responded, “If only I had the strength ˹to resist you˺ or could rely on a strong supporter.”” is Lot’s lament, and the commentators note the two wishes and their meaning. The verse records the lament: “If only I had the strength to resist you or could rely on a strong supporter,” or “qala law anna lee bikum quwwatan aw awee ila ruknin shadeed,” and the commentators explain the two wishes, for Lot wished for the strength, the “quwwah,” the power to resist the crowd, and for the “rukn shadeed,” the strong supporter, the pillar, the tribe, the ally that would back him, and the two wishes are the lament of the lonely, the confession of the weakness of the prophet who stood alone against the crowd, and the commentators note the tenderness of the lament, for the prophet who had no supporter among the people wished for the support that the Lord was about to send, and the wish was the prelude of the deliverance. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for Lot had no tribe among his people, for he was the stranger in the town, and the wish for the “rukn shadeed” was the wish for the support that the angels would reveal, and the lament is the last word of the man before the revelation of the help.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the weakness, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own reliance. The believer is taught that the weakness of the believer is the occasion of the divine strength, and the one who confesses the weakness is the one whom the Lord supports, and the wish for the supporter is the prayer that the Lord answers, and the believer who laments the lack of the strength is the believer whom the strength of the Lord will fill, for the “rukn shadeed” that Lot wished for was the Lord Himself, and the Lord is the strongest of the supporters. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the comfort of the weakness: Lot wished for the strength and the support, and the angels answered the wish, and the believer who confesses his weakness to the Lord is the believer whose weakness the Lord turns to the strength, and the support that the believer lacks is the support that the Lord supplies. The verse is the record of the lament, and the teaching of the strength that the weakness invites.",
  "81": "“The angels said, “O Lot! We are the messengers of your Lord. They will never reach you. So travel with your family in the dark of night, and do not let any of you look back, except your wife. She will certainly suffer the fate of the others. Their appointed time is the morning. Is the morning not near?”” ends the concealment, and the commentators note the revelation and the command. The verse records the revelation: “O Lot! We are the messengers of your Lord. They will never reach you,” or “qaloo ya Lootu inna rusulu rabbika lan yasiloo ilayka,” and the commentators explain the revelation, for the angels declared their identity and the safety of Lot, and the declaration is the answer to the lament of the previous verse, the strength and the support that Lot had wished for, and the assurance that the crowd would never reach him. Ibn Kathir notes the significance, for the concealment of the angels was ended, and the truth of their mission was declared, and the fear of the host was turned to the safety. The verse then records the command: “So travel with your family in the dark of night, and do not let any of you look back, except your wife. She will certainly suffer the fate of the others,” or “fa-asri bi-ahlika bi-qit‘in mina al-layli wa la yaltafit minkum ahadun illa imra’ataka innahu museebuha ma asabahum,” and the commentators note the two parts of the command, the travel in the dark of the night, and the prohibition of the looking back, and the exception of the wife, and the verse closes with the appointment: “Their appointed time is the morning. Is the morning not near?” or “inna maw‘idahumu as-subhu a-laysa as-subhu bi-qareeb,” and the commentators note the question, for the morning was the appointed time of the destruction, and the question “is the morning not near” is the declaration of the nearness, the assurance that the deliverance and the destruction were at hand.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the command, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own obedience. The believer is taught that the command of the deliverance is the command of the night, the departure without the looking back, and the looking back is the attachment to the world that the deliverance demands the believer to leave, and the exception of the wife is the teaching that the salvation is not the kinship but the faith, and the morning is the appointed time that the believer awaits with the trust. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the discipline of the departure: the travel was at night, the look was forbidden, and the morning was near, and the believer who departs from the sin with the obedience of Lot is the believer whose deliverance the Lord completes. The verse is the record of the revelation and the command, and the teaching of the departure that the deliverance requires.",
  "82": "“When Our command came, We turned the cities upside down and rained down on them clustered stones of baked clay,” describes the destruction of Lot’s people, and the commentators note the two instruments of the punishment. The verse states the command and the punishment: “When Our command came, We turned the cities upside down,” or “fa-lamma ja’a amruna ja‘alna ‘aliyaha safilaha,” and the commentators explain the overturning, for the cities of the plain were lifted and inverted, the “aliyaha safilaha,” the high made low, the tops turned to the bottoms, and the overturning is the punishment that the Quran records, the inversion of the cities that the sin had corrupted. Ibn Kathir notes the detail, for the cities of Sodom and the neighbouring towns were the ones that the punishment overturned, and the overturning is the complete destruction, the ruin that the sin of the inversion earned, and the commentators note the symmetry, for the people who inverted the order of the nature were punished with the inversion of their cities. The verse then records the second instrument: “and rained down on them clustered stones of baked clay,” or “wa amtarna ‘alayha hijaratan min sijjeelin mandood,” and the commentators explain the stones, the “hijarah min sijjeel,” the stones of the baked clay, the “sijjeel,” and the “mandood,” the clustered, the piled, the successive, and the raining of the stones upon the overturned cities was the completion of the punishment, the shower that followed the overturning, and the two instruments together are the complete destruction, the inversion and the stoning, and the verse is the record of the punishment that the sin earned.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the inversion, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own warning. The believer is taught that the sin that inverts the order of the Lord is the sin that the inversion of the punishment answers, and the cities that were turned upside down are the monument of the corruption that the Lord does not spare, and the stones that rained upon the overturned are the measure of the wrath that the shamelessness invites, and the believer who sees the punishment in the verse is the believer who holds to the order that the Lord established. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the gravity of the inversion: the people who inverted the nature were inverted with their cities, and the believer who guards the order of the Lord is the believer whom the inversion spares, and the punishment of the cities is the warning that the order of the Lord is the order that the corruption cannot defy. The verse is the record of the destruction, and the teaching of the inversion that the sin invites.",
  "83": "“marked by your Lord ˹O Prophet˺. And these stones are not far from the ˹pagan˺ wrongdoers!”” describes the stones and directs the warning, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The first is the description: “marked by your Lord O Prophet,” or “musawwamatan ‘inda rabbika,” and the commentators explain the meaning, for the stones that rained upon the cities were marked, the “musawwamah,” the marked, the designated, each stone marked with the name of the one it was destined for, and the marking is the precision of the punishment, the declaration that the destruction was not the blind chance but the designated judgment, and the marking is the measure of the justice, the punishment that the Lord appointed for each. Ibn Kathir notes the detail, for the stones were marked by the Lord, and the marking is the teaching that the punishment of the Lord is the precise and the just, the decree that the sin had specified. The second part is the warning: “And these stones are not far from the pagan wrongdoers!” or “wa ma hiya mina az-zalimeena bi-ba‘eed,” and the commentators explain the meaning, for the verse declares that the stones, or the punishment that they represent, are not far from the wrongdoers of the Prophet’s own time, the pagans of Mecca, and the declaration is the warning, the teaching that the punishment of the people of Lot is the punishment that awaits the wrongdoers of every age, and the nearness of the stones is the nearness of the judgment, the assurance that the decree is not the ancient history but the present threat.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the nearness, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own fear. The believer is taught that the punishment of the wrongdoers is not the remote event but the near threat, and the stones that rained upon the cities are the warning that the same justice watches the wrongdoers of the present, and the believer who fears the nearness of the judgment is the believer who hastens to the obedience, and the heedless who forgets the nearness is the heedless whom the judgment will surprise. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the immediacy of the warning: the stones are not far from the wrongdoers, and the believer who reads the warning knows that the decree of the Lord is the present reality, not the past tale, and the nearness of the punishment is the urgency of the repentance. The verse is the description of the stones and the warning, and the teaching of the nearness that the heedless forget.",
  "84": "“And to the people of Midian We sent their brother Shu’aib. He said, “O my people! Worship Allah. You have no god other than Him. And do not give short measure and weight. I do see you in prosperity now, but I truly fear for you the torment of an overwhelming Day.”” opens the account of Shu‘ayb, and the commentators note the message and the specific command. The verse introduces the people and the prophet: “And to the people of Midian We sent their brother Shu‘ayb,” or “wa ila Madyana akhahum Shu‘ayban,” and the commentators explain the identity, for the Midian were the commercial people of the northwest, and Shu‘ayb was their brother, and the message that he brought began with the same call: “O my people! Worship Allah. You have no god other than Him,” or “ya qawmi i‘budoo Allaha ma lakum min ilahin ghayruhu,” the call to the worship of the One, and then the specific command: “And do not give short measure and weight,” or “wa la tanqusoo al-mikyala wal-meezana,” and the commentators explain the command, for the sin of the Midian was the cheating of the scales, the defrauding of the measure and the weight, and the command is the correction of the sin, the demand of the justice in the market. Ibn Kathir notes the context, for the Midian were the traders, and the message of Shu‘ayb joined the call to the worship with the command of the honest measure, and the two together are the complete faith, the worship of the One and the justice of the hand. The verse closes with the observation and the warning: “I do see you in prosperity now, but I truly fear for you the torment of an overwhelming Day,” or “innee arakum bi-khayrin wa innee akhafu ‘alaykum ‘athaba yawmin muheet,” and the commentators note the two parts, the acknowledgment of the present prosperity and the fear of the overwhelming Day, and the pairing is the teaching, for the prosperity of the present is not the guarantee of the safety, and the fear of the Day is the warning that the prosperity could not silence.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the market, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own honesty. The believer is taught that the justice of the scale is the part of the faith, and the one who cheats the measure has cheated the Lord, and the prosperity that the cheating built is the prosperity that the Day will weigh, and the believer who trades with the honest measure is the believer whose trade the Lord blesses, and the fear of the overwhelming Day is the fear that the honest and the dishonest alike must hold. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the completeness of the worship: the worship of the One and the justice of the scale are the two halves of the faith, and the believer who worships and cheats has divided the faith, and the message of Shu‘ayb is the warning against the division. The verse is the opening of the account of Shu‘ayb, and the teaching of the justice that the worship requires.",
  "85": "“O my people! Give full measure and weigh with justice. Do not defraud people of their property, nor go about spreading corruption in the land.”” continues the command of the justice, and the commentators note the three prohibitions and their meaning. The first is the command of the fullness: “Give full measure and weigh with justice,” or “ya qawmi awfoo al-mikyala wal-meezana bil-qisti,” and the commentators explain the “qist,” the justice, the equity, and the command, for the fullness of the measure and the justice of the weight are the two pillars of the honest trade, and the command is the positive of the previous verse’s prohibition, the demand of the fairness that the cheating had abandoned. Ibn Kathir notes the pairing, for the verse commands the fullness after the previous verse had prohibited the shorting, and the positive and the negative together are the complete law of the scale. The second is the prohibition of the defrauding: “Do not defraud people of their property,” or “wa la tabkhasoo an-nasa ashya’ahum,” and the commentators explain the “bakhs,” the defrauding, the shortchanging, the stealing of the value, and the prohibition is the protection of the property of the people, the declaration that the wealth of the other is the trust that the trader must not betray. The third is the prohibition of the corruption: “nor go about spreading corruption in the land,” or “wa la ta‘thaw fi al-ardi mufsideen,” and the commentators note the word “ta‘thaw,” the spreading, the going about, and the “ifsad,” the corruption, and the prohibition is the command of the order, the demand that the believer not spread the disorder in the land, and the three together are the complete law of the society, the justice of the scale, the protection of the property, and the preservation of the order.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the justice, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own dealings. The believer is taught that the justice is the foundation of the society, and the one who gives the full measure and protects the property and preserves the order is the one who builds the society that the Lord blesses, and the corruption of the scale and the property and the land is the corruption that the Lord destroys, and the believer who deals with the justice of the verse is the believer whose dealings are the worship. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the completeness of the justice: the measure, the weight, the property, and the land, and the four are the four trusts of the society, and the believer who honours the four is the believer whose society the Lord preserves, and the corruption of the four is the corruption that the punishment of Midian answered. The verse is the command of the justice, and the teaching of the four trusts that the believer must honour.",
  "86": "“What is left ˹as a lawful gain˺ by Allah is far better for you if you are ˹truly˺ believers. And I am not a keeper over you.”” argues from self-interest, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The first is the argument: “What is left as a lawful gain by Allah is far better for you if you are truly believers,” or “baqiyyatu Allahi khayrun lakum in kuntum mu’mineen,” and the commentators explain the “baqiyyat Allah,” the remainder of God, the lawful gain that the honest measure leaves, and the argument, for Shu‘ayb told the people that the lawful remainder was better than the unlawful fullness, and the better is the “khayr” that the honest trade secures, and the condition “if you are truly believers” is the test, for the one who believes knows that the lawful remainder is the better, and the one who doubts is the one who cheats. Ibn Kathir notes the argument, for the verse appeals to the self-interest of the believers, the teaching that the honest gain is the greater gain, and the condition of the belief is the key, for the faith is the measure that weighs the lawful as the better. The second part is the disclaimer: “And I am not a keeper over you,” or “wa ma ana ‘alaykum bi-hafeez,” and the commentators explain the meaning, for Shu‘ayb declared that he was not the keeper, the guardian, the watcher over the people, and the disclaimer is the declaration of the limit, the teaching that the prophet is the warner, not the enforcer, and the people are the ones who bear the responsibility of their own deeds, and the disclaimer is the same that the prophets always made, the freedom of the messenger from the compulsion of the people.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the remainder, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own gain. The believer is taught that the lawful remainder is the better, and the one who cheats for the fullness has traded the better for the worse, and the faith is the measure that knows the difference, and the believer who leaves the unlawful for the lawful is the believer whose gain the Lord multiplies, and the disclaimer of the keeper is the teaching that the responsibility is the believer’s own, and the one who cheats is the one who bears the loss of the cheat. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the wisdom of the honest gain: the remainder of the Lord is the better, and the believer who knows this is the believer who is free of the need for the keeper, for the faith is the keeper that the law cannot replace. The verse is the argument of the remainder, and the teaching of the gain that the faith weighs.",
  "87": "“They asked ˹sarcastically˺, “O Shu’aib! Does your prayer command you that we should abandon what our forefathers worshipped or give up managing our wealth as we please? Indeed, you are such a tolerant, sensible man!”” records the sarcasm of the people, and the commentators note the two objections and the mockery. The first is the objection to the worship: “Does your prayer command you that we should abandon what our forefathers worshipped,” or “a-salatuka ta’muruka an natruka ma ya‘budu aba’una,” and the commentators note the sarcasm, for the people attributed the command of Shu‘ayb to his prayer, and the attribution was the mockery, the sneer at the worship that the command had come from, and the objection to the abandonment of the forefathers’ gods is the oldest of the rejecters’ arguments, the appeal to the inherited that the truth must correct. Ibn Kathir notes the sarcasm, for the word “salatuka” — your prayer — was the mockery of the people, the reduction of the message to the private devotion, and the mockery is the measure of the contempt. The second is the objection to the wealth: “or give up managing our wealth as we please,” or “aw an naf‘ala fee amwalina ma nashao,” and the commentators note the objection, for the people resented the command of the justice that limited their freedom in the wealth, and the resentment is the confession of the sin, the declaration that the wealth was theirs to manage as they pleased, and the confession is the diagnosis of the corruption. The verse closes with the mockery: “Indeed, you are such a tolerant, sensible man!” or “innaka la-anta al-haleemu ar-rasheed,” and the commentators note the irony, for the people mocked the forbearance and the sense of Shu‘ayb, and the mockery of the virtues is the seal of the contempt, the sneer at the very qualities that the prophet possessed.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the sarcasm, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own patience. The believer is taught that the truth is met with the sarcasm, and the mockery of the virtues is the last word of the falsehood, and the believer who is mocked for the forbearance and the sense is the believer whose qualities the falsehood confesses by the mockery, and the patience of the believer is the answer to the sneer, the endurance that the mockery cannot break. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the nature of the opposition: the people mocked the prayer and the forbearance and the sense, and the mockery is the tribute that the falsehood pays to the truth, and the believer who faces the sarcasm with the patience of Shu‘ayb is the believer whose qualities the Lord will vindicate. The verse is the record of the sarcasm, and the teaching of the patience that the mockery requires.",
  "88": "“He said, “O my people! Consider if I stand on a clear proof from my Lord and He has blessed me with a good provision from Him. I do not want to do what I am forbidding you from. I only intend reform to the best of my ability. My success comes only through Allah. In Him I trust and to Him I turn.”” is Shu‘ayb’s full reply, and the commentators note the five declarations that the verse joins. The first is the appeal: “Consider if I stand on a clear proof from my Lord and He has blessed me with a good provision from Him,” or “ya qawmi a-ra’aytum in kuntu ‘ala bayyinatin min rabbee wa razaqanee minhu rizqan hasanan,” and the commentators note the form, for the “a-ra’aytum” is the same invitation to the consideration that the prophets always made, and the mention of the provision is the answer to the objection of the wealth, the declaration that the provision that Shu‘ayb had was the good provision from the Lord, and the proof and the provision are the two credentials of the messenger. Ibn Kathir notes the significance, for the verse answers the mockery of the previous verse with the dignity of the appeal. The second is the integrity: “I do not want to do what I am forbidding you from,” or “wa ma ureedu an ukhalifakum ila ma anhakum ‘anhu,” and the commentators note the declaration, for the messenger who forbids the corruption is the messenger who is free of the corruption, and the declaration is the proof of the integrity, the teaching that the warner is the first to follow the warning. The third is the intention: “I only intend reform to the best of my ability,” or “in ureedu illa al-islaha ma istata‘tu,” and the commentators note the word “islah,” the reform, the setting aright, and the limitation “ma istata‘tu,” to the best of my ability, and the two together are the declaration of the pure intention and the human limit. The fourth and fifth are the reliance: “My success comes only through Allah. In Him I trust and to Him I turn,” or “wa ma tawfeeqee illa billahi ‘alayhi tawakkaltu wa ilayhi uneeb,” and the commentators note the two declarations, the “tawfeeq,” the success, which is only through Allah, and the “tawakkul,” the trust, and the “inabah,” the turning, and the three together are the complete reliance of the messenger.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the integrity, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own sincerity. The believer is taught that the warner must be the first to follow the warning, and the reform is the intention that the believer declares, and the success is the gift that the believer seeks from the Lord alone, and the trust and the turning are the posture of the one who has done his best and left the outcome to the Lord. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the completeness of the integrity: the proof, the provision, the freedom from the sin, the intention of the reform, and the reliance, and the five together are the portrait of the sincere caller, and the believer who calls with the same sincerity is the believer whose success the Lord grants. The verse is the reply of Shu‘ayb, and the teaching of the integrity that the warning requires.",
  "89": "“O my people! Do not let your opposition to me lead you to a fate similar to that of the people of Noah, or Hûd, or Ṣâliḥ. And the people of Lot are not far from you.”” warns by the examples of the past, and the commentators note the warning and the list. The verse records the warning: “Do not let your opposition to me lead you to a fate similar to that of the people of Noah, or Hud, or Salih,” or “ya qawmi la yajrimannakum shiqaqee an yuseebakum mithlu ma asaba qawma Noohin aw qawma Hoodin aw qawma Salihin,” and the commentators explain the meaning, for Shu‘ayb warned his people that the opposition to him would bring upon them the fate of the destroyed nations, and the list — Noah, Hud, and Salih — is the roll of the ruin, the three peoples whose rejection had met the destruction, and the warning is the appeal to the history, the teaching that the sunnah of God does not change, and the same sin meets the same end. Ibn Kathir notes the list, for the people of Noah were drowned, the ‘Ad were destroyed by the wind, and the Thamud by the blast, and the three are the witnesses of the law that the Midian would repeat. The verse then adds the nearness: “And the people of Lot are not far from you,” or “wa ma qawmu Lootin minkum bi-ba‘eed,” and the commentators explain the meaning, for the people of Lot were the neighbours of the Midian, and the nearness is both the nearness of the place and the nearness of the time, and the warning is the teaching that the example is not the remote history but the present threat, and the ruins of the people of Lot were the visible witness that the Midian passed by.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the history, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own reflection. The believer is taught that the history is the warning, and the fate of the destroyed nations is the fate of the nation that repeats their sin, and the nearness of the example is the urgency of the warning, and the believer who reads the history and reflects is the believer who avoids the fate, and the heedless who passes the ruins without the lesson is the heedless whom the fate will reach. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the use of the history: the examples of Noah and Hud and Salih and Lot are the mirrors, and the believer who looks in the mirrors is the believer who sees his own reflection, and the warning of the past is the mercy of the present. The verse is the warning by the examples, and the teaching of the history that the believer must read.",
  "90": "“So seek your Lord’s forgiveness and turn to Him in repentance. Surely my Lord is Most Merciful, All-Loving.”” gives the remedy after the warning, and the commentators note the two commands and the two names. The first part is the command: “So seek your Lord’s forgiveness and turn to Him in repentance,” or “fastaghfiroo rabbakum thumma tooboo ilayhi,” and the commentators note the two commands, the “istighfar” and the “tawbah,” the seeking of the forgiveness and the turning of the repentance, and the “so” is the link, for the warning of the previous verse is followed by the remedy of this verse, and the remedy is the return, the same two commands that every prophet delivered, and the two together are the door of the mercy that the warning had opened. Ibn Kathir notes the sequence, for the warning is the preparation of the repentance, and the repentance is the answer of the warning, and the two together are the complete message, the fear that warns and the mercy that receives. The second part is the praise: “Surely my Lord is Most Merciful, All-Loving,” or “inna rabbee raheemun wadood,” and the commentators note the two names, the “raheem,” the Most Merciful, and the “wadood,” the All-Loving, the One whose love is the attribute, and the commentators dwell on the name “wadood,” for it is the declaration that the Lord loves the repentant, and the love of the Lord is the reward of the return, and the pairing of the mercy and the love is the assurance that the door of the return is not only the door of the pardon but the door of the affection.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the remedy, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own return. The believer is taught that the warning is the prelude of the remedy, and the repentance is the answer of the warning, and the Lord who warns is the Lord who loves, and the believer who hears the warning and returns is the believer whom the mercy and the love receive, and the name “wadood” is the comfort that the return is not the fear alone but the affection, the love of the Lord for the one who comes back. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the warmth of the mercy: the Lord is the Most Merciful and the All-Loving, and the believer who turns with the repentance is the believer whom the Lord loves, and the two names are the two arms of the reception that the return finds. The verse is the command of the remedy, and the teaching of the love that the repentance receives.",
  "91": "“They threatened, “O Shu’aib! We do not comprehend much of what you say, and surely we see you powerless among us. Were it not for your clan, we would have certainly stoned you, for you are nothing to us.”” records the threat of the people, and the commentators note the three parts of the threat. The first is the dismissal: “We do not comprehend much of what you say,” or “ma nafqahu katheeran mimma taqool,” and the commentators note the admission, for the people declared that they did not understand the message, and the admission is the confession of the heedlessness, the declaration that the truth had reached them and they had not opened the heart to it, and the incomprehension is the state of the one who hears with the ear and not the heart. Ibn Kathir notes the irony, for the admission of the incomprehension is the admission of the blindness, and the blindness is the disease that the message had come to cure. The second is the assessment: “and surely we see you powerless among us,” or “wa inna la-naraka feena da‘eefan,” and the commentators note the word “da‘eef,” the weak, the powerless, for the people measured the prophet by the power of the tribe, and the measure is the confession of the worldly scale, the assessment of the truth by the strength of the bearer. The third is the threat: “Were it not for your clan, we would have certainly stoned you, for you are nothing to us,” or “wa law la rahtuka la-rajamnaka wa ma anta ‘alayna bi-‘azeez,” and the commentators note the threat, for the people declared that the clan of Shu‘ayb was the only shield, and the declaration is the measure of the contempt, the statement that the prophet was nothing without the tribe, and the threat of the stoning is the last word of the rejection, the violence that the argument could not supply.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the threat, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own protection. The believer is taught that the world measures the truth by the power, and the bearer of the truth is often the weak in the eyes of the world, and the clan that shields the prophet is the clan that the Lord has appointed, and the believer who is threatened for the truth is the believer whose protection is the Lord, and the threat of the stoning is the threat that the Lord answers with the deliverance. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the reality of the protection: the people threatened Shu‘ayb and trusted the clan, and the Lord protected Shu‘ayb and destroyed the threateners, and the believer who is threatened for the truth is the believer whose shield is the Lord, not the clan. The verse is the record of the threat, and the teaching of the protection that the Lord supplies.",
  "92": "“He said, “O my people! Do you have more regard for my clan than for Allah, turning your back on Him entirely? Surely my Lord is Fully Aware of what you do.”” turns the threat into a question, and the commentators note the two parts of the reply. The first is the question: “Do you have more regard for my clan than for Allah, turning your back on Him entirely?” or “ya qawmi a-raqhtee a‘azzu ‘alaykum mina Allahi wattakhathtumoohu wara’akum zihriyyan,” and the commentators explain the meaning, for Shu‘ayb asked his people whether the clan was more honoured by them than God, and the question exposed the scale of the people, for the clan that they feared was the creature, and the Lord whom they defied was the Creator, and the phrase “turning your back on Him entirely” — “ittakhathtumoohu wara’akum zihriyyan,” literally “you cast Him behind your backs” — is the description of the neglect, the casting of the Lord behind the back, and the question is the rebuke, the exposure of the inversion that the fear of the clan had revealed. Ibn Kathir notes the force of the question, for the people who feared the clan and defied the Lord had reversed the order, and the reversal is the measure of the corruption, and the question is the correction of the scale. The second part is the warning: “Surely my Lord is Fully Aware of what you do,” or “inna rabbee bima ta‘maloona muheet,” and the commentators note the word “muheet,” the encompassing, the surrounding, for the Lord is the One who encompasses the deeds, and the warning is the declaration that the deeds of the people were not hidden, and the threat of the stoning and the fear of the clan were all within the knowledge of the Lord, and the encompassing knowledge is the assurance of the judgment.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the scale, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own fear. The believer is taught that the fear of the clan is the fear of the creature, and the fear of the Lord is the fear of the Creator, and the one who fears the clan more than the Lord has cast the Lord behind his back, and the believer who corrects the scale is the believer who fears the Lord alone, and the encompassing knowledge of the Lord is the comfort of the one who is threatened, for the Lord who knows the deeds is the Lord who judges the threateners. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the correction of the fear: Shu‘ayb asked the question that exposed the inversion, and the believer who asks the same question of his own heart is the believer who has corrected the scale, and the fear of the Lord is the fear that the threat of the creature cannot shake. The verse is the reply of Shu‘ayb, and the teaching of the scale that the fear must correct.",
  "93": "“O my people! Persist in your ways, for I ˹too˺ will persist in mine. You will soon come to know who will be visited by a humiliating torment and is a liar! And watch! I too am watching with you.”” is Shu‘ayb’s final word, and the commentators note the two parts of the declaration. The first is the persistence: “Persist in your ways, for I too will persist in mine,” or “ya qawmi i‘maloo ‘ala makanatikum innee ‘amilun,” and the commentators explain the meaning, for Shu‘ayb declared that he would persist in the message and the warning, and the declaration is not the despair but the constancy, the statement of the prophet who would not cease the call, and the persistence of the prophet is the answer to the persistence of the people, the two paths that the verse places before them. Ibn Kathir notes the force of the declaration, for the persistence of the prophet is the confidence of the one who knows the outcome, and the persistence of the people is the stubbornness of the one who has chosen the ruin. The second part is the challenge: “You will soon come to know who will be visited by a humiliating torment and is a liar! And watch! I too am watching with you,” or “sawfa ta‘lamoona man ya’teehim ‘athabun yukhzeehi wa man huwa kathibun wartaqiboo innee ma‘akum raqeeb,” and the commentators note the two parts of the challenge, the knowledge that the people would soon gain, and the watching that both sides would share, and the challenge is the same that the prophets always made, the commitment of the outcome to the judgment of the Lord, and the word “raqeeb,” the watcher, is the description of the prophet who awaits the decree, and the verse closes the account of Shu‘ayb with the challenge that the next verses answer.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the persistence, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own constancy. The believer is taught that the persistence of the truth is the answer to the persistence of the falsehood, and the prophet who persists in the message while the people persist in the sin is the prophet whose constancy the Lord vindicates, and the watching of the prophet is the patience of the one who awaits the judgment, and the believer who persists in the truth and awaits the judgment is the believer whose outcome is the outcome of the truthful. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the constancy of the caller: Shu‘ayb persisted and watched, and the believer who follows his example is the believer whose persistence the Lord crowns, and the challenge of the prophet is the assurance that the outcome belongs to the Lord. The verse is the final word of Shu‘ayb, and the teaching of the persistence that the judgment rewards.",
  "94": "“When Our command came, We saved Shu’aib and those who believed with him by a mercy from Us. And the ˹mighty˺ blast overtook the wrongdoers, so they fell lifeless in their homes,” records the rescue and the punishment, and the commentators note the pattern and its completion. The verse states the rescue: “When Our command came, We saved Shu‘ayb and those who believed with him by a mercy from Us,” or “wa lamma ja’a amruna najjayna Shu‘ayban wallatheena amanoo ma‘ahu bi-rahmatin minna,” and the commentators note the constancy of the pattern, for the rescue of Shu‘ayb is the same rescue that Noah and Hud and Salih received, the salvation by the mercy, and the constancy is the teaching of the constancy of the divine dealing, the mercy that the faithful always find. Ibn Kathir notes the phrase “by a mercy from Us,” for the salvation is the gift, and the believer who is saved is the one whom the mercy reached. The verse then records the punishment: “And the mighty blast overtook the wrongdoers, so they fell lifeless in their homes,” or “wa akhathati allatheena zalamoo as-sayhatu fa-asbahoo fee diyarihim jathimeen,” and the commentators note the instrument, for the blast of the Midian was the same “sayhah” that had struck the Thamud, and the fall in the homes is the same image, the people struck down in the dwellings, and the parallel is the teaching that the sin of the corruption meets the same punishment, and the “jathimeen,” the fallen, the lifeless, is the word that the Quran reserves for the destroyed.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the pattern, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own hope. The believer is taught that the mercy rescues the faithful and the blast overtakes the corruptors, and the two outcomes are the two sides of the single law, and the believer who stands with the faithful is the believer whom the mercy saves, and the corruptor who persists is the corruptor whom the blast overtakes, and the constancy of the pattern is the comfort of the believer and the warning of the corruptor. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the certainty of the outcome: the rescue and the punishment are the two ends of the single decree, and the believer who trusts the Lord trusts the end, and the corruptor who defies the Lord defies the end, and the blast of Midian is the witness of the end that the corruption invites. The verse is the record of the rescue and the punishment, and the teaching of the pattern that the believer trusts.",
  "95": "“as if they had never lived there. So away with Midian as it was with Thamûd!”” completes the account of Midian, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse and the parallel. The first is the erasure: “as if they had never lived there,” or “ka-an lam yaghnaw feeha,” and the commentators note the repetition of the phrase, for the erasure of the Midian is the same erasure that the Thamud received, and the repetition is the teaching that the destruction of the corruptors is the complete, the removal of the people as if they had never been, and the phrase is the Quran’s way of declaring the totality of the ruin. Ibn Kathir notes the parallel, for the verse uses the same words for the Midian that it used for the Thamud, and the parallel is the teaching of the single law, the same sin and the same end. The second part is the verdict: “So away with Midian as it was with Thamud!” or “ala bu‘dan li-Madyana kama ba‘idat Thamood,” and the commentators note the double verdict, for the Midian received the same “bu‘d,” the distance, the away, that the Thamud received, and the naming of the two together is the summation of the chapter’s histories, the declaration that the corruption of the scale and the corruption of the idolatry both end in the distance, and the verse closes the account of Shu‘ayb with the verdict that the rejection earned.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the parallel, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own warning. The believer is taught that the sin of the market and the sin of the idol are both the rejection, and the destruction of the Midian and the Thamud is the witness that the Lord does not distinguish between the forms of the rejection, and the believer who guards the scale and the worship is the believer who avoids the distance, and the heedless who corrupts the one and the other is the heedless whom the distance awaits. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the unity of the law: the Midian and the Thamud were two peoples and one end, and the believer who reads the two histories reads the one law, and the distance of the two is the warning that the believer must heed. The verse is the verdict upon the Midian, and the teaching of the single law that the histories together declare.",
  "96": "“Indeed, We sent Moses with Our signs and compelling proof” opens the account of Moses in this chapter, and the commentators note the two credentials that the verse names. The verse states the mission: “Indeed, We sent Moses with Our signs and compelling proof,” or “wa laqad arsalna Moosa bi-ayatina wa sultanin mubeen,” and the commentators explain the two things that Moses brought: the “ayat,” the signs, the miracles, and the “sultan mubeen,” the compelling proof, the manifest authority, and the two together are the complete credentials of the messenger, the signs that the senses witnessed and the proof that the reason could not deny, and the pairing is the teaching that the message of Moses was not the bare claim but the demonstrated truth. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the “sultan” is the authority, the proof, the argument that silences, and the “mubeen,” the clear, is the description of the proof, and the verse declares that Moses came with the signs and the authority that the tyrant could not answer. The verse is the opening of the account of Moses, and the statement of the credentials that the confrontation would test, and the commentators connect it to the pattern of the chapter, for the account of Moses is the last of the prophetic histories, the summary of the confrontation that the shorter accounts had introduced.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the proof, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own certainty. The believer is taught that the truth of the message is the truth of the proof, and the messenger who comes with the signs and the authority is the messenger whose claim the reason can test, and the believer who has examined the proof is the believer whose faith is the certainty, and the one who rejects the proof is the one whose rejection is the will, not the intellect. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the nature of the message: the signs and the authority are the evidence, and the believer who receives them with the open heart is the believer whose faith the evidence anchors, and the denier who rejects them with the closed heart is the denier whose rejection the evidence exposes. The verse is the opening of the account, and the teaching of the proof that the truth supplies.",
  "97": "“to Pharaoh and his chiefs, but they followed the command of Pharaoh, and Pharaoh’s command was not well guided.”” records the mission and the response, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The first is the recipients: “to Pharaoh and his chiefs,” or “ila Fir‘awna wa mala’ihi,” and the commentators note the identification, for the mission of Moses was addressed to the tyrant and his ruling class, and the addressing of the head of the state is the teaching that the message confronts the power, and the messenger does not fear the throne. Ibn Kathir explains the significance, for the sending of Moses to Pharaoh was the sending of the truth to the mightiest power of the age, and the confrontation of the messenger and the tyrant is the permanent drama of the history. The second part is the response: “but they followed the command of Pharaoh, and Pharaoh’s command was not well guided,” or “fattaba‘oo amra Fir‘awna wa ma amru Fir‘awna bi-rasheed,” and the commentators note the two clauses, the following of the people and the misguidance of the command, and the word “rasheed,” the well-guided, the right, is the key, for the verse declares that the command of Pharaoh was not the right guidance, and the declaration is the verdict upon the rule of the tyrant, the teaching that the command that is followed without the guidance is the command that leads the followers to the ruin, and the following of the people is the corruption that the misguidance produced.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the following, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own allegiance. The believer is taught that the following of the tyrant is the following of the misguidance, and the command that is not the well-guided is the command that the believer must not follow, and the obedience of the believer is to the command that is the right, the command of the Lord and His messenger, and the one who follows the misguidance is the one whose end is the end of the follower. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the criterion of the following: the command of Pharaoh was not the well-guided, and the believer who follows the command must ask whether the command is the right, and the following of the wrong command is the corruption that the verse condemns. The verse is the record of the response, and the teaching of the allegiance that the right command alone deserves.",
  "98": "“He will be before his people on the Day of Judgment and will lead them into the Fire. What an evil place to be led into!”” records the fate of Pharaoh and his followers, and the commentators note the two images and the verdict. The first is the leading: “He will be before his people on the Day of Judgment and will lead them into the Fire,” or “yaqdimu qawmahu yawma al-qiyamati fa-awradahumu an-nar,” and the commentators explain the meaning, for Pharaoh will walk before his people on the Day of Judgment and lead them into the Fire, and the leading is the recompense of the following, for the people who followed the command of the tyrant in the world will follow him into the Fire in the next, and the verse teaches the terrible symmetry, the leader and the followers bound together in the ruin that the leadership earned. Ibn Kathir notes the image, for the “yaqdimu” is the walking before, and the “awrada,” the leading to the water, is used here with the terrible irony, for the tyrant who led his people to the false worship will lead them to the Fire, and the leading is the harvest of the following. The second part is the verdict: “What an evil place to be led into!” or “wa bi’sa al-wirdu al-mawrood,” and the commentators note the word “wirdu,” the watering place, the destination, and the exclamation, for the Fire is the evil destination, the “bi’sa al-wirdu” that the Quran reserves for the worst of the ends, and the verdict is the seal of the account, the declaration that the end of the following is the evil that the verse names.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the leadership, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own responsibility. The believer is taught that the leadership is the trust, and the leader who leads to the misguidance leads to the Fire, and the follower who follows the misguidance is the follower whom the leader drags into the ruin, and the believer who is given the leadership must lead to the guidance, and the believer who is given the following must follow the right, and the two responsibilities are the two sides of the verse’s warning. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the weight of the following: the people of Pharaoh followed and were led into the Fire, and the believer who follows the wrong command is the believer whose end is the end of the follower, and the right command is the command that leads to the Paradise, and the believer must choose the leader by the destination. The verse is the record of the fate, and the teaching of the leadership that the Day will weigh.",
  "99": "“They were followed by a curse in this ˹life˺ and ˹will receive another˺ on the Day of Judgment. What an evil gift to receive!”” records the two-fold curse upon Pharaoh and his people, and the commentators note the parallel with the earlier verdicts. The verse states the curse: “They were followed by a curse in this life and will receive another on the Day of Judgment,” or “wa utbi‘oo fee hathihi la‘natan wa yawma al-qiyamah bi’sa ar-rifdu al-marfood,” and the commentators explain the meaning, for the curse followed Pharaoh and his people in this world and will follow them on the Day of Judgment, and the two curses are the same two that the ‘Ad received, and the parallel is the teaching of the single law, the rejection of the Lord meeting the same end in every age. Ibn Kathir notes the parallel, for the verse uses the same words for Pharaoh that it used for the ‘Ad, and the parallel is the summation of the chapter, the declaration that the tyrant and the idolater and the corruptor all receive the same curse. The verse closes with the verdict: “What an evil gift to receive!” or “bi’sa ar-rifdu al-marfood,” and the commentators note the word “rifd,” the gift, the grant, and the irony, for the curse is the evil gift, the “rifd marfood,” the grant that the rejection earned, and the exclamation is the seal of the account, the declaration that the recompense of the rejection is the evil that the verse names.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the curse, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own fear. The believer is taught that the curse of the world and the Day is the recompense of the rejection, and the tyrant and his followers are the warning of the end that the defiance earns, and the believer who avoids the curse is the believer who holds to the guidance, and the evil gift is the measure of the distance that the rejection invites. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the completeness of the recompense: the curse of the world and the curse of the Day, and the believer who reads the two curses is the believer who clings to the mercy that the rejection forfeits, and the chapter’s histories together are the single warning that the believer must heed. The verse is the verdict upon Pharaoh, and the teaching of the curse that the defiance earns.",
  "100": "\"These are accounts, We relate to you ˹O Prophet˺, of the ˹destroyed˺ cities\" steps back from the narratives and speaks about them as a group. Six communities have now been described, and the chapter pauses to make sure the reader understands why they were told.\n\nThe Arabic word for these accounts indicates news or reports, information conveyed about real events. The Quran presents them as history rather than as parables, and the previous mention of these accounts in this chapter emphasised that neither the Prophet nor his people had known them before.\n\n\"Some are still standing ˹barren˺, while others have been mowed down\" divides the ruins into two kinds, and the observation is precise enough to be verified. Some destroyed settlements leave visible remains. The carved dwellings of Thamud still stand in the north of Arabia, and the Prophet's companions saw them on the way to Tabuk. Other places disappear completely, their traces erased by sand, water, or time, so that nothing marks where a nation once lived. The Arabic image for the second kind is of a crop that has been harvested and cut down to the ground.\n\nBoth outcomes serve the same purpose, though in different ways. Visible ruins are a standing warning that can be walked through. Vanished cities are a warning of another sort, showing that a people can leave nothing at all behind.\n\nThere is an implicit argument in this pairing. People often assume that a civilisation's importance is measured by what survives of it. The verse treats both fates as equally final. Whether the stones remain or not, the community is gone, and the question of what happened to the people themselves has been settled elsewhere.\n\nThe purpose of the whole survey becomes clear in the verses that follow. The Meccans were living in a region marked by such sites and travelling past them regularly. They were being asked to read the landscape as evidence rather than scenery, and to consider whether the pattern that produced those ruins was operating in their own city.",
  "101": "\"We did not wrong them, rather they wronged themselves\" states the conclusion the entire chapter has been building toward. Six nations have been destroyed in these pages, and someone might reasonably ask whether such punishment was just. The answer is placed here, immediately after the survey of the ruins.\n\nThe Arabic word \"zulm\" means putting something in the wrong place, and it covers injustice and oppression. Its denial with respect to Allah is absolute. Every one of these peoples was warned by a messenger raised from among themselves, given clear evidence, offered forgiveness, and given time, in some cases centuries. Nothing was withheld from them. When they refused, they were choosing an outcome that had been described to them in advance.\n\nThe previous chapter made the same point in nearly identical terms, saying that Allah does not wrong people in the least but it is people who wrong themselves. Its repetition here, at the summary of six destructions, shows how central it is.\n\n\"The gods they invoked beside Allah were of no help at all when the command of your Lord came\" returns to what these communities had relied on. Each of them had objects of worship they defended fiercely, and in every case the same thing happened at the decisive moment: nothing. The idols of Noah's people, the gods Hud's people accused of striking him with madness, the deities of Midian, and the divine claims of Pharaoh all proved equally silent.\n\n\"And only contributed to their ruin\" adds a further point. These gods were not merely useless; they were part of the cause. Attachment to them was what prevented these peoples from accepting the truth, so the very things they thought would protect them were what destroyed them.\n\nThere is a practical extension of this that is easy to overlook. People today rarely worship carved images, yet the same mechanism operates whenever something created becomes the thing a person genuinely relies on. Whatever a person turns to instead of Allah will behave the same way when it is finally tested.",
  "102": "\"Such is the ˹crushing˺ grip of your Lord when He seizes the societies entrenched in wrongdoing\" gathers the six accounts into a single statement about how divine justice operates. The Arabic word for the seizing indicates taking hold of something with force and not letting go, and it is used deliberately after so many descriptions of nations being removed.\n\nThe phrase \"such is\" points backward. This is what it looks like: the flood, the wind, the blast, the overturned cities, the drowned army. The verse is not introducing a new idea but naming the pattern the reader has just watched repeat.\n\nThe description of the communities matters as well. They are described as wrongdoing, using the same root that appeared in the previous verse about people wronging themselves. Divine punishment in the Quran never falls on a community simply because it is different or unsuccessful. It falls on entrenched, persistent wrongdoing after warning has been delivered and refused.\n\n\"Indeed, His grip is ˹terribly˺ painful and severe\" states the nature of that seizure without softening it. The Arabic combines pain with intensity, and the wording is meant to be sobering.\n\nSome readers find such verses difficult, and the difficulty is worth addressing honestly rather than avoided. The Quran presents both aspects of Allah together and does not allow either to be dropped. In this very chapter He is described as All-Forgiving, Most Merciful, and as the Loving. The same chapter describes His grip as painful and severe. Neither cancels the other, and a picture that keeps only one is a picture of something other than what the Quran describes.\n\nThere is a hadith recorded by al-Bukhari and Muslim in which the Prophet said that Allah gives respite to the wrongdoer until, when He seizes him, He does not let him escape, and then he recited this very verse. That comment captures the relationship between the delay described earlier in the chapter and the seizure described here. The waiting is real, and so is the end of it.",
  "103": "\"Surely in this is a sign for those who fear the torment of the Hereafter\" identifies who actually benefits from everything the chapter has recounted. The ruins, the histories, and the patterns are all available to everyone, but they function as a sign only for a particular kind of listener.\n\nThe description is precise: those who fear the punishment of the Hereafter. Not those who are frightened of disaster in general, and not those who merely find history interesting. Someone who believes an accounting is coming reads these accounts as information about his own situation. Someone who does not treats them as archaeology.\n\nThis connects to something stated at the end of the previous chapter's discussion of signs, where it was said that neither signs nor warners benefit those who refuse to believe. The evidence does not change; the reader does.\n\n\"That is a Day for which humanity will be gathered\" moves from the destroyed nations to the event that concerns everyone. The Arabic indicates a collecting together of all people, and the scope is total. Every community mentioned in this chapter, along with every generation before and since, will be present in one place.\n\nThis is worth pausing over, because it answers a question the destruction accounts raise. Those punishments were partial. Whole nations were removed, but the individuals within them had not yet received a final verdict. The gathering is where that is completed.\n\n\"And a Day ˹that will be˺ witnessed ˹by all˺\" adds that nothing about it will be private. The Arabic indicates a day at which all will be present, seen and attended. Commentators have explained that everyone will witness it, and that everything done will be witnessed there.\n\nThe contrast with worldly justice is instructive. Much wrongdoing in this life happens unseen, and much of what is judged is judged on incomplete evidence in front of a handful of people. That Day is described as complete in both respects: everyone is present, and everything is visible. For those who were wronged without anyone noticing, this is not a threat but a promise.",
  "104": "\"We only delay it for a fixed term\" is the shortest verse in this section, and it answers the objection that the previous verses invite. If such a Day is coming, and if the pattern of judgement is as certain as six destroyed nations suggest, why has nothing happened yet?\n\nThe answer is that the delay is deliberate and measured. The Arabic word \"ajal\" means an appointed term set in advance, and it has appeared throughout these two chapters: every community has one, and when it arrives it can be neither advanced nor postponed. Nothing about the waiting is accidental, and nothing about it indicates absence or weakness.\n\nThe word \"only\" in the verse is doing important work. The delay is nothing more than a delay. It is not a cancellation, not a change of plan, and not evidence that the matter has been forgotten. This chapter earlier described people asking what was holding the punishment back, and answered that on the day it overtakes them it will not be averted.\n\nThere is a useful comparison in ordinary experience. A scheduled appointment that has not yet arrived is not a cancelled appointment, and the fact that a person cannot see the date on the calendar does not mean the date is unfixed. What people call delay is usually just the distance between now and a time someone else has set.\n\nThe Quran also explains why the term exists at all. Surah an-Nahl notes that if Allah were to punish people immediately for their wrongdoing, He would not leave a single living creature on earth, and that He delays them for an appointed term. The gap is mercy in operation. It is the space in which repentance is possible, in which the people of Jonah turned back and were spared, and in which the fiercest opponents of the Prophet in Mecca eventually became believers.\n\nRead that way, the delay is not a reason for confidence in escaping. It is the opportunity itself, and it is finite.",
  "105": "\"When that Day arrives, no one will dare speak except with His permission\" describes the atmosphere of the gathering, and the detail chosen is silence. Of everything that could have been mentioned about such a Day, the Quran points first to the absence of speech.\n\nThe choice is telling. In this world, speech is how people defend themselves. Excuses are made, arguments are constructed, influence is exercised through words, and the skilled speaker often escapes what the honest one cannot. On that Day, none of that machinery operates. Nobody speaks unless permitted, which removes at a stroke every advantage that eloquence, status, and quick thinking provide.\n\nThe Quran describes this in several places. Some passages mention that people will not be permitted to make excuses, and others describe faces humbled before the Ever-Living, with voices reduced to a hush so that nothing is heard but a faint whisper. Intercession itself will happen only with permission, as Surah al-Baqarah states in the verse of the Throne.\n\nFor anyone who has been wronged by someone better at talking, there is real justice in this picture. The case will be decided on what actually happened, not on who argues it more persuasively.\n\n\"Some of them will be miserable, others joyful\" divides all of humanity into two groups, and the simplicity is deliberate. Every distinction that mattered in the world, wealth, nationality, tribe, education, reputation, disappears. Two categories remain.\n\nThe Arabic terms indicate wretchedness on one side and felicity on the other. These are not descriptions of momentary emotion but of settled condition, which the following verses go on to detail.\n\nThere is a clarity in this that ordinary life rarely offers. People spend their years sorting themselves into countless overlapping groups, and the sorting feels enormously important while it lasts. This verse cuts through all of it. When the accounting is finished, the only classification that will exist is the one determined by what a person believed and did.",
  "106": "\"As for those bound for misery, they will be in the Fire\" begins the description of the first group, and the Quran characteristically deals with the difficult side first before turning to the good news. The Arabic word for their state indicates wretchedness and ruin, a settled condition rather than a passing feeling.\n\n\"Where they will be sighing and gasping\" gives a detail that is unusual and unsettling. The two Arabic words describe sounds made in breathing under extreme distress: a drawing in of breath with a groan and a shuddering exhalation. Commentators have explained them as sounds of anguish, and some described them by comparison with the braying of a donkey, which begins with a harsh intake and ends with a rasping release.\n\nThe choice to describe punishment through sound rather than through visual detail is worth noticing. The Quran could have described appearances. Instead it makes the reader hear something, and sounds of suffering affect people differently from images of it.\n\nIt is also worth observing what is not being described. This is not the noise of argument, protest, or complaint. The previous verse established that nobody speaks without permission. What remains is involuntary, the sound a body makes when it can no longer form words.\n\nSome readers ask why the Quran includes such passages at all, and the answer lies in what warnings are for. A doctor who describes the consequences of an untreated illness in vivid terms is not being cruel; he is trying to produce action while action is still possible. The Quran pairs these descriptions with repeated offers of forgiveness, and this chapter in particular has been full of them: seek your Lord's forgiveness and turn to Him, He is Most Merciful, All-Loving.\n\nThe Prophet's own attitude reflects this balance. He warned intensely and wept for those who would face this, and he told his companions in a hadith recorded by al-Bukhari and Muslim that he was pulling them back by their waists from the Fire while they were pushing toward it like moths flying into a flame.",
  "107": "\"Staying there forever, as long as the heavens and the earth will endure\" describes the duration of that state, and the expression used has been discussed carefully by commentators. In Arabic usage, saying that something lasts as long as the heavens and the earth endure was a way of expressing permanence, since these were the most enduring things people knew.\n\nScholars have also explained that the heavens and earth referred to here may be those of the Hereafter, since the Quran mentions elsewhere that the earth will be changed into another earth and the heavens as well. On that reading, the comparison points to the permanent creation of the next life rather than to the temporary one we currently inhabit. Either way, the meaning is lasting continuation.\n\n\"Except what your Lord wills\" is the phrase that has generated the most discussion, and honesty requires acknowledging the range of views. Some early commentators held that this exception refers to the period before the wrongdoers enter the Fire, or to a category of sinful believers who are eventually removed from it, which is firmly established in the authentic hadith. Numerous reports in al-Bukhari and Muslim describe people leaving the Fire through intercession and through Allah's mercy, after which they enter Paradise.\n\nA small number of early figures suggested other possibilities, but the mainstream position among scholars is that those who died rejecting faith remain permanently, since many verses state this explicitly, while sinful believers may be purified and then removed. The exception is generally understood to preserve the fact that Allah's will is never constrained, even by His own announced arrangements.\n\n\"Surely your Lord does what He intends\" closes the verse with exactly that point. His decisions are not subject to anyone's expectations, including the expectations formed by readers of scripture.\n\nThere is a discipline in this ending. After a detailed description of a fixed outcome, the Quran reminds the reader that the One deciding is free, wise, and not accountable to human reasoning about what He should do.",
  "108": "\"And as for those destined to joy, they will be in Paradise\" turns to the second group after the description of the wretched, and the structure mirrors the previous verses deliberately. The same phrases are used, which allows the reader to feel the contrast at every point.\n\nThe Arabic word for their state indicates felicity and lasting well-being. Like its opposite, it describes a settled condition rather than a passing mood. Whatever mixture of ease and difficulty a person experienced in the world, this is the state that remains.\n\n\"Staying there forever, as long as the heavens and the earth will endure, except what your Lord wills\" repeats the exact expression used for the Fire, and the parallel raises the same question about the exception. Commentators explained it in a similar spirit, understanding it as preserving the freedom of Allah's will rather than introducing an end to Paradise, since the next phrase rules that out explicitly and many verses elsewhere describe the reward as never being cut off.\n\n\"A ˹generous˺ giving, without end\" is the phrase that settles the matter. The Arabic word \"'ata'\" means a gift, something granted freely rather than owed, and it is described as never being interrupted or withdrawn. Two ideas are being held together here, and both matter.\n\nThe first is that Paradise is a gift. It is not wages calculated against a record of deeds, which is why the Prophet said, in a hadith recorded by al-Bukhari, that nobody's deeds will admit him to Paradise, including his own, unless Allah covers him with mercy. Deeds are the condition, not the purchase price.\n\nThe second is permanence. Every good thing in this life carries the knowledge that it will end, and that awareness quietly shadows even great happiness. Health declines, people are lost, and pleasures fade with familiarity. A gift described as without end removes the one flaw that runs through all worldly enjoyment.\n\nPlacing this immediately after the description of the Fire is characteristic of the Quran, which rarely leaves fear standing alone. Warning is delivered, and then the alternative is shown as reachable.",
  "109": "\"So do not be in doubt ˹O Prophet˺ about what those ˹pagans˺ worship\" returns from the scenes of the Hereafter to the immediate situation in Mecca. As with similar addresses in these chapters, the words are directed to the Prophet while the audience is everyone listening, and the purpose is to strengthen certainty rather than to accuse him of wavering.\n\nThe doubt being ruled out concerns their religion. Someone watching a large, confident society practising an ancient faith might wonder whether all those people could really be mistaken, especially when their traditions are old and their leaders respected. The verse addresses that thought directly.\n\n\"They worship nothing except what their forefathers worshipped before ˹them˺\" gives the reason there is nothing to doubt. Their religion has no evidence behind it, only inheritance. This same objection was raised by the people of Salih, who asked how he dared forbid what their forefathers worshipped, and by the people of Shu'aib. It has been the standard defence of falsehood in every generation described in this chapter.\n\nAge is not a proof of truth. A mistake repeated for a thousand years is still a mistake, and a practice that began without evidence does not acquire evidence by being continued. This is why the Quran keeps asking whether people would follow their forefathers even if those forefathers understood nothing.\n\nIt is worth noting the balance in this. Islam does not reject tradition as such, and much of religious knowledge is transmitted from earlier generations. The difference is that transmission in Islam is meant to carry evidence with it, tracing back to revelation. Inheritance is a means of preserving proof, not a substitute for it.\n\n\"And We will certainly give them their share ˹of punishment˺ in full, without any reduction\" closes with the outcome, using the language of exact measurement. Nothing will be lessened and nothing added. This precision has been a theme throughout the chapter, from the merchants of Midian who were told to give full measure to the assurance here that the final accounting will not shortchange anyone in either direction.",
  "110": "\"Indeed, We had given Moses the Scripture, but differences arose regarding it\" introduces a historical parallel that would have been directly relevant to the Prophet's situation. He was facing rejection and division over the revelation he brought, and this verse points out that the same thing happened to the greatest scripture before him.\n\nThe Torah was given to Moses complete, as guidance and mercy, and its recipients still split over it. This is stated without elaboration, but the pattern is described in more detail elsewhere in the Quran, where those given the Scripture are said to have differed only after knowledge came to them, out of rivalry between themselves. The previous chapter made the same point about the Children of Israel, noting that they did not differ until knowledge came to them.\n\nThat detail deserves attention. Division is usually blamed on ignorance, and the assumption is that better information would settle it. Here, knowledge arrived first and division followed. The cause lies in what people want rather than in what they know.\n\n\"Had it not been for a prior decree from your Lord, their differences would have been settled ˹at once˺\" explains why the disputes were allowed to continue. Allah could have ended them immediately, exposing the truth so plainly that argument became impossible. Instead a prior decision determined that judgement would be deferred, which is the same principle as the appointed term mentioned repeatedly in this chapter. The world is a place of testing, and testing requires that the answer not be forced.\n\n\"They are truly in alarming doubt about it\" describes the state that results. The Arabic indicates disturbing, unsettling uncertainty, the same expression used earlier when the people of Thamud described their reaction to Salih's message.\n\nThere is a caution here for every religious community, including Muslims. Possessing a scripture has never protected a people from splitting over it. What protects is sincerity, and the willingness to accept an answer that does not favour one's own position.",
  "111": "\"And surely your Lord will fully pay all for their deeds\" answers the question left open by the previous verse. If disputes are not settled now, and if people differ over revelation while the delay continues, does that mean the matter is simply left unresolved? No. Payment is guaranteed; only its timing has been deferred.\n\nThe Arabic verb indicates paying something in full, giving the whole of what is due without deduction. This language of complete payment runs through the closing section of this chapter, and it is the same standard Shu'aib demanded of his merchants. The One who instructed people to give full measure does not measure short Himself.\n\nThe wording covers everyone. It is not restricted to the deniers described in the previous verses or to the believers mentioned earlier. Every person receives exactly what their deeds have earned, and the Quran states elsewhere that whoever does an atom's weight of good will see it, and whoever does an atom's weight of evil will see it.\n\nThere is real comfort in this for anyone whose good work has gone unnoticed. Ordinary life is full of effort that nobody records: patience with a difficult relative, honesty in a transaction where cheating would never be detected, kindness offered to someone who cannot repay it. None of this appears in any human account. All of it appears in this one.\n\n\"He is certainly All-Aware of what they do\" gives the basis for such precise payment. The Arabic name \"Khabir\" indicates knowledge of the inner reality of things, not merely their outward form. This matters, because deeds are not judged only by appearance. Two people may perform the identical action with entirely different intentions, and any human observer would rate them the same.\n\nThat inner knowledge cuts in both directions. It means the quiet sincerity nobody noticed is fully known, and it means the impressive public act performed for reputation is equally known for what it was. Perfect payment requires perfect information, and the verse supplies both together.",
  "112": "\"So be steadfast as you are commanded ˹O Prophet˺\" gives the practical conclusion of everything the chapter has recounted. Six nations, their prophets, their refusals, and their endings have all been described, and the instruction that follows is a single word: remain firm.\n\nThe Arabic term \"istiqamah\" means standing straight, holding to a path without deviating to either side. It is reported that the Prophet said this chapter and others like it had turned his hair grey, a report mentioned in the collections and discussed by commentators, and scholars have often connected that statement to the weight of this particular command. Being told to remain perfectly upright, exactly as commanded, is a demanding instruction for anyone who takes it seriously.\n\nThe qualification \"as you are commanded\" is important. Steadfastness is not measured by personal enthusiasm or by how much effort a person invents for himself. It is measured against what was actually instructed, which protects believers from both laziness and excess.\n\n\"Along with those who turn ˹in submission to Allah˺ with you\" extends the command to the community. The Prophet was not told to stand firm alone, and his followers were not told to stand firm without him. This chapter has repeatedly shown small groups of believers surviving alongside their prophets, and the pattern holds here.\n\n\"And do not transgress\" warns against the opposite danger. The Arabic indicates going beyond the proper limit, and this is a caution that is easy to overlook. A person striving to be firm can drift into excess, whether in worship beyond what was prescribed, in harshness toward others, or in responding to opposition with more than is permitted. The Prophet warned in a hadith recorded by al-Bukhari against those who make religion harder than it is, and taught that this religion is easy and that no one overburdens himself in it without being overcome.\n\n\"Surely He is All-Seeing of what you ˹believers˺ do\" closes the instruction with the awareness that makes it sustainable. Both the steadiness and the restraint are seen.",
  "113": "\"And do not be inclined to the wrongdoers or you will be touched by the Fire\" adds a warning that has troubled many readers because of how little it seems to require. The Arabic verb indicates leaning toward something, tilting in its direction. It does not describe joining the wrongdoers, adopting their beliefs, or helping them oppress. It describes an inclination.\n\nEarly commentators discussed the meaning carefully, and the range of their explanations is instructive. Some understood it as approving of their wrongdoing, others as showing them affection because of their wrongdoing, others as relying on them or flattering them for worldly advantage. What the verse does not mean is that ordinary dealings with unjust people are forbidden. The Prophet traded with people who were not believers, accepted their protection in Mecca, made treaties with them, and dealt with them justly. Islam requires fair treatment of everyone, including opponents.\n\nThe line being drawn is about the heart's tilt. A believer may work with, live beside, and be courteous to people who do wrong. What he cannot do is find their wrongdoing acceptable, defend it for the sake of gain, or quietly wish he were part of what they have.\n\nThe consequence, being touched by the Fire, is stated with the same verb of touching used elsewhere in these chapters for harm reaching a person. The mildness of the wording matches the mildness of the offence being described, and that is precisely the point: even an inclination has weight.\n\n\"For then you would have no protectors other than Allah, nor would you be helped\" explains the danger of leaning toward the powerful in the first place. People tilt toward wrongdoers because those wrongdoers can protect, employ, promote, and shelter them. The verse points out where that protection ends. On the day it is needed, the patrons a person courted will have nothing to offer, and the One who could have helped is the One whose displeasure was earned in courting them.",
  "114": "\"Establish prayer ˹O Prophet˺ at both ends of the day and in the early part of the night\" moves from warnings about inclination and transgression to the practice that keeps a person steady. After a long chapter of destroyed nations and firm commands, the remedy offered is prayer.\n\nThe times mentioned are the two ends of the day and the early hours of the night. Commentators have understood the two ends as the morning prayer and the afternoon and sunset prayers, with the early part of the night covering the evening prayer, and there are differences among them about the exact distribution. This chapter was revealed in Mecca, and the full detail of the five daily prayers came with the Night Journey, so the verse is best read as establishing the rhythm of prayer across the day rather than as a complete schedule.\n\nWhat the timing accomplishes is worth noticing. Prayer is distributed so that a person is never far from it. The day begins with it, is interrupted by it, and closes with it, which prevents any long stretch of life from being lived in complete forgetfulness.\n\n\"Surely good deeds wipe out evil deeds\" gives the reason this practice matters so much. The Arabic verb indicates removing something, making it disappear the way writing is erased. The Prophet illustrated this with an image recorded by al-Bukhari and Muslim, asking his companions whether dirt would remain on a man who bathed in a river outside his door five times a day, and telling them that the five prayers work in the same way.\n\nIt is important to be clear about the scope. Scholars have generally understood this erasure to apply to lesser sins rather than to major ones, which require sincere repentance, and to rights owed to other people, which require making them right. The Prophet's own words in a hadith recorded by Muslim state that the five prayers expiate what lies between them so long as major sins are avoided.\n\n\"That is a reminder for the mindful\" closes by naming who benefits: those who keep the matter in view.",
  "115": "\"And be patient!\" repeats the instruction that has run through both of these chapters, and its placement here is deliberate. It comes immediately after the command to establish prayer, and the two belong together. Prayer supplies the strength; patience is what that strength is spent on. Surah al-Baqarah joins them explicitly, telling believers to seek help through patience and prayer.\n\nThe Arabic \"sabr\" covers more ground than the English word suggests. It includes bearing hardship without collapsing, holding back from what is forbidden when it is available and attractive, and continuing in obedience when it has become tiring and unrewarding. All three were required of the Prophet in Mecca, and all three were required of the small community around him.\n\nBy this point in the chapter, patience has been illustrated rather than merely commanded. Noah persisted for centuries. Hud stood alone against a nation and invited them to do their worst. Salih waited through three days that he had announced himself. Lot spent a night at his door with a crowd outside. Shu'aib was threatened with stoning and told his people to keep watching. The instruction here asks the Prophet to join a line of men who did exactly this.\n\n\"Certainly Allah does not discount the reward of the good-doers\" gives the reason patience is reasonable rather than merely admirable. The Arabic verb indicates causing something to be lost or wasted, and its denial is absolute. Nothing spent in this way disappears.\n\nThat guarantee addresses the particular difficulty of patience, which is that it often produces no visible result. A person who works and sees progress can keep going on the evidence. A person who endures without any sign of change has nothing to hold except trust that the effort is registered somewhere.\n\nThe word chosen for those who receive this promise is \"muhsinin,\" those who do good with excellence. The Prophet defined this quality, in the hadith recorded by Muslim, as worshipping Allah as though you see Him, knowing that if you do not see Him, He sees you.",
  "116": "\"If only there had been among the ˹destroyed˺ peoples before you, ˹O believers,˺ virtuous individuals who forbade corruption in the land\" expresses regret over something missing from every nation the chapter has described. Looking back across those societies, the Quran identifies the absence that allowed them to collapse: they had no effective body of people willing to stand against the corruption spreading among them.\n\nThe Arabic wording carries a sense of longing, as though wishing such people had existed. The description is specific. They would have been individuals of remaining virtue and understanding, and their function would have been to forbid corruption. Not merely to disapprove privately, but to speak and act against it.\n\n\"Other than the few We had saved ˹from the torment˺\" acknowledges that such people did exist, but in numbers too small to change anything. Noah's handful of passengers, the believers with Hud and with Salih, Lot's household. Each community had a remnant, and each remnant was rescued while the society around it was removed.\n\nThis raises a serious question about collective responsibility. The chapter has consistently said that Allah does not wrong anyone, and that the punished nations wronged themselves. This verse adds a further dimension: a society is not only judged by the wrongs committed in it, but by whether anyone stood against them. Silence has a cost.\n\n\"But the wrongdoers ˹only˺ pursued their ˹worldly˺ pleasures, becoming wicked\" describes what occupied them instead. The Arabic indicates being given over to luxury and ease, softened by comfort. The Quran repeatedly identifies this class of the affluent and indulged as the first to reject prophets, and here their comfort is presented as the reason they had no interest in reform. People enjoying a system rarely wish to examine it.\n\nThe practical implication is direct and uncomfortable. Every society produces wrongdoing. What determines its fate, according to this verse, is whether it also produces people willing to name that wrongdoing and resist it while there is still time.",
  "117": "\"And your Lord ˹O Prophet˺ would never destroy a society unjustly while its people were acting rightly\" states the principle that governs every destruction described in this chapter. Placed at the end of the sequence, it functions as a guarantee: nothing recounted in the previous pages was arbitrary.\n\nTwo conditions are named together, and both matter. Allah does not destroy a community unjustly, and He does not destroy one whose people are acting rightly. The Arabic word for their conduct, from the root of \"islah,\" refers to putting things right, repairing and maintaining what is sound. It is the same word Shu'aib used when he said he intended only reform to the best of his ability.\n\nThis connects directly to the previous verse. There, the fatal absence in the destroyed nations was the lack of people forbidding corruption. Here, the presence of people who put things right is described as protection for a society.\n\nCommentators have discussed what \"acting rightly\" refers to, and many understood it as dealing justly with one another. On that reading, a community whose members treat each other fairly is not destroyed, even if their belief is deficient, whereas injustice between people brings ruin quickly. Others took it more broadly to include right belief and right conduct together. Both readings preserve the same principle: the destruction described in the Quran always follows sustained wrongdoing.\n\nThere is an important clarification here for anyone reading these accounts uneasily. The Quran is not describing a God who removes nations that displease Him in matters of doctrine alone. In every case recounted in this chapter, the community had rejected clear warning, oppressed the weak, cheated in trade, assaulted travellers, or claimed divinity outright.\n\nIt is also worth noting the practical implication. If injustice among people brings a society down, then reform is not an optional extra alongside worship. It is part of what keeps a community standing, which is precisely why the prophets in this chapter addressed scales, hospitality, and the treatment of the poor as religious matters.",
  "118": "\"Had your Lord so willed, He would have certainly made humanity one single community ˹of believers˺\" addresses a question that naturally arises after a chapter full of division, rejection, and destroyed nations. Why is there so much disagreement? Could it not have been avoided?\n\nThe answer begins by confirming that unity was entirely within Allah's power. Human beings could have been created without the capacity to differ, believing uniformly the way the angels obey without deviation. This is the same point made near the end of the previous chapter, where it was said that had the Lord so willed, everyone on earth would have believed, followed by the question of whether the Prophet would then force people into faith.\n\n\"But they will always ˹choose to˺ differ\" states the reality that follows from how human beings were actually made. The Arabic indicates continuing difference, an ongoing condition rather than an occasional event.\n\nIt is worth being careful about what kind of difference is meant. The Quran distinguishes between differences that arise from honest human variation, which are natural and often harmless, and the division over revealed truth that this chapter has described, which comes from rivalry and desire rather than from a lack of clarity. Verse after verse in this chapter has shown communities who received clear guidance and split over it anyway.\n\nThe reason such difference is permitted returns to the purpose stated at the beginning of this chapter: creation was arranged to test which of us is best in deeds. A test requires genuine alternatives. If belief were automatic, there would be no merit in it, no struggle in choosing it, and nothing meaningful to reward.\n\nUnderstanding this changes how a believer views the disagreement around him. Widespread rejection of the truth is not evidence that the truth has failed or that Allah has lost control of His creation. It is the expected condition of a world designed for choosing, and the next verse names the exception to it.",
  "119": "\"Except those shown mercy by your Lord\" identifies the one group that escapes the endless differing described in the previous verse. Their exemption is not credited to their intelligence, their upbringing, or their effort. It is attributed to mercy.\n\nThis is a humbling way to describe guidance, and it appears consistently in the Quran. Those who reach the truth are recipients rather than achievers. The believers rescued in this chapter, alongside Noah, Hud, Salih, Lot, and Shu'aib, were each saved by a mercy from Allah, and the same word is used every time.\n\n\"And so He created them ˹to choose freely˺\" gives the purpose behind the arrangement. Commentators have understood this phrase in two ways. Some read it as meaning that Allah created humanity for the outcome just described, so that some would receive mercy and others would differ. Others, including a view reported from Ibn Abbas, read it as saying He created them for mercy, which fits the well-known hadith recorded by al-Bukhari and Muslim in which Allah declares that His mercy prevails over His wrath.\n\nBoth readings are held by respected scholars, and neither conflicts with the rest of the chapter, which has insisted throughout that people are responsible for their own choices and that Allah wrongs no one.\n\n\"And so the Word of your Lord will be fulfilled: \"I will surely fill up Hell with jinn and humans all together.\"\" states the outcome that has been decreed. This is knowledge, not compulsion. Allah knows what people will freely choose, and His knowing does not make the choice for them. A teacher who has taught for thirty years may know with certainty that some students will fail; his knowledge is not the cause of the failure.\n\nThe inclusion of both jinn and humans is worth noting. Both were created with the ability to choose, both were addressed by revelation, and both are accountable. The Quran describes jinn who listened to the recitation and believed, which shows that the same door stands open to them.",
  "120": "\"And We relate to you ˹O Prophet˺ the stories of the messengers to reassure your heart\" states plainly why this chapter contains what it contains. Six prophetic histories have been told at length, and the purpose is now given: to strengthen the heart of the man carrying the same burden.\n\nThe Arabic expression means to make the heart firm, to steady it. This is a remarkable admission of what the Prophet was experiencing. He needed steadying. Insult, boycott, accusations of madness and forgery, the deaths of his wife Khadijah and his uncle Abu Talib, and the rejection at Ta'if had all occurred during this Meccan period. The Quran does not present him as untouched by any of it.\n\nThe method of reassurance is worth noticing, because it is not what most people would choose. He is not told that his difficulties will end soon, and he is given no timetable. He is shown history. Every messenger before him faced the same accusations, the same demands, the same class of arrogant leaders, and the same small band of followers dismissed as worthless. Knowing that a hardship is normal for the role changes how it feels, even when nothing about the hardship itself changes.\n\n\"And there has come to you in this ˹sûrah˺ the truth\" names what the chapter has delivered. The accounts were not offered as inspiring tales; they are presented as what actually happened.\n\n\"A warning ˹to the disbelievers˺, and a reminder to the believers\" identifies the two audiences and what each receives. The same words function differently depending on who is listening. A person refusing the message hears a description of what happened to those who refused before him. A believer hears confirmation that the path he is on has always been narrow, always been mocked, and has always ended in rescue.\n\nThat double function runs through the entire Quran. Nothing in it is addressed to only one kind of listener, and the same verse can steady one heart while unsettling another.",
  "121": "\"Say to those who disbelieve, \"Persist in your ways; we will certainly persist in ours.\"\" gives the Prophet the same closing statement that Shu'aib delivered to his people a few verses earlier. That repetition is intentional. The chapter has just explained that these histories were told to steady the Prophet's heart, and now he is handed the very words an earlier prophet used at the end of his own argument.\n\nThe Arabic tells the deniers to act according to their position and ability, doing whatever lies within their power. It is not an approval of what they do. It is a recognition that the discussion has reached the point where words have done everything they can, and each side will now simply continue.\n\nThere is a quiet strength in this kind of ending. It contains no anger, no insult, and no plea. It also contains no compromise, since the second half of the sentence declares that the believers will continue on their own path with equal determination. The separation is complete and mutual, exactly as the previous chapter expressed it when the Prophet was told to say that his deeds were his and theirs were theirs.\n\nThe instruction is worth reflecting on for anyone engaged in calling others to good. There is a stage at which continued argument becomes counterproductive, and recognising that stage is part of wisdom. What remains after that point is to hold to one's own path visibly and consistently, which is often more persuasive than anything that could be said.\n\nHistorically, this is precisely what happened. The Muslims in Mecca continued praying, trading honestly, and enduring, and their persistence did more to change minds than any debate. Within a generation, many of the people who had been told to persist in their own way had abandoned it entirely.\n\nThe phrase also carries an unstated challenge. Two groups cannot both be right about something this fundamental, and inviting each side to continue is an invitation to let the outcome decide.",
  "122": "\"And wait! Surely we ˹too˺ are waiting.\" completes the statement begun in the previous verse and gives it its final shape. Both sides are told to wait, and the believers announce that they are already doing so.\n\nThe word for waiting here indicates expectation, watching for something anticipated. This is the same posture Shu'aib described to his people, and the same instruction the Prophet was given at the close of the previous chapter, when those demanding punishment were told to keep waiting while he waited alongside them.\n\nWhat makes the statement so pointed is that both sides are waiting for the same event with opposite expectations. The deniers were waiting for the movement to collapse, for the Prophet to be silenced, and for their own position to be confirmed. The believers were waiting for the promise they had been given. Only one of these expectations could be met.\n\nThere is a particular kind of confidence required to end an argument this way. A person who is uncertain wants to keep talking, to add another proof, to secure agreement now. A person who is certain can afford to stop and let time do the rest. Every prophet in this chapter reached that position eventually, and none of them reached it through wishful thinking. They reached it through knowing where the outcome rested.\n\nFor the small Muslim community in Mecca, this instruction was not comfortable. Waiting is hard when the waiting has no announced end and the pressure is constant. The chapter has already given them what makes it bearable: prayer at both ends of the day, patience that is never wasted, steadfastness alongside others who turn to Allah, and six accounts of communities who once looked permanent and are now gone.\n\nThe two verses together form one of the most dignified conclusions to an argument found anywhere in scripture. No threats are issued, no insults exchanged, and nothing is conceded. Each side is invited to continue and to watch.",
  "123": "\"To Allah ˹alone˺ belongs the knowledge of what is hidden in the heavens and the earth\" closes the chapter by naming what makes all the waiting reasonable. Neither side in the argument knows what is coming. One party does, and everything now rests with Him.\n\nThe Arabic word \"ghayb\" covers everything beyond human perception: the future, the unseen present, and the past that no one witnessed. This chapter itself has been an example of that last category, since the accounts of Noah and the destroyed nations were described earlier as stories of the unseen that neither the Prophet nor his people knew before.\n\n\"And to Him all matters are returned\" states where every situation finally goes. Not some matters, and not only religious ones. Every affair, whether it concerns a nation or a single household, ends up in the same place.\n\n\"So worship Him and put your trust in Him\" gives the two instructions that follow from this, and the order is natural. Worship is what a person owes; trust is what a person then rests upon. The chapter opened with the command to worship none but Allah, and it closes by repeating it, having filled the space between with every reason such worship is warranted.\n\nTrust here is the same \"tawakkul\" that carried Noah while he built a ship on dry land, Hud when he challenged an entire nation to plot against him, and Shu'aib when he was threatened with stoning. It is not passivity. Each of those men worked hard and then left the outcome where it belonged.\n\n\"And your Lord is never unaware of what you do\" ends the chapter with the awareness that has been present throughout it. The Arabic denies any heedlessness or inattention on His part. Nothing slips past unnoticed, whether it is the fraud of a merchant adjusting his scales or the patience of a believer holding on quietly while nothing seems to change.\n\nFor the audience first hearing it, that final line was both warning and comfort, depending entirely on which side of it they had chosen to stand."
};
